    || '["M1"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('4d3a70ca-224a-5850-a040-254327e132a7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-02', 'Adaptation Requires Repeated Relevant Exposure', ('{"id":"D101-02","title":"Adaptation Requires Repeated Relevant Exposure","statement":"A single session produces responses. Chronic adaptation requires repeated, relevant, appropriately dosed exposure over time. Coaches evaluate trends rather than inferring adaptation from one workout.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known for the stimulus-response-adaptation concept; F for the claim that exact adaptation timelines and the optimal next exposure are individually predictable with the precision simplified supercompensation curves imply. The repeated-bout effect means a familiar bout produces less damage and soreness after prior exposure — so reduced soreness does not establish that a program has stopped working. [EP101-04, EP101-05]","teaching_boundary":"Trend-reading is not an excuse for never evaluating. A coach who says \"it''s a trend, give it time\" for six months without a defined outcome or review point has not applied the doctrine — they have avoided measurement.","sources":["EP101-04","EP101-05"],"summary_class":"A / F on precision","primary_modules":["M2"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('e68ca4eb-7a0b-5437-96c3-7aa39e34dae5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-03', 'Overload Is Multi-Variable', ('{"id":"D101-03","title":"Overload Is Multi-Variable","statement":"Progressive overload means progressively appropriate training demand relative to current capacity and intended adaptation. It is not synonymous with adding external weight.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known. Resistance-training variables influence strength and hypertrophy responses, and load, volume, frequency, density, range of motion, contraction emphasis, velocity and intent, exercise complexity, technical demand, and exposure distribution can all change task demand. F for \"progressive overload means add weight every session,\" which is too narrow and can be unsafe or counterproductive when technical, fatigue, or schedule constraints are limiting. [EP101-05, EP101-21, EP101-22]","teaching_boundary":"Rejecting load-only progression is not rejecting load. Load remains one of the most reliable and most easily coached progression variables. The doctrine forbids the reduction, not the tool.","sources":["EP101-05","EP101-21","EP101-22"],"summary_class":"A / F on load-only","primary_modules":["M3"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('2d84f33c-fba1-5a7b-9f2e-cf8a3ece7c0f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-04', 'Progression Must Serve the Target', ('{"id":"D101-04","title":"Progression Must Serve the Target","statement":"Load, volume, frequency, density, range, velocity or intent, complexity, specificity, or exposure distribution may progress. The variable selected must match the athlete and the intended adaptation.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Probable that training demand should evolve when justified by target, response, and context; D — Applied coaching inference for the selection of which variable progresses first; F for any universal progression sequence — no evidence supports one. [EP101-05, EP101-07, EP101-21]","teaching_boundary":"\"It depends\" is not the answer. The coach must name the variable, name the target it serves, and name what would make them choose differently. A progression decision without a stated target is not individualization; it is improvisation.","sources":["EP101-05","EP101-07","EP101-21"],"summary_class":"B / D / F on universal sequence","primary_modules":["M3"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('e2afe2c6-a28b-5c2b-afee-aa3000018002', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-05', 'Specificity Is Multidimensional', ('{"id":"D101-05","title":"Specificity Is Multidimensional","statement":"Specificity is determined by relevant task demands — force, velocity, contraction mode, range, coordination, energetic demand, timing, and skill characteristics — not by how closely an exercise visually resembles the sport.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known. Adaptations are shaped by the characteristics of the task performed, and training a target quality tends to improve that quality more directly than unrelated work. F for \"an exercise looks like the sport, therefore it transfers better\" — visual resemblance alone cannot establish positive transfer, and may reduce loadability, alter coordination, or displace sport practice without proving benefit. [EP101-03, EP101-04]","teaching_boundary":"Rejecting resemblance is not rejecting relevance. A method can be highly specific in the dimensions that matter and look nothing like the sport; another can look identical and be specific in none of them. The question is always specific in which dimension, to which demand.","sources":["EP101-03","EP101-04"],"summary_class":"A / F on resemblance","primary_modules":["M4"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('0e8b5b1a-fea7-59a3-acbc-c206d8d352f0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-06', 'Transfer Must Be Evaluated', (('{"id":"D101-06","title":"Transfer Must Be Evaluated","statement":"Improvement in an exercise, a test, or a physical quality does not automatically prove improvement in sport performance. Transfer is assessed at a defined target.","evidence_class":"A for the specificity principle underlying transfer; C to D","evidence_note":"A for the specificity principle underlying transfer; C to D — Conditional for predicting transfer from a single exercise. Positive, negative, and neutral transfer are all real outcomes. Stronger transfer claims require a clearly described intervention, valid target testing, a relevant comparison, sufficient exposure, and a meaningful outcome beyond the trained task — and sport-performance claims need especially careful interpretation because technical and tactical context, opponent, team system, and competition noise are large confounders. [EP101-03, EP101-04, EP101-30]","teaching_boundary":"Demanding transfer evidence is not demanding a controlled trial before every squat. It is demanding that the coach state the target and the evaluation in advance, and report honestly when transfer has not been established rather than assuming it.","sources":["EP101-03","EP10'
    || '1-04","EP101-30"],"summary_class":"A principle / C–D prediction","primary_modules":["M4"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('9e72053d-a31c-5bac-8ce7-2028b60087d0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-07', 'Physical Preparation Supports Sport; It Does Not Replace Sport', ('{"id":"D101-07","title":"Physical Preparation Supports Sport; It Does Not Replace Sport","statement":"Performance training develops physical capacities. Sport practice develops sport-specific skill, perception, decision-making, and tactical execution. The two are not substitutes.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known that physical training can improve component qualities such as strength, hypertrophy, sprint, jump, and power outcomes within studied populations; B — Probable that improvements in component qualities may contribute to sport performance, with the practical effect sport-, athlete-, and task-dependent. Direct sport-performance transfer must be demonstrated rather than inferred from mechanics. [EP101-03, EP101-04, EP101-13, EP101-25, EP101-26]","teaching_boundary":"This is not a demotion of the weight room. A method can be valuable even when it does not resemble a sport action, provided it develops a limiting or target quality that contributes in the athlete''s context. The doctrine sets the claim boundary, not the value.","sources":["EP101-03","EP101-04","EP101-13","EP101-25","EP101-26"],"summary_class":"A / B","primary_modules":["M1"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('a722e5cc-9461-5eb6-9675-598b020c5b9f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-08', 'Individualization Begins With Meaningful Differences', (('{"id":"D101-08","title":"Individualization Begins With Meaningful Differences","statement":"Training age, maturation, current capacity, sport demand, schedule, history, availability, and response matter. Individualization does not require unnecessary complexity.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known that athletes differ in baseline status, maturation, training age, strength level, schedule, injury history, sport demands, and recovery context, and that these alter both tolerance and response; B — Supported practice that individualization should begin with known, decision-relevant variables rather than bespoke programming of every variable for every athlete; D for athlete preference and adherence influencing selection where multiple methods are similarly appropriate. [EP101-01, EP101-04, EP101-11, EP101-14]","teaching_boundary":"Individualization is not the same as customization. Twelve different programs for twelve athletes who share a demand, a schedule, and a training age is not individualization — it is a coaching load the environment cannot sustain, and it makes evaluation impossible.","sources":["EP101-01","EP101-04","EP101-11","EP101-14"],"summary_class":"A / B / D"'
    || ',"primary_modules":["M5"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('c1fac1f0-c282-52a2-9646-cdb1ff20df85', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-09', 'Variation Must Have a Reason', (('{"id":"D101-09","title":"Variation Must Have a Reason","statement":"Variation is useful when it solves a programming problem, changes an intended stimulus, manages fatigue, or addresses changing demands. Novelty for its own sake is not a training principle.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Probable that planned variation can be useful, particularly for strength-oriented programming and logistical management: volume-equated synthesis found periodized training had an advantage for maximal strength over non-periodized training, while hypertrophy superiority was not clearly established and linear versus undulating approaches showed no consistent difference. D for keeping a structure stable long enough to coach, load, and evaluate it. F for \"muscle confusion,\" constant rotation, and novelty as goals. [EP101-05, EP101-07]","teaching_boundary":"Two findings are held together and neither is dropped: planned variation beats no plan for strength, and no named model beats another. A coach who cites the first to justify a branded system has used half the evidence.","sources":["EP101-05","EP101-07"],"summary_class":"B / D / F on novelty","primary_modules":["M6"],"standing":"G3 Doctr'
    || 'ine"}'))::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('5441e55c-1f94-53a9-bdd4-ef99a03b7154', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-10', 'Fatigue Is a Cost, Not the Product', ('{"id":"D101-10","title":"Fatigue Is a Cost, Not the Product","statement":"Fatigue and soreness may accompany training but do not establish effectiveness. Training quality is judged against intended adaptation and performance outcomes.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known that fatigue is a reduction in capacity that can occur alongside productive training; F for soreness or fatigue as proof of session quality — soreness can occur with novel or eccentric work but does not quantify adaptation. Absence of fatigue is likewise not proof of failure. [EP101-04, EP101-05, EP101-10, EP101-15, EP101-19]","teaching_boundary":"The correction does not make fatigue meaningless. Fatigue is real information about exposure and tolerance, and managing it is a coaching obligation (obligation 6). What it is not is a measure of whether the session worked.","sources":["EP101-04","EP101-05","EP101-10","EP101-15","EP101-19"],"summary_class":"A / F on soreness","primary_modules":["M7"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('53b3f1dd-d809-53f9-b148-ad0aa99c3851', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-11', 'Recovery Is Contextual', ('{"id":"D101-11","title":"Recovery Is Contextual","statement":"Recovery depends on the athlete, the exposure, sport demands, sleep, nutrition, psychological stress, and time. No universal 48- or 72-hour rule and no supercompensation timetable governs all athletes.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known that sleep, nutritional adequacy, psychological stress, sport demands, and training load influence recovery and performance context; B — Supported practice for using multiple signals to make proportionate adjustments; F for the claim that soreness, fatigue, a fixed 48/72-hour rule, or a readiness score can precisely determine when a specific athlete is fully recovered. [EP101-04, EP101-10, EP101-15, EP101-19]","teaching_boundary":"Rejecting the fixed clock does not mean scheduling is arbitrary. A G3 coach still plans spacing deliberately — they simply plan it from demand, schedule, and observed response rather than from a number they were told.","sources":["EP101-04","EP101-10","EP101-15","EP101-19"],"summary_class":"A / B / F on fixed rules","primary_modules":["M7"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('eba24853-0ee7-575b-b124-8c0054dedfc4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-12', 'Dose Is Outcome- and Context-Specific', (('{"id":"D101-12","title":"Dose Is Outcome- and Context-Specific","statement":"More training is not automatically better. Effective dose balances adaptation opportunity against fatigue, adherence, schedule, and recoverability.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known that in healthy adults volume is associated with strength and hypertrophy outcomes with likely diminishing returns as dose rises, the exact curve differing by outcome and design; B — Probable that lower doses can improve performance, especially in novices or when a quality is maintained rather than maximized; D for \"minimum effective dose\" and \"maximum recoverable volume\" as communication heuristics; F for presenting either as a precisely discoverable individual constant. Unknown for exact individualized thresholds. [EP101-04, EP101-05, EP101-21, EP101-22, EP101-29]","teaching_boundary":"Rejecting \"more is better\" is not adopting \"less is better.\" Dose is a judgment about opportunity and cost, made against a target — and a coach who systematically under-doses because dosing feels risky has made the opposite error with the same root.","sources":["EP101-04","EP101-05","EP101-21","EP101-22","EP101-29"],"summ'
    || 'ary_class":"A / B / D / Unknown","primary_modules":["M3"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('89ec3800-e4bf-57f6-a45d-605c53109067', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-13', 'Concurrent Training Is Manageable', (('{"id":"D101-13","title":"Concurrent Training Is Manageable","statement":"Strength, power, and endurance can coexist. Interference is conditional and must be managed rather than treated as universal incompatibility.","evidence_class":"A/B","evidence_note":"A/B — Known-Probable that strength and endurance can be developed concurrently and that blanket exclusion of conditioning is unsupported; B — Probable that interference becomes practically meaningful when high endurance volume coexists with strength or power priorities, when sessions are closely coupled, when running and impact demand is high, or when fatigue management is poor. A systematic review reported small interference for lower-body strength in males but not females, varying by training status; other meta-analytic evidence suggests explosive strength/power and fiber hypertrophy may be more vulnerable than maximal strength, with running potentially more problematic than cycling for some outcomes. F for \"cardio kills gains.\" Exact thresholds are not universal. [EP101-08, EP101-09, EP101-31]","teaching_boundary":"\"Manageable\" is not \"ignorable.\" The doctrine rejects the prohibition, not the phenomenon. A coach who stack'
    || 's a heavy running block against a power priority and cites this doctrine has inverted it.","sources":["EP101-08","EP101-09","EP101-31"],"summary_class":"A/B / B","primary_modules":["M8"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('06bbc737-4b1b-580c-a480-b7b621e3c23c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-14', 'Training Age and Maturation Outrank Arbitrary Age Rules', ('{"id":"D101-14","title":"Training Age and Maturation Outrank Arbitrary Age Rules","statement":"Chronological age alone does not determine readiness for resistance, speed, plyometric, or power training.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Supported practice. Training age is exposure and competency history, not chronological age; biological maturation and prior training can be more decision-relevant than age alone, especially in youth. Novice/intermediate/advanced labels are useful shorthand, but no universal evidence-based cut points classify every athlete. [EP101-11, EP101-12, EP101-13]","teaching_boundary":"Rejecting age gates does not make age irrelevant. Chronological age carries real information about maturation, schooling, supervision context, and psychological readiness — it simply is not a sufficient criterion on its own.","sources":["EP101-11","EP101-12","EP101-13"],"summary_class":"B","primary_modules":["M9"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('72e04339-7dd7-57c5-bc59-c89e7c1ddc07', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-15', 'Youth Can Train', (('{"id":"D101-15","title":"Youth Can Train","statement":"Properly supervised and developmentally appropriate youth resistance, speed, plyometric, and power training are legitimate components of athletic development.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known that position statements and systematic reviews support youth resistance training when qualified instruction, supervision, sensible progression, and appropriate technique are present; B — Probable that youth can train strength, power, sprinting, and plyometric qualities when task demands are scaled to competency and context, with adolescent meta-analyses reporting improvements in jump, sprint, COD, and explosive-strength outcomes. F for: lifting stunts growth; a specific age must be reached first; youth should only use bodyweight; youth cannot train power; puberty must occur before meaningful physical preparation. [EP101-11, EP101-12, EP101-13, EP101-25, EP101-26]","teaching_boundary":"These findings do not justify unsupervised lifting, arbitrary heavy loading, adult-style volume, or progression beyond technical and psychological readiness. The research is more robust for supervised interventions than for unsupervised real-'
    || 'world implementation. Samples are frequently adolescent and male-dominant rather than prepubertal or female.","sources":["EP101-11","EP101-12","EP101-13","EP101-25","EP101-26"],"summary_class":"A / B / F on prohibitions","primary_modules":["M9"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('b83e4469-d557-5a32-9464-05ca8671ded5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-16', 'Technique Is Task- and Athlete-Constrained', (('{"id":"D101-16","title":"Technique Is Task- and Athlete-Constrained","statement":"Coaches require safe and effective technical standards, but one universal movement solution does not fit every athlete or task.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Supported practice that coaches should establish task-relevant setup, intent, execution constraints, and progression criteria, using demonstration, concise cues, and feedback appropriate to athlete and task; augmented feedback can improve motor learning and acute resistance-training performance, with effectiveness depending on form, timing, frequency, and context — constant immediate correction is not automatically optimal. F for a universal movement-screen score, one asymmetry threshold, or one technique template predicting injury or dictating a single correct solution for every athlete. [EP101-01, EP101-20, EP101-23, EP101-24]","teaching_boundary":"Movement variability is not a licence for uncoached movement. Anthropometry, history, task goal, equipment, and coordination produce legitimate variation within task-relevant constraints the coach still defines and still enforces.","sources":["EP101-01","EP101-20","EP101-23","EP101-24"],'
    || '"summary_class":"B / F on templates","primary_modules":["M10"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('7c316315-c161-5835-be3f-85102a8e23e0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-17', 'Monitoring Informs; It Does Not Diagnose', (('{"id":"D101-17","title":"Monitoring Informs; It Does Not Diagnose","statement":"Load, wellness, readiness, heart rate, GPS, RPE, movement screens, asymmetry, and ACWR can contribute information. None independently determines injury, recovery, adaptation, or prescription.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Supported practice that monitoring supports a multifactorial decision process; C — Conditional that athlete-reported measures relate to subsequent performance or load, with variable relationships that do not support deterministic prescription from a single score; F for a readiness score automatically determining today''s training, for ACWR as a precise injury predictor with a fixed safe zone, and for movement-screen composites as standalone injury prediction. [EP101-15, EP101-16, EP101-18, EP101-19, EP101-32, EP101-33, EP101-35, EP101-36, EP101-37]","teaching_boundary":"\"Does not diagnose\" is not \"do not collect.\" A coach who abandons monitoring because it cannot diagnose has replaced false precision with no information. The standard is triangulation (AS-101-07), not abstinence.","sources":["EP101-15","EP101-16","EP101-18","EP101-19","EP101-32","EP101-33","EP101-35","EP'
    || '101-36","EP101-37"],"summary_class":"B / C / F on standalone use","primary_modules":["M11"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('ab4c41f5-c8dd-5e63-8b73-9bc6cc6f698b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-18', 'Testing Must Earn Interpretation', ('{"id":"D101-18","title":"Testing Must Earn Interpretation","statement":"A measurement must be sufficiently valid, reliable, standardized, and relevant before it should influence a coaching decision.","evidence_class":"A","evidence_note":"A — Known that a test can be valid for one construct and not another, that a reliable test is required to detect small meaningful change, and that standardized protocols and familiarization reduce avoidable noise; B — Supported practice for preferring repeated within-athlete baselines and context-consistent testing over uncritical normative comparison; C — Conditional for smallest worthwhile change, which is conceptually useful but whose calculation and practical threshold depend on context, test reliability, and decision use — it is not a universal cut point. [EP101-38, EP101-39]","teaching_boundary":"The bar is proportional to the decision. A coach does not need laboratory reliability to decide whether to add five pounds; they do need it before telling an athlete a quality has changed. Match the rigor to the consequence.","sources":["EP101-38","EP101-39"],"summary_class":"A / B / C","primary_modules":["M11"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('d62ce76e-ad57-530e-a05d-a793f6783028', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-19', 'Technology Does Not Create Validity', ('{"id":"D101-19","title":"Technology Does Not Create Validity","statement":"Technology can improve data acquisition. It cannot rescue an invalid question, an unreliable measure, a poor protocol, or an unsupported interpretation.","evidence_class":"F for \"technology makes programming more scientific\"","evidence_note":"F for \"technology makes programming more scientific\" — technology can improve measurement, but validity, reliability, context, and interpretation still govern use. Session RPE, GPS and wearables, heart rate, and wellness questionnaires can be useful monitoring inputs when measurement is standardized and interpreted alongside context; they are not direct measures of tissue state, adaptation, or injury causation. [EP101-18, EP101-38]","teaching_boundary":"This is not technology scepticism. A device that answers a question the coach had already formed is worth having. The doctrine simply denies that the device supplies the question, the protocol, or the meaning.","sources":["EP101-18","EP101-38"],"summary_class":"F on the claim","primary_modules":["M11"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('41e21726-56bd-5072-90e4-5393fec91a08', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-20', 'Evidence Sets Boundaries; Coaching Judgment Operates Within Them', (('{"id":"D101-20","title":"Evidence Sets Boundaries; Coaching Judgment Operates Within Them","statement":"Research evidence, coaching expertise, athlete context, and operational constraints must be integrated, without allowing either research slogans or personal experience to override stronger evidence.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Supported practice. Evidence-based practice integrates best available research with coach expertise, athlete values and preferences, and environmental constraints. It is neither \"research says, therefore everyone must\" nor \"experience overrides research.\" Survey evidence indicates practitioners, coaches, and athletes value research-informed practice while also valuing sport knowledge, experience, and communication — perception evidence, not evidence that any individual practice is effective. [EP101-01, EP101-02, EP101-40]","teaching_boundary":"The integration is not a 50/50 split, and it is not permission to overrule strong evidence with a strong feeling. Judgment operates within the boundary the evidence sets — and the coach must be able to say where that boundary is (§21.3).","sources":["EP101-01","EP101-02","EP101-40"],"summary_class":'
    || '"B","primary_modules":["M12"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('be09e1dd-7dc8-5e33-b8ca-3ebd77c5be92', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'doctrine', 'D101-21', 'Scope of Practice Is Part of Coaching Competence', (('{"id":"D101-21","title":"Scope of Practice Is Part of Coaching Competence","statement":"Performance coaches train and monitor performance. They do not independently diagnose medical conditions, provide medical clearance, or substitute for qualified healthcare professionals.","evidence_class":"A/B","evidence_note":"A/B — Known-Probable. Performance coaches should manage training exposure, technique, progressions, observation, and communication, and should not diagnose, independently clear return to sport, prescribe rehabilitation beyond scope, or substitute for athletic trainers, physicians, physical therapists, psychologists, or dietitians when indicated. Functional overreaching, nonfunctional overreaching, and overtraining syndrome are not interchangeable; OTS requires broader assessment and exclusion of other conditions and is unsuitable for casual coach diagnosis. [EP101-10, EP101-11, EP101-12]","teaching_boundary":"Scope is a competence, not a disclaimer. Recognizing the boundary early, communicating clearly, and referring well is skilled coaching — not an admission of limitation. Referring reflexively to avoid a conversation is its own failure.","sources":["EP101-10","EP101-11'
    || '","EP101-12"],"summary_class":"A/B","primary_modules":["M12"],"standing":"G3 Doctrine"}'))::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('af2a3dcc-3331-5a94-9d29-4f8a4d7d4e95', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-01', 'Define the Target Before the Method', ('{"id":"AS-101-01","title":"Define the Target Before the Method","statement":"Name the desired performance quality or outcome before selecting exercises or technology.","evidence_basis":"A (D101-05, D101-06).","enforcement":"every exposure object requires target_quality and demand (§21.4).","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c1'),
  ('aa6abfde-0e37-5d69-9564-cea9ae467516', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-02', 'Separate Acute Response From Chronic Adaptation', ('{"id":"AS-101-02","title":"Separate Acute Response From Chronic Adaptation","statement":"Do not use soreness, fatigue, pump, sweat, or a single performance response as proof of adaptation.","evidence_basis":"A (D101-02, D101-10).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"acute information is still information — it is evidence about exposure and tolerance, not about adaptation.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c2'),
  ('810959cf-8509-5149-a685-1568e3906f44', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-03', 'Progress the Variable That Solves the Problem', ('{"id":"AS-101-03","title":"Progress the Variable That Solves the Problem","statement":"Do not default to load progression when another variable better serves the target.","evidence_basis":"D — applied inference from multi-variable overload (D101-03, D101-04).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"no universal progression sequence is published (CJ-19).","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c3'),
  ('829fc224-c248-5b24-b087-1b3b44d0d136', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-04', 'Require a Transfer Rationale', ('{"id":"AS-101-04","title":"Require a Transfer Rationale","statement":"For every major method, state what it is expected to improve and how that improvement will be evaluated.","evidence_basis":"A/C — the specificity principle is A; transfer prediction from a single exercise is C–D (D101-06).","enforcement":"every exposure object requires transfer_rationale and evaluation_method (§21.4).","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c4'),
  ('7ea6292c-d3ee-584a-8e90-d0a5c7b5f2d8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-05', 'Preserve Program Stability Long Enough to Coach and Evaluate', ('{"id":"AS-101-05","title":"Preserve Program Stability Long Enough to Coach and Evaluate","statement":"Do not rotate exercises solely to create novelty. Change when demand, response, constraints, or practical utility justify it.","evidence_basis":"D (D101-09).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"no minimum block length is published. Stability long enough to coach, load, and evaluate is a judgment (CJ-19).","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c5'),
  ('0f20961e-0aae-5b72-87fa-2db54ed062e5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-06', 'Individualize From Known Variables First', ('{"id":"AS-101-06","title":"Individualize From Known Variables First","statement":"Prioritize training age, maturation, current capacity, technical competency, schedule, sport exposure, injury and training history, and response.","evidence_basis":"B (D101-08).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"individualization is not customization; a shared program for athletes who share a demand is a legitimate result.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c6'),
  ('d57873d9-24be-5b07-82a0-7c16aff52cbc', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-07', 'Use Multiple Inputs for Readiness Decisions', ('{"id":"AS-101-07","title":"Use Multiple Inputs for Readiness Decisions","statement":"Combine athlete report, recent exposure, schedule, performance trend, and coach observation when adjustment is necessary.","evidence_basis":"B (D101-17).","enforcement":"readiness_decision requires at least three distinct input types (§21.4).","boundary":null,"standing":"the three-input minimum is a G3 Applied Standard, not a research finding.","label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c7'),
  ('b67e9e73-af15-5a2e-8e2a-d13151d1ddc7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-08', 'No Single Metric Governs Training', ('{"id":"AS-101-08","title":"No Single Metric Governs Training","statement":"No ACWR value, movement-screen score, asymmetry percentage, wellness score, or readiness value independently dictates programming.","evidence_basis":"F for the standalone claims (D101-17).","enforcement":null,"boundary":null,"standing":"binding prohibition — enforced in the data layer (§21.4).","label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c8'),
  ('a9d63c02-55e9-53d1-a944-6cdf445dfb3c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-09', 'Standardize Before Comparing', ('{"id":"AS-101-09","title":"Standardize Before Comparing","statement":"Testing requires consistent protocol, equipment, instructions, warm-up, familiarization, and calculation.","evidence_basis":"A (D101-18).","enforcement":"test_result requires protocol and baseline_ref (§21.4).","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'c9'),
  ('42dcff54-9ac1-5fab-bd76-9520cae617e0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-10', 'Interpret Change Against Measurement Error', ('{"id":"AS-101-10","title":"Interpret Change Against Measurement Error","statement":"Do not treat small test changes as real without considering reliability, typical error, baseline variability, and decision consequences.","evidence_basis":"A/C — the principle is A; smallest worthwhile change as a calculation is C (D101-18).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"no universal cut point is published — the threshold depends on the test and the decision.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'd0'),
  ('99c24b62-ff29-5044-9bac-8b69753454a8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-11', 'Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure', ('{"id":"AS-101-11","title":"Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure","statement":"Use supervision, technique, training age, maturity, psychological readiness, and task demand rather than unsupported age prohibitions.","evidence_basis":"A/B (D101-14, D101-15).","enforcement":"supervision ratio documented for every youth group.","boundary":"competence-based progression is not a licence for adult dosing (§18.1).","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'd1'),
  ('10744a1b-e7c1-5033-b27c-5aeefcbcca5d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-12', 'Manage Concurrent Demands', ('{"id":"AS-101-12","title":"Manage Concurrent Demands","statement":"Account for endurance, strength, speed, power, sport practice, competition, and recovery rather than treating conditioning as categorically harmful.","evidence_basis":"A/B (D101-13).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"no separation interval, sequence, or modality rule is published (CJ-08).","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'd2'),
  ('d0eea913-f4da-52a9-a6de-4b304a9ea06d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-13', 'Technique Standards Must Be Purposeful', ('{"id":"AS-101-13","title":"Technique Standards Must Be Purposeful","statement":"Coach task-relevant positions, intent, execution, and safety constraints while allowing legitimate athlete-specific movement solutions.","evidence_basis":"B (D101-16).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"no universal movement template is published (CJ-20), and no template may be inferred from an example.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'd3'),
  ('6ea44505-fa54-5383-9959-0decd2013f94', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-14', 'Use Technology Only for an Actionable Question', ('{"id":"AS-101-14","title":"Use Technology Only for an Actionable Question","statement":"Do not collect a metric unless its meaning, reliability, and the possible coaching response are understood before collection.","evidence_basis":"B/F (D101-19).","enforcement":"monitoring_metric requires question_answered and possible_responses[] (§21.4).","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'd4'),
  ('ded4d2e6-ea3a-57dd-a559-d2f1ce26432e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-15', 'Refer Beyond Scope', ('{"id":"AS-101-15","title":"Refer Beyond Scope","statement":"Escalate medical, injury, nutritional, psychological, or return-to-play concerns to qualified professionals when appropriate.","evidence_basis":"A/B (D101-21).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"referral is not avoidance — the coach still communicates, documents, and adapts training within scope.","standing":"binding.","label":"applied_standard","production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'd5'),
  ('d4b73f6a-9512-53f2-a3e6-ada1388f4513', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'applied_standard', 'AS-101-16', 'Disclose Population Transfer', ('{"id":"AS-101-16","title":"Disclose Population Transfer","statement":"Any claim extended beyond the population in which it was established carries an explicit disclosure at the point of the claim.","evidence_basis":"A — the Evidence Pack''s population-transfer limitations are explicit and extensive (§18.3).","enforcement":"every module evidence block carries a population-transfer line; a module object without one fails production validation (§21.5).","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard","production_addition":true}')::jsonb, 'd6'),
  ('2892580e-537c-5626-b0d8-14dc915d57e3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-01', 'Exact weekly volume', ('{"id":"CJ-01","area":"Exact weekly volume","evidence_position":"Volume relates to strength and hypertrophy outcomes with diminishing returns; exact curve differs by outcome and design","g3_supplies":"D101-12, AS-101-03","coach_decides":"The weekly volume for this athlete and phase"}')::jsonb, 'd7'),
  ('195e3092-ef20-5333-a9e5-0aa8a25016e0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-02', 'Exact frequency', ('{"id":"CJ-02","area":"Exact frequency","evidence_position":"Frequency can facilitate distribution; independent effect often entangled with volume and training status","g3_supplies":"D101-12","coach_decides":"The frequency"}')::jsonb, 'd8'),
  ('ead821c6-c2c9-54c7-8c63-36396401b7ac', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-03', 'Exact intensity', ('{"id":"CJ-03","area":"Exact intensity","evidence_position":"Outcome-, athlete-, and task-dependent","g3_supplies":"D101-03, D101-04","coach_decides":"The intensity"}')::jsonb, 'd9'),
  ('ba1d3cb4-4924-559e-9358-dc8c5bc070ec', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-04', 'Fixed recovery windows', ('{"id":"CJ-04","area":"Fixed recovery windows","evidence_position":"F for the 48/72-hour rule","g3_supplies":"D101-11, AS-101-07","coach_decides":"The spacing"}')::jsonb, 'e0'),
  ('47d35991-a155-5885-bae1-936c80c19ad1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-05', 'Universal minimum effective dose', ('{"id":"CJ-05","area":"Universal minimum effective dose","evidence_position":"D as a communication heuristic; Unknown as an individual constant","g3_supplies":"D101-12","coach_decides":"Whether the concept helps here, and what it means locally"}')::jsonb, 'e1'),
  ('73277a12-ed88-5299-8ddc-282b62c9e69c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-06', 'Universal maximum recoverable volume', ('{"id":"CJ-06","area":"Universal maximum recoverable volume","evidence_position":"D as a cautionary heuristic; Unknown as a discoverable constant","g3_supplies":"D101-12","coach_decides":"The ceiling for this athlete"}')::jsonb, 'e2'),
  ('6d9c3a2e-776e-5f9e-b054-8cf7ef94f8a7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-07', 'Universal periodization model', ('{"id":"CJ-07","area":"Universal periodization model","evidence_position":"F — linear versus undulating superiority not consistently established","g3_supplies":"D101-09, §17.4","coach_decides":"Whether and how to organize"}')::jsonb, 'e3'),
  ('7862a94f-eb9d-56b7-a541-02a2eb44fd2a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-08', 'Exact concurrent separation interval', ('{"id":"CJ-08","area":"Exact concurrent separation interval","evidence_position":"No universal separation, sequence, or modality rule","g3_supplies":"D101-13, AS-101-12","coach_decides":"The placement"}')::jsonb, 'e4'),
  ('9014012b-3b30-507b-ac5f-49a793bcd02c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-09', 'Exact exercise order', ('{"id":"CJ-09","area":"Exact exercise order","evidence_position":"Contextual","g3_supplies":"AS-101-03","coach_decides":"The order in this session"}')::jsonb, 'e5'),
  ('73bac331-da70-53c7-a7ab-73b1ee7a3e4a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-10', 'Fixed GPP/SPP ratios', ('{"id":"CJ-10","area":"Fixed GPP/SPP ratios","evidence_position":"E / Organizational Choice for the taxonomy; F for a published ratio","g3_supplies":"§3.3","coach_decides":"Emphasis distribution"}')::jsonb, 'e6'),
  ('f2f31f9b-6220-5f84-a987-8621c29f9d92', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-11', 'Universal readiness threshold', ('{"id":"CJ-11","area":"Universal readiness threshold","evidence_position":"F for a score determining today''s session","g3_supplies":"D101-17, AS-101-07","coach_decides":"The readiness call"}')::jsonb, 'e7'),
  ('c60a9583-cf39-5a31-818e-4366f2063a25', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-12', 'Universal wellness threshold', ('{"id":"CJ-12","area":"Universal wellness threshold","evidence_position":"C for athlete-reported measures; F for a cut-point rule","g3_supplies":"AS-101-07","coach_decides":"How much weight to give the report"}')::jsonb, 'e8'),
  ('c70c78ba-56d0-560d-a6e3-f0ff22bbf0b6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-13', 'Universal ACWR safe zone', ('{"id":"CJ-13","area":"Universal ACWR safe zone","evidence_position":"C for association; F for a safe zone","g3_supplies":"§19.5, D101-17","coach_decides":"Whether to track load ratios at all"}')::jsonb, 'e9'),
  ('346d79fb-320b-5e7e-81f4-b6e0effd8439', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-14', 'Universal movement-screen cutoff', ('{"id":"CJ-14","area":"Universal movement-screen cutoff","evidence_position":"F for injury prediction from a composite","g3_supplies":"§19, D101-16","coach_decides":"What the observed movement means here"}')::jsonb, 'f0'),
  ('4597f8c2-f947-596d-89a8-e6656c0c12e4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-15', 'Universal asymmetry threshold', ('{"id":"CJ-15","area":"Universal asymmetry threshold","evidence_position":"F for a universal predictive threshold","g3_supplies":"§19, AS-101-08","coach_decides":"How to interpret a measured difference"}')::jsonb, 'f1'),
  ('3baa2fc0-576b-54e1-90e0-8e4854c6ae64', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-16', 'Universal youth age threshold', ('{"id":"CJ-16","area":"Universal youth age threshold","evidence_position":"F — supported youth training does not require a fixed age","g3_supplies":"D101-14, D101-15, AS-101-11","coach_decides":"Readiness for this athlete and task"}')::jsonb, 'f2'),
  ('23887a3e-96a9-5a19-a749-5abdc6dd4428', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-17', 'Puberty requirement', ('{"id":"CJ-17","area":"Puberty requirement","evidence_position":"F — puberty is not a prerequisite for meaningful physical preparation","g3_supplies":"D101-15, §18.1","coach_decides":"Maturation-aware progression"}')::jsonb, 'f3'),
  ('fd010e56-4d91-5119-8d81-fd5f47e53eb6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-18', 'Universal "heavy" threshold for youth', ('{"id":"CJ-18","area":"Universal \"heavy\" threshold for youth","evidence_position":"F for an arbitrary blanket prohibition; loading requires supervision, competency, progression, and context","g3_supplies":"AS-101-11","coach_decides":"The loading decision"}')::jsonb, 'f4'),
  ('aa294a94-5456-52a8-a0a1-dc18c0facfc9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-19', 'Universal exercise progression sequence', ('{"id":"CJ-19","area":"Universal exercise progression sequence","evidence_position":"F — no evidence supports one universal sequence","g3_supplies":"AS-101-03, AS-101-05","coach_decides":"The sequence"}')::jsonb, 'f5'),
  ('f4b1708c-3586-5e2e-90a6-47691c1d2732', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-20', 'Universal movement technique template', ('{"id":"CJ-20","area":"Universal movement technique template","evidence_position":"F — one solution does not fit every athlete or task","g3_supplies":"D101-16, AS-101-13","coach_decides":"The task-relevant constraints and the acceptable variation"}')::jsonb, 'f6'),
  ('e200277c-f82b-5837-aebe-78f402aa3d0b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-21', 'Universal specialization age', ('{"id":"CJ-21","area":"Universal specialization age","evidence_position":"F / Overstated — sport-specific and developmental context matter; broad claims exceed the evidence","g3_supplies":"§18.1","coach_decides":"The advice given to this family"}')::jsonb, 'f7'),
  ('884bf3f9-8973-5c50-a7e5-727a3055873c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-22', 'Exact sex-specific programming rules', ('{"id":"CJ-22","area":"Exact sex-specific programming rules","evidence_position":"A/B that relative adaptation is broadly similar under equivalent training; F for one female-specific programming rule","g3_supplies":"§18.2","coach_decides":"Individual modification on individual grounds"}')::jsonb, 'f8'),
  ('ad3aa6a3-d34e-5b81-b702-f24bb1bf7db7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-01', 'No pain, no gain', ('{"id":"CA-01","claim":"No pain, no gain","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Pain is not a required marker of productive adaptation and may signal injury or inappropriate loading. Discomfort and pain are not the same thing, and the coach must be able to distinguish them. D101-10, §19. [EP101-10]","sources":["EP101-10"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'f9'),
  ('adb61780-bdf5-514e-85ca-c07d128f0682', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-02', 'Soreness means the workout worked', ('{"id":"CA-02","claim":"Soreness means the workout worked","class":"F / Known against claim","g3_teaches":"Soreness can occur with novel or eccentric work but does not quantify adaptation or session quality. Adaptation is read from a trend on a defined outcome. D101-02, D101-10, AS-101-02. [EP101-04, EP101-05]","sources":["EP101-04","EP101-05"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g0'),
  ('dd19c934-9639-514f-8e51-9cf41f08f08b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-03', 'More is better', ('{"id":"CA-03","claim":"More is better","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Dose-response includes diminishing returns, fatigue cost, and context dependence. Less is not therefore better — dose is a judgment against a target. D101-12. [EP101-21, EP101-29]","sources":["EP101-21","EP101-29"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g1'),
  ('ad049a97-b11a-5a3e-97de-5be90cd8f726', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-04', 'Heavier is always better', ('{"id":"CA-04","claim":"Heavier is always better","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Outcome, technique, velocity, volume, and athlete readiness all matter. Load remains a primary tool; it is not the only one and not always the right one. D101-03, D101-04. [EP101-21, EP101-22]","sources":["EP101-21","EP101-22"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g2'),
  ('64cdcb78-fa56-5040-8214-6febdf9e1a50', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-05', 'Every workout must create overload', ('{"id":"CA-05","claim":"Every workout must create overload","class":"F / Probable against claim","g3_teaches":"Maintenance, technical practice, recovery, and strategic low-load exposures can be appropriate. A session with a purpose other than overload is not a wasted session. D101-03. [EP101-05]","sources":["EP101-05"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g3'),
  ('30f65cf0-1cf1-5e99-8394-8d4d38258a43', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-06', 'Progressive overload means adding weight', ('{"id":"CA-06","claim":"Progressive overload means adding weight","class":"F / Known against claim","g3_teaches":"Multiple variables can progress: load, volume, frequency, density, ROM, contraction emphasis, velocity and intent, complexity, technical demand, exposure distribution. The variable must serve the target. D101-03, D101-04, AS-101-03. [EP101-05, EP101-21]","sources":["EP101-05","EP101-21"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g4'),
  ('8a6d459f-8bf2-5bb5-bca7-6dc005dc875e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-07', 'Sport-specific exercises should resemble the sport', ('{"id":"CA-07","claim":"Sport-specific exercises should resemble the sport","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Specificity is multidimensional. Resemblance is not proof of transfer and may reduce loadability, alter coordination, or displace sport practice. Name the dimension. D101-05, D101-06. [EP101-03]","sources":["EP101-03"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g5'),
  ('41b0f542-c99f-56bb-bf97-08fddde06eb4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-08', 'Unstable-surface training is automatically more sport-specific', ('{"id":"CA-08","claim":"Unstable-surface training is automatically more sport-specific","class":"F / Probable against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Visual and contextual similarity does not establish transfer, and instability may reduce force expression. The method is not thereby useless — it is simply not sport-specific by virtue of instability. D101-05. [EP101-03]","sources":["EP101-03"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g6'),
  ('dfbe720b-8c6c-54bf-8c4e-c447d8cd99a2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-09', 'Every athlete needs identical movement standards', ('{"id":"CA-09","claim":"Every athlete needs identical movement standards","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Task-relevant standards matter, and individual solutions legitimately vary with anthropometry, history, goal, equipment, and coordination. Constraints, not templates. D101-16, AS-101-13. [EP101-01, EP101-23]","sources":["EP101-01","EP101-23"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g7'),
  ('ba1fed83-3302-52df-9d35-444d6a75718b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-10', 'Cardio kills strength and muscle gains', ('{"id":"CA-10","claim":"Cardio kills strength and muscle gains","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Concurrent training can develop both. Interference is conditional on outcome, modality, dose, training status, sequencing, proximity, and recovery — and it is not zero. D101-13, AS-101-12. [EP101-08, EP101-09, EP101-31]","sources":["EP101-08","EP101-09","EP101-31"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g8'),
  ('46137578-262e-510f-a11c-3c55ff9fefac', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-11', 'Youth lifting stunts growth', ('{"id":"CA-11","claim":"Youth lifting stunts growth","class":"F / Known against claim","g3_teaches":"Supervised youth resistance training is supported and no fixed-age ban is justified. D101-15, AS-101-11. [EP101-11, EP101-12]","sources":["EP101-11","EP101-12"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'g9'),
  ('d2c152cc-f9c4-529d-8097-eebf102d6059', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-12', 'Youth should not lift heavy', ('{"id":"CA-12","claim":"Youth should not lift heavy","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"Load decisions require supervision, competency, progression, and context. An arbitrary blanket prohibition is unsupported — and so is arbitrary heavy loading. Both fail. D101-15, CJ-18. [EP101-11, EP101-12]","sources":["EP101-11","EP101-12"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h0'),
  ('41c1e25c-559e-5cce-b7fd-f9b9a7ca4e66', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-13', 'Youth should not perform power training', ('{"id":"CA-13","claim":"Youth should not perform power training","class":"F / Known against claim","g3_teaches":"Appropriately supervised plyometric and power training can improve youth outcomes. Scaled to competency and context. D101-15. [EP101-25, EP101-26]","sources":["EP101-25","EP101-26"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h1'),
  ('f9a24f75-0e69-58b6-8e9d-be1aeffeda1d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-14', 'Athletes must specialize early to become elite', ('{"id":"CA-14","claim":"Athletes must specialize early to become elite","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"Sport-specific and developmental context matter; the blanket claim exceeds the evidence. The opposite blanket claim — that multisport participation is always superior — also exceeds it. CJ-21. [EP101-11, EP101-12]","sources":["EP101-11","EP101-12"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h2'),
  ('d37b673e-f574-51ac-89c9-52897f5009aa', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-15', 'More variation prevents plateaus', ('{"id":"CA-15","claim":"More variation prevents plateaus","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"Purposeful variation can help; novelty alone is not a mechanism. Variation must solve a problem. D101-09, AS-101-05. [EP101-05, EP101-07]","sources":["EP101-05","EP101-07"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h3'),
  ('dfd93400-f667-51b6-817b-45a2f344fdc6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-16', 'Muscle confusion improves adaptation', ('{"id":"CA-16","claim":"Muscle confusion improves adaptation","class":"F / Unsupported","g3_teaches":"No credible scientific construct supports the claim. The correction is not \"never change anything\" — it is \"change for a reason.\" D101-09. [EP101-05]","sources":["EP101-05"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h4'),
  ('1637d4d7-4982-5fd7-b1dd-5b42d0f68fa5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-17', 'Recovery follows a fixed 48/72-hour rule', ('{"id":"CA-17","claim":"Recovery follows a fixed 48/72-hour rule","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Recovery varies by task, athlete, exposure, and context. No replacement window is published. D101-11, CJ-04. [EP101-04, EP101-15]","sources":["EP101-04","EP101-15"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h5'),
  ('0f9d78f8-16b2-508c-83c3-1d0c11047387', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-18', 'Supercompensation tells you exactly when to train again', ('{"id":"CA-18","claim":"Supercompensation tells you exactly when to train again","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"A useful heuristic for explaining that training creates both adaptation and fatigue — not an individual biological timetable. Class E (§17.4). D101-11. [EP101-04, EP101-05]","sources":["EP101-04","EP101-05"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h6'),
  ('66731a39-d8f3-553a-b8ba-7bc7c78f3b2b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-19', 'Fatigue means the session was productive', ('{"id":"CA-19","claim":"Fatigue means the session was productive","class":"F / Known against universal claim","g3_teaches":"Fatigue can be expected, excessive, or unrelated to the desired adaptation. It is a cost, not the product — and its absence is not failure. D101-10. [EP101-10, EP101-15]","sources":["EP101-10","EP101-15"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h7'),
  ('084ed1ad-0c83-5562-acd3-82e50d9a578e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-20', 'Training to failure is required', ('{"id":"CA-20","claim":"Training to failure is required","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"No foundational evidence supports failure as a universal requirement; dose and task context matter. Failure may be used deliberately where its cost is justified. D101-12. [EP101-21, EP101-22]","sources":["EP101-21","EP101-22"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h8'),
  ('07ef573e-beda-5334-a635-6274de6d6fb4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-21', 'Every athlete requires formal periodization', ('{"id":"CA-21","claim":"Every athlete requires formal periodization","class":"B / Probable, not absolute","g3_teaches":"Planned variation can help — the formality and the model depend on context. This entry is a boundary, not a plain F: the claim is over-stated, not false. D101-09, CJ-07. [EP101-07]","sources":["EP101-07"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'h9'),
  ('e41d659f-331c-52f1-9736-488cce4ba3b4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-22', 'One periodization model is universally superior', ('{"id":"CA-22","claim":"One periodization model is universally superior","class":"F / Unsupported","g3_teaches":"Linear versus undulating superiority is not consistently established. Organize deliberately; call the organization an Organizational Choice. D101-09, §17.4. [EP101-07]","sources":["EP101-07"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'i0'),
  ('6165d52b-6f4f-5140-b516-0fe0bfb49b19', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-23', 'Movement screens predict injury', ('{"id":"CA-23","claim":"Movement screens predict injury","class":"F / Known against standalone claim","g3_teaches":"Screening evidence is inconsistent or insufficient for injury prediction. A screen describes task performance under a protocol. Describing is still useful. D101-16, §19.2. [EP101-32, EP101-33, EP101-34]","sources":["EP101-32","EP101-33","EP101-34"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'i1'),
  ('fcb9e3de-b3b5-54c4-9d61-977158484291', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-24', 'Asymmetry automatically predicts injury', ('{"id":"CA-24","claim":"Asymmetry automatically predicts injury","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"Asymmetry requires task and context interpretation; no universal predictive threshold is established. No replacement threshold is published. §19.3, AS-101-08. [EP101-32, EP101-33]","sources":["EP101-32","EP101-33"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'i2'),
  ('3ef0f5f2-020d-54dd-b0a3-015f013f75c3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-25', 'ACWR predicts injury', ('{"id":"CA-25","claim":"ACWR predicts injury","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"Associations exist in some datasets, but calculation heterogeneity and statistical concerns prevent standalone prediction. One contextual input. §19.5, CJ-13. [EP101-35, EP101-36, EP101-37]","sources":["EP101-35","EP101-36","EP101-37"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'i3'),
  ('7cae6c7d-2941-5015-b8ca-ea67418fd17e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-26', 'A readiness score automatically determines today''s training', ('{"id":"CA-26","claim":"A readiness score automatically determines today''s training","class":"F / Unsupported","g3_teaches":"Multidimensional trends and context are required. A score is one input among at least three (AS-101-07). D101-17. [EP101-15, EP101-19]","sources":["EP101-15","EP101-19"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'i4'),
  ('bce0a54c-c244-5890-a121-32821a308fe5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-27', 'Technology makes programming more scientific', ('{"id":"CA-27","claim":"Technology makes programming more scientific","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"Technology can improve measurement, but validity, reliability, context, and interpretation still govern use. A device does not supply the question. D101-19, AS-101-14. [EP101-18, EP101-38]","sources":["EP101-18","EP101-38"],"production_addition":false}')::jsonb, 'i5'),
  ('e0fb5163-c41f-5062-ae22-a35475051634', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'claim_audit', 'CA-28', 'A poor response to one metric after one block proves a biological "nonresponder"', ('{"id":"CA-28","claim":"A poor response to one metric after one block proves a biological \"nonresponder\"","class":"F / Overstated","g3_teaches":"Apparent nonresponse may be outcome-specific, dose-dependent, adherence-dependent, measurement-dependent, or temporary; broad global nonresponse is unlikely on current conceptual analysis. Run the five checks (§10.10). D101-08. [EP101-27, EP101-28]","sources":["EP101-27","EP101-28"],"production_addition":true}')::jsonb, 'i6'),
  ('ad038e87-e603-5ebd-8952-c24f29ce8b6d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-01', 'Athletic performance & physical preparation', ('{"id":"C101-01","competency":"Athletic performance & physical preparation","ladder_level":"KNOW → APPLY","observable_standard":"Defines both; separates capacity, skill, and performance in every claim; marks GPP/SPP language as G3 language.","modules":["M1"],"knowledge_items":"K1.1–K1.5","applied":["A1"],"cases":[],"practical_evidence":"Session labels rewritten as demand and target, in the coach''s own program","sign_offs":["PS-1"]}')::jsonb, 'i7'),
  ('1fde9ead-39c0-5318-811c-02af88c33831', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-02', 'Adaptation', ('{"id":"C101-02","competency":"Adaptation","ladder_level":"UNDERSTAND → EVALUATE","observable_standard":"Separates acute from chronic; reads a trend against a defined outcome; explains the repeated-bout effect without inferring failure.","modules":["M2","M5","M7","M11"],"knowledge_items":"K2.1–K2.5","applied":["A2"],"cases":["3","9","10"],"practical_evidence":"Trend evaluation plan with a defined outcome, interval, and baseline","sign_offs":["PS-1"]}')::jsonb, 'i8'),
  ('aec7c333-4458-5bec-bef9-7e0b9b70ab76', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-03', 'Overload & progression', ('{"id":"C101-03","competency":"Overload & progression","ladder_level":"APPLY → COACH","observable_standard":"Names the demand variable and the target it serves; never reduces overload to load; imports no sequence.","modules":["M3"],"knowledge_items":"K3.1–K3.5","applied":["A3"],"cases":["1"],"practical_evidence":"Progression variable selected and justified against a target — stated aloud","sign_offs":["PS-1"]}')::jsonb, 'i9'),
  ('d7b25bb1-0d9b-551e-8de7-8440edea59c4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-04', 'Specificity', ('{"id":"C101-04","competency":"Specificity","ladder_level":"UNDERSTAND → DEFEND","observable_standard":"States the dimension of specificity for any method; refuses resemblance as a justification.","modules":["M4","M1"],"knowledge_items":"K4.1, K4.2","applied":["A4"],"cases":["2"],"practical_evidence":"Written specificity analysis naming the dimension","sign_offs":["PS-1"]}')::jsonb, 'j0'),
  ('009e40a5-ffad-5458-99cf-fc52f4fe7f1a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-05', 'Transfer', ('{"id":"C101-05","competency":"Transfer","ladder_level":"UNDERSTAND → DEFEND","observable_standard":"Defines the target; distinguishes exercise improvement from transfer; states honestly when transfer is not established.","modules":["M4"],"knowledge_items":"K4.3–K4.5","applied":["A4"],"cases":["2"],"practical_evidence":"Transfer rationale with target and evaluation method","sign_offs":["PS-1","PS-4"]}')::jsonb, 'j1'),
  ('db40e26c-9508-5609-bcaa-1d93eda9ac59', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-06', 'Individualization', ('{"id":"C101-06","competency":"Individualization","ladder_level":"APPLY → COACH","observable_standard":"Individualizes from decision-relevant variables; defends a shared program where a shared program is right.","modules":["M5"],"knowledge_items":"K5.1–K5.5","applied":["A5"],"cases":["10"],"practical_evidence":"Individualization map with adjustments and non-adjustments justified","sign_offs":["PS-2"]}')::jsonb, 'j2'),
  ('a718a3a0-f024-5677-b984-f3da7d5e5c35', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-07', 'Variation', ('{"id":"C101-07","competency":"Variation","ladder_level":"APPLY → DEFEND","observable_standard":"States the problem the variation solves; preserves stability long enough to evaluate; refuses novelty as a rationale.","modules":["M6"],"knowledge_items":"K6.1, K6.3","applied":["A6"],"cases":["11"],"practical_evidence":"Written variation rationale tied to a programming problem","sign_offs":["PS-2"]}')::jsonb, 'j3'),
  ('ee889480-a075-5859-b0a9-27901d99cf93', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-08', 'Fatigue & recovery', ('{"id":"C101-08","competency":"Fatigue & recovery","ladder_level":"UNDERSTAND → EVALUATE","observable_standard":"Separates fatigue, soreness, recovery, and adaptation; refuses fixed recovery rules without inventing new ones.","modules":["M7","M2"],"knowledge_items":"K7.1–K7.5","applied":["A7"],"cases":["3","6"],"practical_evidence":"Five triangulated adjustment decisions over the practical window","sign_offs":["PS-2"]}')::jsonb, 'j4'),
  ('2f1f0ee7-7f73-5bda-96d2-b97006f145c4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-09', 'Dose-response', ('{"id":"C101-09","competency":"Dose-response","ladder_level":"UNDERSTAND → APPLY","observable_standard":"Reasons about dose and diminishing returns without asserting a threshold; uses MED and MRV as heuristics only.","modules":["M3","M5","M8"],"knowledge_items":"K3.2, K8.4","applied":["A3","A8"],"cases":["1","4","10"],"practical_evidence":"Dose reasoning with diminishing returns addressed and no threshold asserted","sign_offs":["PS-2","PS-4"]}')::jsonb, 'j5'),
  ('4be898f3-d704-5762-b04b-48b7da04374a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-10', 'Concurrent training', ('{"id":"C101-10","competency":"Concurrent training","ladder_level":"UNDERSTAND → DEFEND","observable_standard":"States interference conditionally with its moderators; counts sport load; imports no separation rule.","modules":["M8"],"knowledge_items":"K8.1–K8.5","applied":["A8"],"cases":["4"],"practical_evidence":"Integrated week with sport load counted and the trade-off stated","sign_offs":["PS-3"]}')::jsonb, 'j6'),
  ('3772e33b-a7db-5c9f-a837-510d6daa0312', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-11', 'Training age & maturation', ('{"id":"C101-11","competency":"Training age & maturation","ladder_level":"KNOW → APPLY","observable_standard":"Distinguishes the three ages; uses training age and maturation as the operative variables.","modules":["M9","M5"],"knowledge_items":"K9.1, K9.4","applied":["A9"],"cases":["5"],"practical_evidence":"Written distinction of the three ages applied to a real athlete","sign_offs":["PS-3"]}')::jsonb, 'j7'),
  ('2dded986-91b3-5a74-8d60-fabbc1b7469c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75', 'competency', 'C101-12', 'Youth athletic development', ('{"id":"C101-12","competency":"Youth athletic development","ladder_level":"APPLY → COACH","observable_standard":"Progresses youth by competence, exposure, and supervision; refuses age and puberty gates; refuses adult dosing.","modules":["M9"],"knowledge_items":"K9.2, K9.3, K9.5","applied":["A9"],"cases":["5"],"practical_evidence":"Youth session designed, delivered, observed; supervision documented","sign_offs":["PS-3"]}')::jsonb, 'j8'),
