    || 'a hypothesis with no falsifier.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who prioritizes reactive and initiation work, or one who prioritizes sprint-technical exposure, both pass with the hypothesis and falsifier stated. Adding a strength block fails absent a force-capacity argument the data does not support."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('f68cbe3b-590a-5c1f-b877-32e13ecbbc8a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '88e4e95a-2751-5612-b3ce-b3b6b7e32fd7', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-1."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('b291b868-3a15-5760-8066-add2bdaa7d00', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6c957173-5ff0-56a6-8458-ba3834e8de83', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('11300a9d-a78f-5c22-a4fe-cc3c06ac3c73', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6c957173-5ff0-56a6-8458-ba3834e8de83', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"d32a1c8d-354c-5a0a-af14-db0d589e9795","title":"G3-105 · M2 Assessment — Mechanical Foundations & Athletic Transfer"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('196f7894-dfc2-5339-9ced-89b04f5344ba', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4ccb9340-6e34-53c3-acae-ffbc5c0be001', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"\"Force-limited\" is a hypothesis, not a diagnosis, and not an identity. The skill is holding it loosely enough to be wrong."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('74cdff48-b519-58b1-81f5-5005cf179523', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4ccb9340-6e34-53c3-acae-ffbc5c0be001', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To establish what strength contributes to power and where that contribution ends, and to teach the multi-source, provisional limitation hypothesis that drives every method decision in this course."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('135a72bf-8e58-555d-8741-19198c8ddabb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4ccb9340-6e34-53c3-acae-ffbc5c0be001', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"a6f56cbd-f8f1-5ca0-8242-bf42fa1c304d","text":"M3.1 State what maximal strength contributes to power output."},{"id":"efe0a843-aaca-5022-bd29-9cfe07ec4433","text":"M3.2 Explain why the contribution is generally larger in weaker or less-trained athletes."},{"id":"e9f09adf-205a-53dd-9073-b0221a5bfa31","text":"M3.3 Explain diminishing returns and task-specific limits."},{"id":"afd6d449-9b5f-5f67-bf1a-a6be5a0463d9","text":"M3.4 Reject the universal strength-before-power sequence and the universal squat ratio."},{"id":"b2fa9d0d-840e-508b-9669-4649c8632c11","text":"M3.5 Build a limitation hypothesis from multiple sources."},{"id":"1d950230-7013-54ac-89ce-cec02f6b6d8c","text":"M3.6 State the confidence level and review point for any hypothesis."},{"id":"42a65a3b-1e52-5013-b555-0b061cdf4b42","text":"M3.7 Revise or abandon a hypothesis the response does not support."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('86adfcb3-7d3b-5478-b465-88e7415d5989', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4ccb9340-6e34-53c3-acae-ffbc5c0be001', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M2"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('b377cb2b-4486-5b0d-b4c6-3f3b6ce27b44', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L3.1 What strength buys","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('f837d2ed-efb5-5d07-a59b-b8c5853a6665', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Larger force capacity can increase force available across velocities. Real, and neither complete nor independent of coordination and velocity expression."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('af70f335-2b4f-5e1a-a162-ed5e39164c0d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L3.2 Who benefits most","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('8f69bc8f-e103-58e2-b30a-4845e9c05e8e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The contribution is generally more consequential in weaker or less-trained athletes — which makes \"weak\" a contextual judgment, not a number."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('1bf0431b-0a2b-5713-adb2-8545eb8f97ac', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L3.3 Where it stops","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('1fef5e61-d7da-5d35-b53b-4d2363da7b9b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Diminishing returns; task-specific limits; and why \"more strength always means more power\" is Class F."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('409634d7-a3d9-5652-bab0-effa71a26b3c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L3.4 No gate","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('6c279885-f445-5dab-96d8-6fbe282b4e37', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The universal strength phase and the squat-ratio prerequisite are unsupported, the latter explicitly. What replaces them: competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demand (AS-105-06)."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('e3f1941c-cb56-5f36-9c72-4271caecb013', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L3.5 Building the hypothesis","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('3d8b8273-03f1-55c4-91d3-75ce88e44716', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Five sources: task performance, force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context. A hypothesis built on one of them is a label."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('8c34af57-afa3-58be-9b38-b0c7010ea081', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L3.6 Confidence and review","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('12f3563a-6c7e-52be-9676-6be80c506e22', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Every hypothesis carries how confident the coach is and when it will be revisited. This is what makes it provisional rather than an identity (AS-105-08)."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('7d1d5b66-ea0c-5234-b20e-873ef6ec6dc1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L3.7 Being wrong well","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('bc8fd29a-83f9-5b63-93c1-8ecf058ab5b0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"What a non-response means, and how to abandon a hypothesis without abandoning the athlete''s block."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('4098eeff-0896-5343-b448-4161c97c9b7f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-04 — Strength Supports Power but Does Not Gate It","body":"Strength matters, especially in weaker and less-trained athletes, but G3 does not require a universal strength phase or squat ratio before appropriate power training. — Teaching boundary: Rejecting the gate is not rejecting strength. Competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demand still govern what an athlete does (AS-105-06) — those are coaching judgments, not a ratio. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('5cab7ccc-5b4f-5c0b-8097-8106800bb66a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-02 — Related Qualities Are Not Interchangeable","body":"Strength, force, impulse, velocity, power, and RFD interact but are distinct. — Teaching boundary: Distinct does not mean unrelated — these qualities interact continuously in every athletic task. The doctrine forbids substitution, not integration. \"Explosiveness\" is Class F as a precise diagnostic term and is replaced by the actual quality and task. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('e57b6e77-de3d-5852-ac26-e5f120793380', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-07 — Transfer Must Be Demonstrated, Not Assumed","body":"Improvement in a jump, lift, throw, profile, or laboratory metric does not automatically establish sport transfer. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-7 and G3 104 D104-2. This is not a claim that transfer is rare — it is a requirement that the transfer pathway be stated and, where possible, observed. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('28bcdbda-8db1-5fe1-bf3c-a69e57ee8015', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A / Known — contribution differs with task and athlete status","body":"Strength is a contributor to power — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('c138a3ca-a94d-5f76-b44c-686f52611fec', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B / Probable — \"weak\" must be task- and population-contextualized","body":"Weaker or less-trained athletes often benefit materially from strength development — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('22b0dbd0-daff-5a80-8e7d-abe98eb358a1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Concurrent development of strength and power-oriented tasks is supported in many populations — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('bff1bd91-1fed-5b85-89cc-5e1f52c6a34d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Athletes must get strong before any power training — Basis: EP105-11, EP105-10; EP105-11; EP105-10"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('d3b76325-b218-59f4-93be-0f5dc9970583', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — no validated universal threshold; the 1.5× prerequisite is explicitly unsupported","body":"A universal squat ratio is required before plyometrics — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('c9f8a2a9-580c-592d-8ca2-f56f3a5f8110', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — diminishing returns and task-specific limits exist","body":"More strength always produces more sport power — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('eef283ea-d0dc-50d1-8f99-3f79a2617278', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — concurrent and mixed approaches are defensible","body":"Strength and power require separate phases — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('5141050c-5d41-59ee-b1cc-7bbad4bd0720', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"A single metric identifies the limiting quality — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-08; EP105-02; EP105-08"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('0def3fd1-cfaa-5b56-a512-6234e09f4968', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-02 — Build Limitation Hypotheses From Multiple Sources","body":"Use task performance, strength/force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context before labeling an athlete."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('ab3f1518-8b5a-557e-a438-0387ccf9e274', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-06 — No Universal Strength Gate","body":"Use competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demands instead of arbitrary bodyweight ratios."}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('b7edfaf0-babd-5a8e-ab72-3bb702550c46', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('20ff7ab6-f971-5e87-89eb-a7d6dcf65805', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the hypothesis itself, its confidence, and its review point (CJ-05, CJ-06)."}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('26c53af0-5be5-5e53-80c6-875ce28a7df4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Strength-before-power absolutes (CA-01) · squat-ratio prerequisites (CA-02) · separate-phase requirements (CA-16) · labels treated as identities · single-source hypotheses · never revisiting a hypothesis that the response contradicted."}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('a785430b-616a-5919-bdab-4e4da1617a77', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"the strength–power evidence is adult-weighted; the squat-ratio rejection derives from a youth-focused review and is stated with that origin."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('4d917875-fe3d-5f3f-aad5-0037db6e7bff', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3bb65f4a-9f32-514f-8a29-8e283d59628a', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Applied at every G3 intake and every phase review. The artifact is a two-sentence hypothesis with its sources, confidence, and review point — short enough that coaches actually write it."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('a741778f-c496-5ff6-9ca0-7abc12b854d9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3bb65f4a-9f32-514f-8a29-8e283d59628a', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Strong High-School Athlete With Poor Acceleration. Marcus, 17, three years of well-coached strength training. Trap-bar deadlift and squat among the strongest in the program absolutely and relative to body mass. CMJ above team average. First-step and 10 m acceleration among the poorest in the squad, consistently, across two testing occasions on the same surface with the same timing method. Video shows a tall first step, late shin angle, and a hesitant initiation under reactive conditions. Sport practice is dense; there has been no dedicated sprint-technical exposure.","steps":[{"id":"6c916f02-2602-5027-bea3-e4e28b9e3e2f","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"5ca321c0-f264-5d02-8077-07ac2d9e96e2","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"a0164a05-1ed6-5a67-b9d9-0d7582380ebd","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"9881ad34-69a6-590e-812e-6605ad41d16b","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is the limitation hypothesis, and what does the block contain?\n\nFailure modes: \"He needs more power\" as the plan · a jump metric used to diagnose acceleration · an F-V label applied without a profile, or with one · vector-matching justification · ignoring the absence of sprint exposure · '
    || 'a hypothesis with no falsifier.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who prioritizes reactive and initiation work, or one who prioritizes sprint-technical exposure, both pass with the hypothesis and falsifier stated. Adding a strength block fails absent a force-capacity argument the data does not support."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('eafb6319-8a45-591a-8bd6-7b1db9ce680c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3bb65f4a-9f32-514f-8a29-8e283d59628a', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-1 (completed at M4). Observed elements from M3: the coach states a hypothesis and its confidence out loud, from real athlete information."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('c5bedb5f-470a-52ca-be81-06cc29836009', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '60f30586-7676-5ab5-869a-e6afbb62006f', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('97e5996e-0148-5d1e-9901-6c134be5e285', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '60f30586-7676-5ab5-869a-e6afbb62006f', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"71df8dd0-bf2f-5801-8cb1-f7f11fb8dab4","title":"G3-105 · M3 Assessment — Strength–Power Relationship & Athlete Limitation Hypotheses"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('b515b427-efa7-5e23-9e87-af3a9ac67945', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a0b6a4d2-463c-5ce3-b9bb-5a2b4aa3a541', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"A profile is a description produced by assumptions. It can start a conversation. It cannot end one."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('ae789722-ad43-583e-936d-77e87f0dfec5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a0b6a4d2-463c-5ce3-b9bb-5a2b4aa3a541', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To teach F-V profiling and load-power relationships accurately — what they describe, what they cannot diagnose, and why the load that maximizes acute power is not automatically the training load."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('3ee010e3-2a3c-5cc9-9fbc-d669f62cf1b8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a0b6a4d2-463c-5ce3-b9bb-5a2b4aa3a541', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"cdff9f65-08e7-5d1b-b617-ac5a3270008f","text":"M4.1 Describe F0, V0, Pmax, slope, imbalance, and \"optimal profile\" as modeled outputs."},{"id":"99dee662-cbf9-534f-adbb-740cacb6bba6","text":"M4.2 State the reliability and validity limits of vertical-jump profiling methods."},{"id":"2ec2386d-b1f9-57a7-bee8-e1c1a73c57bc","text":"M4.3 Explain why external load-velocity relationships are not the intrinsic muscle force-velocity relationship."},{"id":"f0fe8424-cd56-545b-aff2-f4af0bc6b6e2","text":"M4.4 Describe the load-power curve and why the peak load varies."},{"id":"a0bc4254-f981-5e50-8508-9f04c25cdea3","text":"M4.5 Distinguish acute peak-power load from optimal chronic training load."},{"id":"791f57d7-ffa5-5fa7-a1b1-6761ae44ebad","text":"M4.6 Use a profile to generate a hypothesis without diagnostic overreach."},{"id":"fa0c1769-ba25-5e2d-b740-f37f028d65a7","text":"M4.7 State when profiling should be optional, advanced, or excluded."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('6083a85e-b8c5-5507-861b-132f9691bd71', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a0b6a4d2-463c-5ce3-b9bb-5a2b4aa3a541', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M3 · Sign-off: PS-1"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('8cfb348a-efc8-5b02-b75a-04a8aa0a69de', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L4.1 What the model does","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('21999f55-4968-54da-bf71-d9a78a40f368', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Measured points, fitted relationship, derived parameters. Every step is an assumption doing work."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('945b72db-b181-5c31-8dc1-3e00b423a894', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L4.2 Reliability, stated in numbers","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('c1b23285-5c12-5653-a2f6-4646af85dc70', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"High linearity does not mean reliable slope; reported cross-method variation for slope is large enough that two methods can label the same athlete differently. That fact — not the label — is what coaches need."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('2ddcbbe3-7a2a-5a70-bdf0-6825c1dc8f58', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L4.3 Validity is method-dependent","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('11b522a3-a489-5bb8-b94d-039b772cc38d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Method selection materially changes interpretation, which means a profile without its method is uninterpretable."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('70004bf2-f96b-59a8-bc3d-fa610e2a7cf5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L4.4 External load-velocity is not muscle F-V","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('51c07bc4-1399-5c79-b166-8530adb936d1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The two are routinely conflated; the conflation is what makes \"deficit\" language sound biological."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('7528be5d-8380-520c-97e6-07355b9326bb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L4.5 The load-power curve","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('17fd76af-55c4-5028-9f90-7db9928ecf8b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Intermediate loads often maximize acute power, and the exact load moves with exercise, athlete, metric, and protocol. G3 publishes no percentage."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('d9ffa81b-ce16-57f7-ab54-14084690a014', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L4.6 Acute versus chronic","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('5d3da0b3-7a4d-5453-af97-4094ee1e1a47', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"§2.4''s acute–chronic rule applied to loading: what produced the highest power once does not determine the training load for twelve weeks."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('c5fc674a-dce1-513b-916c-acca6a745891', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L4.7 When to profile","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('471fee30-e2c9-528e-b809-73115487f52e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"An organizational question, answered by AS-105-11: does it change a decision here, at acceptable cost, with a method you can standardize?"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('d6eb4e6a-93ca-53c6-8c7c-21f99f69f606', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-09 — Profiling Generates Hypotheses","body":"Force–velocity profiles may describe performance and support hypotheses; profile labels are not definitive diagnoses. — Teaching boundary: The model–measurement rule (§2.4) governs this doctrine. A profile is a description produced by assumptions. No F-V threshold, deficit classification, or optimal-profile target is published (CJ-03, CJ-04). — Evidence class: B for describing performance across selected loads with transparent protocol; C/D for generating training hypotheses; F for definitively diagnosing \"force deficiency\" or \"velocity deficiency,\" for using an \"optimal profile\" as an exact target for all athletes, and for replacing sprint/jump testing, technical observation, and sport context"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('489c73e9-21c4-515a-a2ac-a59aa1024d0b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-05 — Acute Output Does Not Dictate Chronic Prescription","body":"The load producing the highest acute measured power is not automatically the best long-term training load. — Teaching boundary: No load percentage is published anywhere in this course (CJ-01, CJ-02). Acute peak-power testing may describe an exercise response for an athlete; it does not set the chronic training load. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('38349ac2-b890-5204-ba62-09779b320b97', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('21fce82f-5b0f-5c7b-be74-5aa1052fff20', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"F-V models combine measured variables with modeled theoretical outputs and are not direct measurements of muscular capacity — Basis: EP105-05, EP105-07; EP105-05; EP105-07"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('264326b0-e419-5343-b600-d9f9172722c5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — reported coefficients of variation of 14–30% in the cited reliability work","body":"Vertical-jump profiles can show high linearity while slope reliability across methods is poor — Basis: EP105-05; EP105-05"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('1ab4f890-40e6-51ab-a3bf-fa71695bc01c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Validity is method-dependent; one common method showed poor-to-fair validity while a two-point method with distal loads performed better — Basis: EP105-06; EP105-06"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('e43e8696-d038-5a63-b820-7064fe41eadd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Describing performance across selected loads with transparent protocol — Basis: EP105-05; EP105-05"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('270616c8-b2cc-538d-81c8-1510f240bd8b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/D — when corroborated by performance and observation","body":"Generating training hypotheses from a profile — Basis: EP105-07, EP105-25; EP105-07; EP105-25"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('a276e54d-738c-58b9-b768-f4c8da5bc663', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Definitively diagnosing \"force deficiency\" or \"velocity deficiency\" — Basis: EP105-05, EP105-06, EP105-07; EP105-05; EP105-06; EP105-07"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('6d050378-c742-5643-8802-bbf8a2abda9e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","body":"Using \"optimal profile\" as an exact target for all athletes — Basis: EP105-07, EP105-16; EP105-07; EP105-16"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('c68f5533-d918-5879-8869-52d7bed2075d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Replacing sprint/jump testing, technical observation, and sport context with profiling — Basis: EP105-07; EP105-07"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('f5c26351-8426-5864-b39b-b61c93d40d76', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C — Emerging; sprint effects non-significant in one review","body":"Profile-based individualized training improving vertical-profile outcomes and jump height — Basis: EP105-16, EP105-25; EP105-16; EP105-25"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('3cefad45-82c5-5ecf-bb73-5c4d54115bb9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Maximal measured power often occurs at an intermediate load — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('4c6b136d-3200-560c-97f7-89541af5b913', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"The exact peak-power load varies by exercise, athlete, method, external vs system power, body mass, and peak vs mean outcome — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('f63ae84b-729e-549e-b17b-d71373f4b128', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"A universal \"30% 1RM\" rule — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('b395c0f4-0fc9-518f-8733-efe840b75cfb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"The acute peak-power load is the best chronic training load — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('c6ca6faf-2b1f-5fdc-bca6-8db6f76564ff', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-08 — Treat F-V Labels as Provisional","body":"Force- and velocity-deficit labels require corroboration. — Enforcement: NovaKore stores a limitation hypothesis with confidence and review point; no label may be stored as a fixed athlete attribute."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('f86162d3-6c17-5e4f-b53e-daa82b80f3c8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-02 — Build Limitation Hypotheses From Multiple Sources","body":"Use task performance, strength/force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context before labeling an athlete."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('6b79abb7-08b4-5e49-bab7-babd6839da45', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('142b40f7-afc4-541c-98b9-01ab4ac40229', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('7db04c80-a08d-5fde-a30e-d0493c6bac2c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether to profile at all; how much weight a profile carries (CJ-03, CJ-04); the training load (CJ-01, CJ-02)."}')::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('3d16a103-ef9e-508b-9b12-ab8df82ed4fd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Reading a profile as a diagnosis (D105-09, AS-105-08) · prescribing 30% 1RM (CA-04) · using an acute peak-power load as the chronic load (CA-05) · comparing profiles across methods or devices · treating \"optimal profile\" as a target · letting a profile replace observation."}')::jsonb, 'c1'),
  ('c9d7da36-f2a9-5aee-9851-80b80bbd13d4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"profiling reliability and validity work is largely adult and vertical-jump-specific; the emerging individualization evidence is new, with varying profile methods, and requires downstream risk-of-bias review before organizational adoption."}')::jsonb, 'c2'),
  ('2c1a5d75-44b0-5a9b-a92a-627bdafb9a37', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5ccc1e15-0ce9-5872-9b17-eed48c42c16b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"In most G3 environments profiling is optional. Where it is used, the defensible form is: one standardized method, transparent protocol, results described rather than labeled, and any hypothesis corroborated against actual performance and observation before it changes a program."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('7ddb4ae8-7da3-5082-b69e-0c755d438a91', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5ccc1e15-0ce9-5872-9b17-eed48c42c16b', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Coach Prescribing 30% 1RM for Every Power Exercise. A G3 coach has standardized every power exercise at 30% 1RM — jump squats, bench throws, and pulls — citing \"the optimal power load.\" Athletes range from a 14-year-old novice to a fourth-year collegiate athlete. The coach also cites an in-house testing day where several athletes produced their highest measured power near that load in the jump squat.","steps":[{"id":"ba511be7-b6b2-5dc0-a897-a76ddeb7d258","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"208b3dc7-732a-50ab-88a8-49277588e7e4","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"6655e0bb-b16c-5f58-b918-c3de31a41277","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"6b645bd2-c0f4-5ab7-82b3-dc3503bd0d76","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is right about this, what is wrong, and what replaces it?\n\nFailure modes: Replacing 30% with another number · keeping the rule for one exercise \"because it was measured\" · dismissing the coach''s testing entirely · treating the acute result as chronic evidence · applying a 1RM-percentage scheme to a novice youth athlete without comment.\n\nDefensible variation: Retaining a loading range as a starting point for a specific exercise and athlete, labeled as'
    || ' a working convention, passes. Retaining any figure as \"the optimal power load\" fails."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('d832cd2a-22a5-5d4c-9549-8fc31beac293', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5ccc1e15-0ce9-5872-9b17-eed48c42c16b', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-1 — Power Evaluation & Method Selection. See §14.2. PS-1 gates M5."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('dd06828f-bf04-5251-92fb-74beabffee72', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '73f93ca7-7dd4-50ab-8e07-78295a95bf39', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('a87a989f-8a10-5bab-9009-530a27d1a67b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '73f93ca7-7dd4-50ab-8e07-78295a95bf39', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"c57e5687-598a-508b-a392-6d25be59769b","title":"G3-105 · M4 Assessment — Force–Velocity & Load–Power Models"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('71a5acf7-d417-5a06-82c2-94cf651d3adc', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"PS-1 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M4. Gates M5. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('824012e3-d843-55c2-892c-4d41265a68be', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('b760606a-d31b-51b8-8115-901c24fac86e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"571bf233-4ac4-5572-829c-b7b5111eafaf","text":"Defines the demand and the output requirement before discussing methods."},{"id":"b2a8816b-6a90-5c44-bd3f-3f962a66e804","text":"Uses multiple sources — task performance, force capacity, observation, history, context — to build the hypothesis."},{"id":"3f465166-7811-5f3f-b56c-4fc949fcfa76","text":"States the hypothesis as provisional, with confidence and a review point."},{"id":"0b5c2abb-a3f3-5f5e-9e65-068feae087b4","text":"Selects a method and states the alternatives considered and why they were not chosen."},{"id":"6ae7b75e-c6cc-54e8-83e8-8b5e28a2ec3c","text":"Separates evidence from inference when asked about any element."},{"id":"e270228e-9b96-5a9f-86e0-afc0bf39c539","text":"Uses no threshold, ratio, percentage, or label as justification."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('c06d60fe-ad07-5565-89f2-4ad4581627f4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('9fc57a06-74f3-5f8a-87b9-23fae99467f6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"c88db82f-1f6c-566d-84a8-589762f49e04","text":"power language audit (A1)"},{"id":"1c1a3d0e-ef04-5102-980d-8c11ab010dee","text":"transfer statement (A2)"},{"id":"7ebfc50c-de78-560d-89a4-1e5ef9c41232","text":"limitation hypothesis (A3)"},{"id":"db99cc32-f47c-5f63-b92c-71a0b512158c","text":"profile interpretation (A4)."}]}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('2d438c53-13fd-5a52-b351-fcd5326c272c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Assessor questions","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('fd4ce593-948e-5837-bf87-c91772c1a3ce', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"ce771e0b-43b7-51e5-8afb-15000993d3f8","text":"What is the output requirement here?"},{"id":"62634158-cd35-5c81-a65b-d8e841f0a375","text":"What are your sources for that hypothesis, and how confident are you?"},{"id":"f01432e7-ffdb-5825-9fc0-7ff3bbccca39","text":"What would make you abandon it?"},{"id":"d092af18-0070-5351-ab55-4e2e4cb91275","text":"What else could you have used instead of this method, and why not?"}]}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('1783a07a-957f-55f0-a289-231b02b0c5ce', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('28187c81-0a8b-5f81-b658-dafbe41fe80f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('90cbb503-8338-5cba-ac6d-ee0fa7b312d0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4344f312-7d4b-539d-be65-f13c37a72927', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"Trying to move fast and moving fast are related, and they are not the same thing. Both matter, and the difference is coachable."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('f3f247bb-4f60-563c-8e08-b5b6228a77c6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4344f312-7d4b-539d-be65-f13c37a72927', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To teach ballistic methods as a viable family with defined boundaries, and to establish intent as a real training variable without overclaiming what intent alone achieves."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('77338fb8-d02b-5e61-b458-351acae4e852', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4344f312-7d4b-539d-be65-f13c37a72927', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"c9398b2d-ec70-5635-9127-c73d60419f76","text":"M5.1 Define ballistic exercise by continued acceleration or projection."},{"id":"35a81028-4e9f-5eaf-9f56-8c9e5909c473","text":"M5.2 Select among loaded jumps, jump squats, bench throws, and ballistic push-ups by athlete and constraint."},{"id":"49978371-ebef-5e7c-a185-25211e5f664f","text":"M5.3 Explain why ballistic intent is not the same as light load."},{"id":"084b7859-ab6c-50f1-ba4c-c7f52cb1d91e","text":"M5.4 Apply maximal concentric intent appropriately across loads."},{"id":"49142385-963e-5da8-be24-73ca2d287dd6","text":"M5.5 State what intent alone does and does not establish."},{"id":"d70c4920-4612-5098-907e-c25f5ba683af","text":"M5.6 Select loading without a universal percentage."},{"id":"0abf8f85-c7e1-5e26-a841-0266497a30e0","text":"M5.7 Preserve output quality within a ballistic set."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('e1c5bb29-d528-5580-9515-15e7f5cac150', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4344f312-7d4b-539d-be65-f13c37a72927', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires PS-1"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('e73b8ea5-f261-5603-a6ae-921584cea4b9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L5.1 What makes a movement ballistic","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('80d99600-7292-5d79-9518-6b67f7d6a9be', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Continued acceleration through the movement or implement release, rather than deliberate end-range deceleration."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('4e47c160-d85e-5a9b-a8d7-efa7591dd018', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L5.2 The family","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('7e2f95ea-2dae-55a6-b92f-78d1ff7c8a37', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Jumps, loaded jumps, jump squats, bench throws, ballistic push-ups, medicine-ball throws — with their equipment, spotting, and tolerance demands stated."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('04bc83f9-8311-5f01-b161-502aaaae729d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L5.3 Intent versus load","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('041eb962-f372-5ac6-ab9e-1348b02688ec', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"A light non-ballistic lift may still require deceleration; a ballistic movement may permit fuller acceleration. Load and ballistic character are different variables."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('5bc4230e-f06c-5efe-9c0e-4d0c47016bec', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L5.4 Intent across loads","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('c2558dbe-3993-5bc1-ae68-128a94c4b316', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Maximal intended concentric acceleration is relevant even where external velocity is constrained by a heavy load — and it does not make heavy and light training equivalent."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('88bd4133-8467-598c-a0cf-cd5506dc3654', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L5.5 Selecting the load","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('a8f58aa4-01a6-5b09-812d-c7f4e53c7051', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"By intended adaptation, athlete competence, and preserved output. No percentage. Where a coach uses a descriptive load test, it describes that athlete in that exercise on that day."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('61ecfeae-4dfd-5fd7-a150-c4ab5f16103c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L5.6 Quality within the set","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('41e36f1c-038e-5a40-92af-866f00dce429', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"What output decline looks like, and the stop criteria the coach sets in advance (AS-105-04)."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('c22e8000-2dcd-5696-8caf-307053bbd2f4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L5.7 Benefit-to-cost","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('afdadf92-d92c-52e6-9103-340411a4eb68', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"AS-105-03 applied: when a simpler ballistic option delivers the needed stimulus at lower equipment, learning, and supervision cost, it is the better selection."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('bb7146a9-4891-538c-bb94-f8687eaa423a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('6941a1fa-c821-5ce3-bebf-0a49d18b3faf', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-05 — Acute Output Does Not Dictate Chronic Prescription","body":"The load producing the highest acute measured power is not automatically the best long-term training load. — Teaching boundary: No load percentage is published anywhere in this course (CJ-01, CJ-02). Acute peak-power testing may describe an exercise response for an athlete; it does not set the chronic training load. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('d40f6a33-2fbe-5a17-988f-503e8906745c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('0a4bb7dc-249a-5452-b142-7b2eeaea480a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Ballistic, plyometric, and weightlifting methods are all viable power-training categories — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-04; EP105-02; EP105-04"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('92c6e663-8e88-5907-ad8c-c4289841f5af', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — reasonable for trained adolescents and adults; youth use requires competence and supervision","body":"Jump squat / loaded jump as a method — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('338b44a0-678a-5501-8a01-ab3b2fa5c75a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — most evidence from trained adult males; requires equipment and spotting","body":"Bench throw as a method — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('407621e9-51b2-5190-811f-761981ec3863', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C — limited direct comparative evidence","body":"Explosive push-up as a method — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('423683ea-c6de-5dc6-ad1e-10abc2b5d18d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"No single peak-power load is universally superior — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('1d465070-f069-5d7b-922a-12c5366ff094', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Ballistic intent is not identical to using a light load — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('36471371-60f3-563b-a0f9-a5fdbe1325d6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B / Probable","body":"Maximal intended concentric acceleration is a relevant training principle — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('82a5369d-b46c-5273-bfbb-ade4fae16190', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Intent alone guarantees identical adaptation across loads — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('a2c01b76-57f9-5705-b0bf-2e1b0dd9717e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Explosive intent matters only with light loads\" — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('300e95c0-6032-5337-b585-9f6333cd305e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Power training must use light loads\" — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('111013ad-26c8-5b9c-acfb-578094352f28', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calendar."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('491a0abc-89a8-5ab8-844a-c6ff77895b66', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation."}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('0534f397-808e-50c9-aab9-ae6f8a58b008', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('40245b31-95ee-5a4b-ae1b-d20e294756e4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the load (CJ-01, CJ-02); which ballistic variants suit this athlete and facility."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('e1df44d2-4ce5-595f-b871-01e8abbb1644', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Light-load-only power training (CA-03) · a universal 30% 1RM (CA-04) · intent claimed to matter only with light loads (CA-17) · using the acute peak-power load as the training load (CA-05) · sets long enough that the last reps train something else · loaded jumps prescribed to athletes without the competence to land them."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('fb704f73-ab6c-58d9-bd55-fdcad3705eff', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"bench-throw and much ballistic evidence comes from resistance-trained adult males; youth application requires competence, supervision, and progression rather than scaled adult loading (Condition 2)."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('0c3475f0-2e41-5a4a-bc0d-6d3ac0569dc9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '95c04cb7-430c-50fc-9879-4aefe4c8d17c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"For a G3 group with limited equipment, unloaded and lightly loaded jump variants plus medicine-ball throws cover most ballistic needs with low technical and equipment overhead — a defensible selection under AS-105-03, not a compromise."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('9a5dc171-39e5-5b5c-82af-f39c6f93c1a1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '95c04cb7-430c-50fc-9879-4aefe4c8d17c', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Coach Prescribing 30% 1RM for Every Power Exercise. A G3 coach has standardized every power exercise at 30% 1RM — jump squats, bench throws, and pulls — citing \"the optimal power load.\" Athletes range from a 14-year-old novice to a fourth-year collegiate athlete. The coach also cites an in-house testing day where several athletes produced their highest measured power near that load in the jump squat.","steps":[{"id":"85ecb686-f5b3-55ea-a099-9cce177632d2","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"2c333d6b-5e68-51ca-a2a4-1f9838ed1088","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"9571eab1-3f29-5285-ab82-e5b873a87e4e","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"c3adca47-e6a8-57b4-b018-a12e9f04dd46","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is right about this, what is wrong, and what replaces it?\n\nFailure modes: Replacing 30% with another number · keeping the rule for one exercise \"because it was measured\" · dismissing the coach''s testing entirely · treating the acute result as chronic evidence · applying a 1RM-percentage scheme to a novice youth athlete without comment.\n\nDefensible variation: Retaining a loading range as a starting point for a specific exercise and athlete, labeled as'
    || ' a working convention, passes. Retaining any figure as \"the optimal power load\" fails."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('c5f70d97-e08d-5bc9-8b12-5322836a8150', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '95c04cb7-430c-50fc-9879-4aefe4c8d17c', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-2."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('9835c733-a435-5974-bec9-8805ef0db86a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '61a4aff6-6108-55ab-838f-6d371aa35a02', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('136e5903-7199-5c60-b4d7-305fdb0280fe', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '61a4aff6-6108-55ab-838f-6d371aa35a02', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"d66a8fd6-30ae-57c3-a289-533cd59b5a4a","title":"G3-105 · M5 Assessment — Ballistic Training & Explosive Intent"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('fb6f8399-b3da-535d-86ad-3d8837fc530d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3b8a624a-b8d9-5f21-9746-ad73ae3d6065', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"\"Plyometrics\" names a family whose members differ more than they resemble each other. Progress the demand, not the count."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('a3d6fcc2-9960-5ab6-beff-292096412545', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3b8a624a-b8d9-5f21-9746-ad73ae3d6065', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To teach plyometric training as a family of distinct demands, RSI as a task ratio rather than a diagnosis, and landing as a coached, progressed exposure with conservative safety language."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('d509fd07-ebae-55d3-987b-c453396a9aed', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3b8a624a-b8d9-5f21-9746-ad73ae3d6065', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"15086824-fd68-56a4-bb36-37abbc788335","text":"M6.1 Describe the SSC without reducing it to stored elastic energy."},{"id":"65217993-aca5-548c-b552-02d55940a4ac","text":"M6.2 Distinguish plyometric tasks by landing, eccentric, contact-time, coordination, and tissue demand."},{"id":"07c473e7-3ed1-5062-9391-262fc9667a69","text":"M6.3 State what plyometric training is supported to improve."},{"id":"a0e1af11-6a05-5403-937c-1c5081128752","text":"M6.4 Interpret RSI and RSI-modified as task-specific ratios."},{"id":"acaffe48-701d-5db9-849b-12f3e3f0eebe","text":"M6.5 Coach and progress landing and braking demand."},{"id":"2a2927f7-2090-5614-bbca-78a8771fbdc5","text":"M6.6 Progress plyometric demand rather than contact count."},{"id":"803131d9-c321-5af2-b98c-d41ed032e76f","text":"M6.7 Apply conservative causal language to landing and injury claims."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('41645d9f-f16f-57c6-bf02-823913f5a62a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3b8a624a-b8d9-5f21-9746-ad73ae3d6065', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M5"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('6fd1c03b-9235-5bef-b3a7-f55ae4570f4d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L6.1 The SSC, honestly","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('bd841a57-3dd7-513d-a087-3ec35d0f0b17', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Eccentric, transition, concentric. Elastic behavior matters and does not explain performance alone. The ~250 ms convention is a convention."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('1c60bf7a-c4bc-54d0-967d-4c912d6c1e4a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L6.2 The family, sorted by demand","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('c2db87b7-783e-5239-a888-c14e4dc4cbab', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Countermovement jumps, hops, bounds, drop and depth jumps, unilateral and bilateral, horizontal and vertical — sorted by landing force, eccentric demand, contact time, coordination, and tissue loading rather than by name."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('a03190c2-ffeb-58bd-98ba-8b4ef670f83e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L6.3 What plyometrics are supported to improve","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('d8221eb0-5bde-5758-9697-a4a2bfab01cf', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Jump outcomes robustly; sprint and COD in many populations; with heterogeneity stated and mechanism claims withheld."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('707081cd-8ee9-5b4f-b705-d1459ca8bf64', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L6.4 RSI and RSI-modified","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('64b9c325-8128-511c-99a4-6537c61c118c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"What the ratios are, what standardization they require, and what they cannot tell you (D105-12)."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('c336e19e-9bdb-55df-b31e-c71c04678395', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L6.5 Landing as the trained quality","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('157aa3b1-8df5-5f55-89c9-05dd7f1143c5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Braking and eccentric absorption; height, surface, direction, support, fatigue, and technique as the variables. Progressive supervised exposure is supported; single-cue prevention claims are not."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('cc3242cb-6414-5265-b8ab-9aac5ea51b26', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L6.6 Progress demand, not contacts","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('1971362f-12e0-53c0-bfb5-703bdcbe3510', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"AS-105-05: landing demand, velocity, amplitude, direction, unilateral demand, external load, complexity, exposure. No contact classification is published."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('5e718523-2ef5-5bd3-9ffc-5620956a02d3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L6.7 Safety language","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('85021225-19e8-5734-a199-452c22af64d8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The accurate sentence, rehearsed: exposure and progression are defensible; guarantees are not (§19, CJ-22)."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('4ed2d04c-2d88-5f87-8645-df9675d2ba28', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('7c268b49-c1b1-5fd9-b35f-2fa2d198c396', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-12 — Reactive Metrics Are Task Measures","body":"RSI, RSI-modified, jump height, contact time, and force-plate variables do not independently define readiness, stiffness, injury risk, or explosiveness. — Teaching boundary: These metrics are worth collecting when standardized and decision-relevant. They are not worth a label. — Evidence class: A that RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio; F for the claim that RSI alone completely measures reactive ability; F/C for the claim that low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit; B/C for RSI and RSI-modified contributing to a broader standardized monitoring battery"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('c6546d24-6f64-51bd-81f3-014294e1e3d5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-08 — Directional Similarity Is Not Sufficient Specificity","body":"Horizontal, vertical, unilateral, bilateral, or visually sport-like exercises do not guarantee direction- or sport-specific transfer. — Teaching boundary: Direction may matter. What is unsupported is the inference from visual direction to guaranteed transfer. Selecting unilateral or bilateral work by task, competence, tolerance, and desired loading is Class D — legitimate coaching inference, labeled as such (CJ-12). — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('a2841453-fa74-5d4e-a55b-d9e2e08f2eb1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-15 — Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management","body":"Coordinate power exposure with sprinting, jumping, strength, practice, competition, and recovery. No universal contact count, frequency, microcycle, or maintenance dose governs all athletes. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-8 and G3 104 D104-12. The athlete''s jumps in practice count. No frequency, contact count, or maintenance dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-15). — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('f9d09e23-db6f-5e81-a34b-e6d918258e47', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('f3b37aad-ebd1-534f-ac7c-69a2715fc939', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Plyometric training is a family of rapid SSC tasks with substantially different demands under one label — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-13; EP105-02; EP105-13"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('6c44ae5c-8b8b-5287-852d-cbf515854283', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A/B — heterogeneity is substantial","body":"Plyometric training improves jump and, in many populations, sprint and COD performance — Basis: EP105-14, EP105-20, EP105-21; EP105-14; EP105-20; EP105-21"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('1f6852fa-4121-5820-884d-2f0a0a3701ec', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Effects cannot be attributed exclusively to one mechanism such as tendon stiffness or RFD — Basis: EP105-14, EP105-20, EP105-21; EP105-14; EP105-20; EP105-21"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('6fb38de7-d7eb-5193-9543-20dda551dc1e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Plyometric versus resistance training shows no consistent difference for jump outcomes; one review found plyometrics superior at 5 m and 20 m sprint with no difference in several other outcomes — Basis: EP105-15; EP105-15"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('3ac8d85d-a6c1-577b-b801-0f11f0fc9ad3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — neural activation, active muscle force, joint mechanics, pre-activation, and coordination also …","body":"\"Stored elastic energy\" as a complete explanation of SSC performance — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-03; EP105-02; EP105-03"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('86ae031b-9a07-56ed-a04d-1ffba5c46b32', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — a practical convention","body":"The ~250 ms fast/slow SSC boundary as a validated biological threshold — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('5f288f03-1676-5eea-b33e-666b01b89a02', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('1877879d-b911-55a1-8a20-f176df82f3f2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"RSI alone completely measures reactive ability — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-24; EP105-08; EP105-24"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('a29032ae-e9c5-55d9-8c01-bf3cb3ead95b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","body":"Low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('351c4403-1ed4-5452-bdd7-fab46a5cd08c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C","body":"RSI/RSI-modified contributing to a broader standardized monitoring battery — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-24; EP105-08; EP105-24"}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('b4c2d7bf-564d-5479-9057-9e620a7551f7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Progressive, supervised landing exposure — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('a7ea7065-5d78-544e-bf0d-5f7209adec49', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/F","body":"Single-cue injury-prevention claims for landing — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('b8db6746-9bb3-506a-9c60-a4c5e40c7c2a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Universal contact-count classifications as evidence-based prescriptions — Basis: EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-19; EP105-21"}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('7e4583d8-558a-5710-a7f4-358a658bea79', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — sample-specific, not a universal prescription","body":"Twice-weekly training over ~8–10 weeks associated with more stable improvements in adolescent team-sport samples — Basis: EP105-21; EP105-21"}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('c57b0bab-ef9e-573f-97f5-61448303e191', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Depth jumps are the best plyometric\" / \"more contacts = more power\" / \"every jump must be maximal\" — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-02; EP105-19; EP105-21"}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('050fcb2e-8d72-5775-b35f-569d6f37a8d2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-05 — Progress Plyometric Demand, Not Just Contacts","body":"Progress landing demand, velocity, amplitude, direction, unilateral demand, external load, complexity, and exposure."}')::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('9a4634d7-5a95-52c8-a278-f4b8e3d28dfe', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation."}')::jsonb, 'c1'),
  ('1a46e439-6d28-5b16-95bd-829e8a9dff34', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."}')::jsonb, 'c2'),
  ('641222e6-9024-55f5-bfc1-efd0109d551f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-14 — Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure","body":"Supervise, progressively dose, technically coach, and scale to maturity and training age without unsupported prohibitions. — Enforcement: supervision ratio documented for every G3 youth power group; the safety claim is stated with its conditions."}')::jsonb, 'c3'),
  ('740192ca-a27d-56c1-a0fb-47b395a3769f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the dose (CJ-08); whether and when depth jumps are used (CJ-09); progression rate."}')::jsonb, 'c4'),
  ('a221c600-0862-5a5f-b81e-c4941d5c3b14', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Counting contacts instead of progressing demand (CA-11) · treating depth jumps as universally best (CA-10) · every jump maximal (CA-12) · RSI read as reactive ability (CA-20) · low RSI read as a tendon diagnosis · plyometrics called inherently high risk (CA-08) · a landing cue promised to prevent ACL injury (CJ-22)."}')::jsonb, 'c5'),
  ('415c5ab5-4b50-50c8-afad-bb88b30d128f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"much plyometric meta-analytic evidence is youth and team-sport specific with high heterogeneity; the dose finding above concerns adolescent team-sport samples and is not generalized."}')::jsonb, 'c6'),
  ('18ac58c0-757c-5b55-8d20-99eb8652fc1c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1bae1aea-abf3-5183-aa8c-90e17fed8989', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"In G3 Volleyball Development the binding issue is usually that practice already supplies enormous jump and landing volume. The competent plan counts it first (AS-105-15) and often reduces gym contacts while raising their quality — which is Case 5."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('3edf1ecd-2516-5173-b7a2-11d2444dab70', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1bae1aea-abf3-5183-aa8c-90e17fed8989', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Middle-School Athlete New to Power Training. Ana, 12, joins a G3 Youth group of 18 with one coach, two 50-minute sessions weekly. No structured training history. Coordinated for her age, attentive, no injury history. She lands noisily and asymmetrically from a two-foot jump and has never been coached to land. Her father asks when she can start \"real explosive training,\" and mentions a coach who told him power work should wait until 16 and until she can squat 1.5× bodyweight.","steps":[{"id":"ec00387d-4e5c-5547-bffa-9277c1eb7a80","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"af1dcf57-4625-548a-a200-fa9b7dcc60c6","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"14b66402-0b87-5291-86f1-1275a84329d6","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"62d625e2-4f3d-5e19-964a-4d02f764a454","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What does Ana''s power exposure consist of, how does it progress, and what do you tell her father?\n\nFailure modes: Accepting either gate · substituting a different threshold · \"explosiveness\" language with a 12-year-old · adult contact counts or loading applied to youth · complexity beyond the supervision ratio · promising injury prevention · leaving landing uncoached because it look'
    || 's like nothing.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who introduces light external load once landing is competent passes with a stated criterion. A coach who stays unloaded for the block and says why also passes."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('13fa63d3-6369-53c0-8a2e-03d1e0157507', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1bae1aea-abf3-5183-aa8c-90e17fed8989', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-2 (completed at M7). Observed elements from M6: the coach teaches and progresses a landing or plyometric task."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('496a6bdc-8e5d-5f1b-a884-2865fd893650', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '758d7ddc-2b5b-5b4f-bb7a-97617b2b011b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('2044e5e5-036c-58a7-b21e-833667c4a114', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '758d7ddc-2b5b-5b4f-bb7a-97617b2b011b', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"f87151a6-8dd1-5283-b055-d4ac866e817f","title":"G3-105 · M6 Assessment — Plyometrics, Reactive Strength & Landing"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('661a3602-60cb-5c9e-a735-b44e83c909d3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '27b5da3c-4fd6-5a96-8afd-531214e61144', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"The clean is a good tool with a large tuition fee. Whether an athlete should pay it is a coaching decision, not an identity."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('3d11d521-b41b-5142-a4b5-11b189ece6eb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '27b5da3c-4fd6-5a96-8afd-531214e61144', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To evaluate weightlifting derivatives, medicine-ball work, and upper-body ballistics on benefit-to-cost, and to settle the \"required versus dangerous\" argument in both directions."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('49a03e74-912c-5930-8d65-a5cac3ee62fb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '27b5da3c-4fd6-5a96-8afd-531214e61144', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"326cb7b7-2096-5525-9e78-d31148c506aa","text":"M7.1 State what weightlifting training is supported to improve."},{"id":"56309ecd-b4b7-56f6-aedf-5d6c34b8c1a8","text":"M7.2 Weigh learning cost, equipment, mobility demand, opportunity cost, and coach competence."},{"id":"7b2b13c2-9a27-5a7a-8c31-f0c7c292f08c","text":"M7.3 Describe no-catch derivatives and their evidence status."},{"id":"b9c35517-f27d-5c82-bb27-ef63b031f110","text":"M7.4 State why catching is not established as necessary."},{"id":"59fc44d2-ede4-5fb8-b55f-b277df72d046","text":"M7.5 Select medicine-ball tasks by movement, implement, and intended output."},{"id":"08a1ba02-3f67-52bb-a09f-77b8d3229698","text":"M7.6 Select upper-body power methods with their evidence limits stated."},{"id":"8b237614-d4d7-5274-b1f2-d7935d324c1d","text":"M7.7 Answer both the \"required\" and the \"too dangerous\" claims accurately."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('aef5eeb1-4e51-5b51-be88-ef1b391e8a1a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '27b5da3c-4fd6-5a96-8afd-531214e61144', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M6 · Sign-off: PS-2"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('7e0e163a-bc45-562a-9df8-06fa32d85e09', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L7.1 What weightlifting delivers","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('c99eaf4b-078b-54e7-8e59-78feb969ff35', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Effective across strength, jump, sprint, and COD-related outcomes — and comparable to plyometric training in several of them. Useful, not necessary."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('6cb798be-3abf-5354-9d97-295b95bc2f4b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L7.2 The tuition fee","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('d0551de0-1680-5779-8d02-80ef0995d60a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Technical content, coaching competence, equipment, mobility and receiving demands, and the opportunity cost of the time spent learning. AS-105-03 in its most consequential application."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('619b25a6-8ff2-549a-8607-0e1f7a95e1ea', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L7.3 No-catch derivatives","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('4f135e79-9223-5679-b162-fd0993af9d3b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Pulls, high pulls, jump shrugs — plausibly retaining force-velocity exposure with fewer constraints, on limited direct comparative evidence. Claims of identical adaptation stay conditional."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('a5391bbb-1c50-5f1c-91aa-f2e1f9315758', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L7.4 Medicine balls","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('58854b6f-a6fc-53ef-a435-53270ea2dd82', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"A genuine ballistic option for upper-body, rotational, and whole-body sequencing. Specify movement, implement, task, and intended output — not \"core power.\""}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('f089c76f-ddb6-53f2-831e-c36a7d93b80f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L7.5 Upper-body power","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('98a4a0a1-cd85-5edc-a67c-84db02a1e373', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Bench throws, ballistic push-ups, upper-body plyometrics; thinner and more exercise-specific evidence than lower-body jump research, stated as such."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('475c8e01-1145-5d4f-b757-f77e298ea995', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L7.6 Both absolutes, answered","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('fa77c02d-0103-5675-b114-c31074861114', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"\"Required\" is Class F. \"Too dangerous\" sits on an F/B boundary where supervision, progression, and context decide. Coaches practise both answers."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('50cdf784-31e1-58ac-a2c9-22af0d9080ea', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L7.7 Choosing for this athlete","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('3d84559f-cc8a-569f-9dbc-1fd432b8e380', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"A worked benefit-to-cost comparison, which is Case 6."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('ec0d66ad-6a6f-597b-9faf-383b96f47fe1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('a3adde2d-4ca8-5243-8583-84f26e60ace7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-07 — Transfer Must Be Demonstrated, Not Assumed","body":"Improvement in a jump, lift, throw, profile, or laboratory metric does not automatically establish sport transfer. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-7 and G3 104 D104-2. This is not a claim that transfer is rare — it is a requirement that the transfer pathway be stated and, where possible, observed. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('717adcb0-f24a-546d-8638-36111c5c6361', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('f949112e-db1c-562a-a79d-e84086da88d2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Weightlifting training is effective for strength, CMJ, squat jump, sprint, and COD-related outcomes — Basis: EP105-04; EP105-04"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('75f254cd-1dc9-5b1a-ae20-7dec2adc08d8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — indicates utility, not unique necessity","body":"Weightlifting and plyometric training may yield similar improvements in speed, power, and strength outcomes — Basis: EP105-04; EP105-04"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('ee9ee67d-0fe7-5f2c-afea-0b393c07fefa', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"No method family is established as universally superior for every athlete and power outcome — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-04, EP105-15; EP105-02; EP105-04; EP105-15"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('31ffad4f-f036-5adb-ae2e-45d31f735722', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D","body":"Weightlifting movements carry high technical content; value depends on benefit exceeding learning cost, equipment, mobility demands, opportunity cost, and coach competence — Basis: EP105-04; EP105-04"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('4ea319d0-e6a4-5978-ba2b-89301bb121e3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C — direct comparative evidence limited","body":"No-catch derivatives may retain meaningful force-velocity exposure while reducing receiving-position constraints — Basis: Evidence Pack §13; supporting record not itemized (§20.3b)"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('9e50f8c5-0565-5a28-bb5e-ee976a8630af', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Every athlete must clean or snatch to develop power\" — Basis: EP105-04; EP105-04"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('218861cb-fabc-5b25-99c9-b64085c08491', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","body":"\"Catching is necessary for power development\" — Basis: Evidence Pack §13"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('4b739040-c2dc-51bc-88eb-deb859918373', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/B boundary — risk depends on supervision, progression, and context","body":"\"Olympic lifts are inherently too dangerous for athletes\" — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('47d6824e-d001-55e5-84ee-82b73298247d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C","body":"Medicine-ball throws as a ballistic, projection-based option — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('8ba3beef-fc76-52df-839d-44019b5e95af', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — task-, sport-, and sex-specific","body":"A 12-week medicine-ball intervention in young female handball athletes produced greater throw-test gains than control — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('75d8b085-450a-5133-8640-79ee276f8bf1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C / limited","body":"Universal medicine-ball load prescriptions and broad sport-transfer claims — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('5d828fe8-7130-541c-a76d-a9a15e75ad93', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C — direct research more limited and exercise-specific","body":"Upper-body ballistic methods (bench throws, ballistic push-ups, upper-body plyometrics) — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-22; EP105-02; EP105-22"}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('b677898c-3d22-59ef-a9a1-34d01bbe554c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calendar."}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('8daab493-da50-5dfe-b889-3d297b3050e5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('00cfcc32-3442-557a-b20e-a1baf52e57f0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('f727fa5a-1794-50e2-b2a0-2ab7d83cf29b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-14 — Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure","body":"Supervise, progressively dose, technically coach, and scale to maturity and training age without unsupported prohibitions. — Enforcement: supervision ratio documented for every G3 youth power group; the safety claim is stated with its conditions."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('962d7877-b120-5f35-aa5f-abab36c56879', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether to teach weightlifting derivatives here (CJ-07); which variant; medicine-ball loading."}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('44bc79b6-e949-50bc-8e9e-d747e99c627e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Olympic lifts declared required or inherently dangerous (CA-06) · catching declared necessary (CA-07) · medicine-ball work described as generic \"core power\" · upper-body evidence generalized beyond its exercise · a method chosen because the coach enjoys teaching it · learning cost ignored in a 30-athlete room."}')::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('624c70a0-101f-5dbc-9680-193c4a867b29', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"bench-throw evidence is largely resistance-trained men; the medicine-ball intervention evidence is young female handball athletes and sport/test specific. Neither generalizes automatically."}')::jsonb, 'c1'),
  ('5b6c509a-8ccc-5136-84a9-86225758e552', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3a9fc52-e162-5c9e-9016-a1ff3af66ee7', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"In a G3 scholastic environment with one coach and thirty athletes, the honest weightlifting answer is usually a derivative selected for coachability at that ratio — or a loaded-jump or medicine-ball alternative that reaches the intended stimulus without the tuition. Either is defensible; the reasoning is what is assessed."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('cd876925-b5cf-5bad-82b1-301c4f6037ba', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3a9fc52-e162-5c9e-9016-a1ff3af66ee7', 'scenario', 1, ('{"intro":"Olympic Lifts vs Loaded Jumps. A G3 scholastic program, one coach, 28 athletes, two 45-minute slots, limited platforms and bumper plates, mixed training ages from novice to third-year. The head coach — who is competent in weightlifting and enjoys teaching it — proposes teaching the power clean to the whole squad as the primary power method. A staff member argues Olympic lifts are too dangerous for high schoolers and should be replaced with loaded jumps.","steps":[{"id":"7ccb1d14-7cfc-5753-9f8b-1e61738e557b","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"ba13d516-1c4c-59fa-9805-0fc52a3e8d5c","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"6a69a5bb-bc8a-519f-a35b-b33fd1b1da4f","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"65ba3ce1-385d-5738-912f-e494dba0a2f7","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: Which method, and how do you answer both arguments?\n\nFailure modes: Method chosen by coach preference or by fear · either absolute retained · learning cost ignored · no-catch equivalence overclaimed · the constraint of 28:1 in 45 minutes left out of the reasoning entirely."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('1f435804-16a0-5d02-8736-aa13af3217ae', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3a9fc52-e162-5c9e-9016-a1ff3af66ee7', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-2 — Power Exercise Coaching. See §14.3. PS-2 gates M8."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('2e0a30aa-7462-506e-b17a-c85f22b4264f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e11d14a2-aa69-5d52-8af0-d7ba9560be67', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('08386b90-1581-5217-b12b-0f9daf65d54c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e11d14a2-aa69-5d52-8af0-d7ba9560be67', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"fb464687-9090-5050-8937-df6544929e58","title":"G3-105 · M7 Assessment — Weightlifting Derivatives, Medicine Balls & Upper-Body Power"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('a5985382-46d4-5e4b-94e5-6af247130fc1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"PS-2 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M7. Gates M8. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('1c9f048f-c316-56f9-a668-67370aac17c8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('0cf45fb1-0a4c-5e46-a6f0-2f191e9b3c6f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"873fdf92-bea7-5b45-b5bf-ee3aa00544c0","text":"Selects a task appropriate to the athlete, facility, and objective."},{"id":"4ab98b88-9172-5a2d-b525-4e2dc72974cc","text":"Coaches setup and intent precisely for the task''s demand."},{"id":"3e2c7985-a17f-56ca-bd28-0fc432e253aa","text":"Coaches landing or projection as a trained element, not an afterthought."},{"id":"3a3df8bf-e40c-5af8-b857-54a138b40788","text":"Preserves output quality across the exposure and acts when it degrades."},{"id":"856eb1f6-5647-5705-8432-c2ae77b60ed3","text":"Regresses or progresses appropriately, changing the right variable."},{"id":"8e153e4c-90d6-5481-b6b5-5735235628fc","text":"States the stop and modify criteria before the set, and applies them."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('0d1f999b-e2fb-5d1a-8881-408948769cce', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('4f5201da-45b2-5d6c-8e61-1a47f155c041', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"f8b5cd1c-9e95-565d-b517-3713c768bbaf","text":"ballistic prescription (A5)"},{"id":"94199501-c116-5f6e-a398-213fe48fbd2c","text":"plyometric progression (A6)"},{"id":"5118c976-a345-59b4-a87f-a8e24a1d87e5","text":"method comparison (A7)."}]}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('f5898338-be23-5cdd-a74a-001386a173dd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Assessor questions","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('5ea1ea27-8266-5df0-9c82-44623eb41e7b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"dbe3d986-422e-5413-8c19-a136a905ffe0","text":"What is this exposure training, in what task?"},{"id":"a6dc17f2-bce4-5948-912e-f7440ff09312","text":"What are you watching for, and what ends it?"},{"id":"4437c003-265c-538a-a41a-1336291ac0b3","text":"Why this progression variable rather than another?"},{"id":"5939f1d8-65d8-5a04-90fd-6057bd703773","text":"What would you use if this equipment weren''t here?"}]}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('ee9552a4-7c0b-5c4d-9e9c-f7ad41f30d97', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('33e8ced8-40c0-5fcb-a5e1-d4d27c354ebd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('e8fd5d11-7367-57c9-a55e-422c8904e756', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1a4fed1b-fd55-5c2d-928c-24e9197335b0', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"Force-time data is the most seductive information in the field. It looks like an explanation and it is a description."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('d2b56738-673c-509c-a49e-21b1cc524e39', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1a4fed1b-fd55-5c2d-928c-24e9197335b0', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To make coaches capable of interpreting force-time constructs accurately, and to establish the measurement-window and reliability limits that prevent RFD and derived metrics from becoming field diagnoses."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('bd268835-1965-54a8-9381-50aa8198c7ce', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1a4fed1b-fd55-5c2d-928c-24e9197335b0', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"a27a0317-cca9-5608-92b0-6bc25d4609b8","text":"M8.1 Define RFD and state its measurement-window sensitivity."},{"id":"e14b829b-939d-58a2-a3fc-74e7e3718289","text":"M8.2 Distinguish early and late RFD determinants."},{"id":"424ed806-89dc-5e33-bd4f-3de31cc572bb","text":"M8.3 Distinguish impulse, peak force, and RFD, and state when each is task-relevant."},{"id":"fdafb5b7-a776-571a-87eb-15e3b94ed7ec","text":"M8.4 Explain why strength improvement and RFD change are not the same."},{"id":"c7fcc676-896e-5e4b-b1f6-6d0f3973cbb3","text":"M8.5 State the reliability limits of derived force-time metrics."},{"id":"8dc27534-cf51-585a-94d5-2d107e3a8b56","text":"M8.6 Explain why no single force-time variable is universally primary."},{"id":"b1501664-5036-588a-928c-5c3aeb38a576","text":"M8.7 State what field inference from force-time data can and cannot support."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('dc48e9c0-9901-55de-ae25-d969aaaee39e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1a4fed1b-fd55-5c2d-928c-24e9197335b0', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires PS-2"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('4ea0c7b6-8bcb-5453-94bd-b70fe2182f0a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L8.1 RFD defined and bounded","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('14cb54e5-44a6-5f6b-b0ad-6870bef53e99', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The force-time slope, and the fact that the number depends heavily on the window and method chosen."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('6317bf24-3d76-5004-a0e2-d0060f06274d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L8.2 Early and late","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('8dbaa73d-5b78-5290-bcf0-379b7fd74868', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Early windows are activation- and method-sensitive; later windows reflect maximal force capacity as time becomes available. Two different questions with one name."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('3abef07a-7086-5b70-8c7e-17056db722be', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L8.3 Impulse as the task question","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('7ccdd49f-473f-5daa-b6fc-1e279b070651', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"When momentum must change in limited time, impulse is usually the more task-informative construct — magnitude, direction, and available time together."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('d61e2b84-4a7b-560c-b9f8-caa3e819e7e3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L8.4 Peak force, late","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('d8a07324-1562-53ea-93dc-f9655736099b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Force achieved after the task is over is not useful force. This is why peak values alone mislead in time-constrained tasks."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('2c7a45e5-6f4b-5d49-bf1c-c58369e41079', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L8.5 Strength up, RFD flat","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('e4bdd534-1824-5b07-a35b-f0cb98fd6442', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"A documented outcome, and a permanent caution against assuming one adaptation implies the other."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('089bf0ee-ebb6-51de-94aa-20655a5dae65', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L8.6 Reliability of the derived","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('701ffad9-3da3-55f6-a210-a7747e3b0bc0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Basic outcomes are generally more reliable than several derived eccentric, stiffness, and RFD variables — which is the mechanical basis for D105-11."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('cc8a4438-6af4-5d47-981d-b3c0c3316f5c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L8.7 What a coach may infer","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('560b58e9-3db5-527e-bccb-285a9908a8c1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Description, comparison against the athlete''s own standardized baseline, and hypothesis generation. Not cause."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('30bdc4bb-6e54-5cc6-b372-753ac8eb1d66', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-02 — Related Qualities Are Not Interchangeable","body":"Strength, force, impulse, velocity, power, and RFD interact but are distinct. — Teaching boundary: Distinct does not mean unrelated — these qualities interact continuously in every athletic task. The doctrine forbids substitution, not integration. \"Explosiveness\" is Class F as a precise diagnostic term and is replaced by the actual quality and task. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('d24af595-9baa-5678-bda9-3ac8e6597f0d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-11 — Simple Reliable Metrics Outrank Metric Volume","body":"Prioritize standardized, reliable, decision-relevant measures over dashboard complexity. — Teaching boundary: This is not an argument against force plates. It is an argument for a small, pre-specified, reliable metric set chosen before the data is collected. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('5c0afc0f-86b9-53e7-9a23-d39fb550cd26', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('1dc4809f-5477-5cd9-98ef-4e4c51f2e21d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Early RFD is strongly influenced by rapid voluntary activation and motor-unit discharge rate — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('24b77300-e1f2-5a13-a7ed-681ff68e56b7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Both explosive and heavy-resistance training can improve RFD in various populations — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('589f156b-120e-5e27-bb12-37760382c972', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Valid and reliable RFD measurement is difficult — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('75acb62b-208c-5bfa-a566-5c86d395fdfd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Early and late RFD windows have different determinants; later windows are more influenced by maximal force capacity — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('ef92d1ae-17b7-5917-b2db-241e68aad1df', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — a specific intervention and sample","body":"Strength improvement does not necessarily produce RFD improvement — Basis: EP105-23; EP105-23"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('bb20f710-9879-5dfa-b1b6-9d8fd1f94500', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Impulse is the force-time integral and is directly relevant when momentum must change within a constrained time — Basis: EP105-01, EP105-02; EP105-01; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('90ed091a-860a-5072-aabe-fcdecbed28cf', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Peak force may be insufficient when achieved too late for the task — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('6a65ab6d-b5c7-5f3d-a161-65764e7d9e5f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Any one force-time variable is universally primary — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-03; EP105-02; EP105-03"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('7b19eaad-3e44-523e-b51f-5dcc8c424647', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Several derived force-plate metrics show weaker or protocol-sensitive reliability than basic outcomes — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('23c43deb-f85e-5bec-be47-0393ef68538c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C","body":"Routine individual RFD diagnosis in field settings — Basis: EP105-03, EP105-09; EP105-03; EP105-09"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('341c8e1f-ed43-5e42-a7e6-085378ff0fa7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('563dfc91-37db-5384-b602-ea20c6fb606a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('c6ccb0d3-76d7-5232-8b35-4e07403197c6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('27c3d3bd-599b-55d6-8b67-ba14eec97d7d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-12 — Interpret Change Against Error and Baseline","body":"Readiness and performance changes require repeated baselines, reliability and error context, and other athlete information."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('0fc9d038-2bcf-504e-b2ed-6acdb937d80c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether force-time measurement is worth running in a given environment (CJ-19)."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('1a0128ac-5918-5ab8-8c11-2a7b8ef17d8b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Using RFD and power interchangeably · comparing RFD values across windows or methods · assuming a strength gain produced an RFD gain · reporting derived metrics with the same confidence as basic outcomes · force plates treated as explanations (CA-19) · building a program around a single force-time variable."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('e450f938-baa2-553c-8186-cda365d61004', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"RFD methodology evidence is laboratory-oriented and adult; field applicability is explicitly limited by the source itself."}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('631a60da-6ec8-51a8-a348-a587df71e76c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c4cb921c-9d41-557e-af01-d6eab6c09e18', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"For G3 environments with force plates, this module produces the pre-specified metric set: a small number of reliable outcomes chosen before testing, with everything else left uncollected or explicitly exploratory."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('cbebb6ec-9bc6-5c54-9696-12497f84d33d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c4cb921c-9d41-557e-af01-d6eab6c09e18', 'scenario', 1, ('{"intro":"Scenario M8-A — The dashboard. A force-plate report listing twenty-two metrics for one athlete. The learner selects the small set that could change a decision, states what each is for, and says what they would stop collecting.","steps":[{"id":"a3043958-6d51-520b-9f5f-8421723e0b5b","situation":"Work the case with your assessor: name the decision, the evidence class behind it, and what would change your answer."}]}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('61e49ece-7898-50a4-b3a1-5de56441f095', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c4cb921c-9d41-557e-af01-d6eab6c09e18', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-3 — the force-time interpretation (A8) is required evidence at PS-3 — and carries into PS-4."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('19b347b1-205d-5b11-a421-5790e1c9e4ba', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '08aeded4-0f8e-5eb4-a80d-aea4d364c3ac', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('7d67a367-095b-5196-bf4e-60f14f3544a3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '08aeded4-0f8e-5eb4-a80d-aea4d364c3ac', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"04067deb-bf97-5a25-86d2-5fb28e960a0a","title":"G3-105 · M8 Assessment — RFD, Impulse & Force-Time Interpretation"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('4071441d-13b3-54a0-bb82-22ae835b1cc0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9c2afa8c-79db-5500-9e63-bd3f485ef061', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"Four popular methods sit in this module. All four have real uses. None of them has the evidence its marketing implies."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('9c106882-12e3-5228-a610-c86038a0d26b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9c2afa8c-79db-5500-9e63-bd3f485ef061', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To evaluate complex/contrast training, PAPE, cluster sets, and VBT accurately — separating acute from chronic evidence and measurement capability from programming superiority."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('2c1a8338-e966-56e3-9633-5340aa2cd14e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9c2afa8c-79db-5500-9e63-bd3f485ef061', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"a54244bb-a4a9-562d-af9d-93275e9e09e7","text":"M9.1 Distinguish PAP from PAPE."},{"id":"1ed0cbc4-109c-5dcf-9cfa-005f750c9866","text":"M9.2 State what acute PAPE evidence supports and what it does not."},{"id":"46159c5b-835a-56e0-91f1-6c18161db999","text":"M9.3 State the chronic evidence status of complex and contrast training."},{"id":"8c93a4df-5bcc-5c79-aa30-6fcd4945d178","text":"M9.4 Describe cluster sets and their conditional use."},{"id":"2353745e-28af-5538-956d-999dd07c6a21","text":"M9.5 State what VBT provides and what it requires to be useful."},{"id":"6b56d7db-e765-56c7-9ca8-64fb9fe86129","text":"M9.6 Explain why no velocity-loss threshold is published."},{"id":"3465c63d-ad22-5508-b342-4e60321f0f99","text":"M9.7 Apply the acute–chronic rule to any method claim."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('2a444725-9ebb-5a8a-a233-c91ada1c68b7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9c2afa8c-79db-5500-9e63-bd3f485ef061', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M8"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('4cbe599c-2fb3-55e4-b666-bcab4bfc7e27', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L9.1 PAP and PAPE are different words","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('5b3d4dd2-cd7a-58f6-a1fe-6e2d813159c8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Physiological potentiation versus a subsequent performance improvement reflecting potentiation-fatigue balance. Using them interchangeably is where the overclaim starts."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('227569c5-c340-5a96-9b53-0866ea35117a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L9.2 What acute evidence shows","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('58f63136-3324-53ed-a620-b593208edc94', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Small, variable, context-dependent enhancement under selected protocols. Where the pack reports a response window from its analyzed studies, that window describes those studies — it is not a G3 prescription (CJ-05 reasoning)."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('efbbc689-a7cd-528a-b402-65949e28abe9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L9.3 The chronic question","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('95af2939-2ab3-528e-aa98-2f83019fae97', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Complex and contrast methods can be useful; consistent superiority over simpler programming is not established and depends on status, pairing design, recovery, and total training quality."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('0fa369d8-032d-5cd9-b423-6edfc835b4fd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L9.4 Cluster sets","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('79b55f10-c168-5a44-b278-7f12ec8fc7c5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"A quality-preservation tool with conditional supporting evidence. Useful when preserving velocity or power is the operational goal; not inherently superior."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('f51e68bd-9b4e-5eee-8bfe-e24454c8c7fa', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L9.5 VBT, honestly","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('1a05b699-4ab3-537e-8854-c161832ff0ae', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Real feedback and real monitoring value, dependent on device, exercise, loading, familiarity, and whether it changes a decision. Not required, not superior, not dismissible."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('4d2dbb06-22b4-5036-80ee-d8c84d893d26', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L9.6 Why no threshold","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('7bc6ce54-f5c9-50d2-b78a-dddffbd856ca', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"No universal velocity-loss cutoff is verified across exercises and athletes. A coach may set a local convention; it is documented as a convention with a review date."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('16dfcbc5-7bad-592c-879a-007647dd63ac', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L9.7 The rule, applied","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('cfceafe4-00a4-58f9-828a-15bfa1a593d6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Coaches practise sorting method claims into acute and chronic bins, which is the reasoning T-01 probes."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('9981349d-5a39-51c2-aa43-347462cf6511', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-13 — Acute Potentiation Is Not Chronic Superiority","body":"PAPE may be useful conditionally; it does not establish long-term superiority of complex or contrast methods. — Teaching boundary: Where the Evidence Pack reports an acute response window from a specific analyzed study set, that figure describes those studies. It is not a G3 prescription and is never published as one (CJ-05). — Evidence class: B/C for acute conditional PAPE effects, which are small and variable and context-dependent; C/B for chronic complex and contrast training, where superiority over simpler programming is not consistently established; F for \"PAPE proves complex training is superior"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('ffd00945-61fa-596f-bdce-20d12669e00c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-10 — Technology Must Serve a Decision","body":"Force plates, VBT, jump apps, and profiling systems are useful only when sufficiently reliable, interpretable, and actionable. — Teaching boundary: Low-tech environments must remain capable of G3-quality coaching. The course is written so that a coach with a tape measure and a stopwatch loses no conceptual ground. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('20821ee9-0b31-511e-92c8-67a1597cfe2a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('68173d40-eec6-5e73-bb53-b07be637e105', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"PAP and PAPE are distinct terms and should not be used interchangeably — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('ed101336-bf7d-5a3f-81e7-eb538a00de07', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C","body":"Acute PAPE effects occur in some contexts and are small and variable — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('7d35b2d0-295d-5b36-ad78-af85c0f4a205', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C — acute outcomes only; the window describes those studies","body":"A 2025 meta-analysis of VBT conditioning activities reported enhancement with a response window in the analyzed studies and substantial context dependence — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('3981b793-6176-513c-8be4-6b912b9649d7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/B — superiority over simpler programming not consistently established","body":"Complex and contrast training can be useful chronically — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-18; EP105-17; EP105-18"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('cf5da774-c43f-5742-ac30-3e1a5a3a9a0c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"PAPE proves complex training is superior long term\" — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-18; EP105-17; EP105-18"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('ccbdc047-0857-5cc5-988f-361c18bd7b6f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/B — method- and population-dependent","body":"Cluster sets can preserve repetition quality; a 2024 complex-training study reported more favorable changes in certain outcomes with cluster incorporation — Basis: EP105-18; EP105-18"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('f79d1389-0957-5db8-85ce-63bbd8b9bd6e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","body":"\"Cluster sets are inherently superior for power\" — Basis: EP105-18; EP105-18"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('b0b3004a-36ad-5ac4-af4f-86bd4de54127', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"VBT can provide feedback and monitor load-velocity, effort, and within-session velocity loss — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-24; EP105-17; EP105-24"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('f5878f8e-340c-5836-a024-0c1eb21af183', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"VBT usefulness depends on device validity, exercise specificity, loading, familiarity, and whether the measure changes a decision — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-24; EP105-17; EP105-24"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('c224491e-af56-59e3-b8ab-dc8e800519e8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"VBT is required for power development\" — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('345be439-6566-5eca-b92e-0a41e6dbed81', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not verified for all exercises and athletes","body":"A universal velocity-loss cutoff — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('5ec6f669-e937-5f84-988a-41dfdd8b0ea4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-07 — Separate Acute and Chronic Evidence","body":"Do not use PAPE, acute peak-power loads, or acute force-plate responses as proof of superior long-term programming. — Enforcement: acute and chronic claims are cited and labeled separately in every module evidence block."}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('03003eff-08fa-5162-81c8-64b036112254', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('5a3d6a2a-da4f-5698-9e62-d3566dd07769', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('f69f183d-f6e9-57f1-afa0-b4ea90e6afaf', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('723c63c4-a968-588f-aa34-51b277c6ee66', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether to use any of these methods; any local velocity-loss convention (CJ-10); rest within contrast pairs (CJ-11)."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('8ffa3d36-dc5b-5f17-be34-76038135f059', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"PAPE cited as proof of chronic superiority (CA-23) · cluster sets declared inherently superior (CA-24) · VBT declared required (CA-18) · an imported velocity-loss threshold · a study''s response window quoted as a prescription · dismissing all four methods because they are over-marketed."}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('9c896b8f-bb60-5e04-866e-492241afa5be', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"PAPE, VBT, and cluster evidence is dominated by resistance-trained adults, frequently male, in laboratory or controlled settings."}')::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('9b23042a-c4d1-56f2-aa55-cdf1708aaeaa', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b841a078-42eb-5ccb-a0bb-ca0c429b0efe', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The most common defensible G3 use is contrast pairing in a session where the objective is quality, with the coach able to say the pairing is an organizational choice with conditional support — not a potentiation system."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('27299b9f-06b5-5809-829e-55404c2bf388', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b841a078-42eb-5ccb-a0bb-ca0c429b0efe', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Complex Training and PAPE. A coach proposes rebuilding the squad''s power block around complex training, arguing that PAPE research demonstrates superiority over separate strength and power work. The proposal cites an acute meta-analysis reporting performance enhancement after conditioning activities, and specifies a fixed rest window taken from that analysis. The squad is scholastic, mixed training age, two slots weekly.","steps":[{"id":"318e125f-0e16-5f71-b821-ca84c371ab5b","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"c7b2128a-bb0b-5371-b6b0-3481af063d66","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"49b7190e-25d3-58a0-bf12-0609320d0677","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"047a3b4d-c371-51ff-af08-409079f87031","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is defensible in this proposal, and what is the rewrite?\n\nFailure modes: Acute evidence cited as chronic proof (CA-23) · an imported rest window presented as a prescription · population transfer ignored · cluster sets or contrast pairing declared inherently superior (CA-24) · abandoning the structure entirely in reaction to the overclaim.\n\nDefensible variation: Using complex pairing passes. Not using it passes. Retaining the superior'
    || 'ity claim in any hedged form fails."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('79deeb2d-e970-5641-9b24-bf9bda9c1ed1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b841a078-42eb-5ccb-a0bb-ca0c429b0efe', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-3 — the method claim audit (A9) is required evidence at PS-3 — and carries into PS-4."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('d94a991c-c63e-53fd-abb3-57dfae0ef679', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '270c81c3-e403-507b-89ef-8325a1418939', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('a1be79e0-9cd1-5e71-9579-12405a36f640', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '270c81c3-e403-507b-89ef-8325a1418939', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"35c5426f-f5e0-5262-8361-0b58f04f6191","title":"G3-105 · M9 Assessment — Complex/Contrast, PAPE, Cluster Sets & VBT"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('33b57c49-19d1-50a4-acd9-fb0a17371124', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '72af9cc7-20bd-53ff-83a6-17aed5e481c3', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"The athlete''s jumps in practice count. Every honest power dose starts by subtracting."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('e805f723-e353-5728-9c26-6f6c4e52e3d7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '72af9cc7-20bd-53ff-83a6-17aed5e481c3', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To teach dose, rest, and order as quality-preservation decisions, and to integrate power exposure with the total high-output load the athlete already carries."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('bcb153f9-ce2d-5ca3-a7e3-6e4a5bf380bb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '72af9cc7-20bd-53ff-83a6-17aed5e481c3', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"dc96799f-2527-5b40-83dd-48678d4fa4d0","text":"M10.1 Set dose by preserved quality rather than by volume."},{"id":"499d9479-d7c0-5e64-bcb0-ba420143b394","text":"M10.2 Apply rest to preserve intended output."},{"id":"6f49553a-12bf-5495-ab44-f359bda9ab27","text":"M10.3 Sequence power work relative to other qualities."},{"id":"577ed825-5354-56d9-bbe7-1174f6433c6d","text":"M10.4 Explain the basis and limits of \"power first\" sequencing."},{"id":"6d04e369-6563-5c66-98b2-587f0206d3f4","text":"M10.5 Count existing sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure."},{"id":"9f313462-e149-52ca-9b9f-50ddd6d3a696","text":"M10.6 State why no universal weekly frequency or microcycle model is published."},{"id":"8201c54e-1310-5e76-ab1f-3ad013162058","text":"M10.7 Define stop and modify criteria in advance."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('01af91a6-229b-511d-b6ce-65a185ed7f2f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '72af9cc7-20bd-53ff-83a6-17aed5e481c3', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M9"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('4e2e349f-073e-5026-ae3e-0033225f3c8c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L10.1 Dose by quality","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('8c46b860-4015-536f-8060-229476e6e6b4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The criterion is whether the athlete is still producing the intended output. Volume is an input, not a goal."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('33dbeca8-4a1e-5483-acb1-0f0a67e7bfbc', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L10.2 Rest for output","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('e8b99933-e43a-55d7-b897-9803faab72fb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"More recovery than hypertrophy work when output is the priority; exact intervals contextual. No number published."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('0e65956e-d9d9-5f52-955d-88cb24080aa5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L10.3 Order","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('e1c430b0-ec1c-5283-86c5-fd5844bb1266', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"\"Power first\" is supported principally as a practical way to protect speed, technique, and high-intensity output from preceding fatigue — a B/D position, not a universal law, and contrast work shows preceding strength work is sometimes deliberate."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('959b0df8-1bbe-5266-a598-1151e41a3f69', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L10.4 Counting what exists","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('e5c9b35a-4721-5055-99e3-41e2da3a8652', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure — counted before anything is added (AS-105-15). In jump-heavy sports this usually reduces gym volume."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('9a3289ea-5827-59e7-a52c-608511c064d9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L10.5 Weekly organization","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('f0ca9dd4-ada9-5f22-83b4-c7aeb3fb7379', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"No universal frequency, high/low model, or microcycle template. The interaction with practice, competition, training age, total workload, and recovery determines the practical dose."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('45779eb7-a73e-5b44-a15e-3804e941f970', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L10.6 Stop and modify criteria","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('c717a206-571b-5598-9a89-0fc4770361b1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Written in advance: what output decline, technical change, or landing quality change ends the exposure."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('661ce268-caf7-5e24-86b5-531867a8c3c4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L10.7 The subtraction habit","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('89484651-abbc-59fd-9e4b-1776238af091', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Building a week by removing redundant high-output exposure before adding anything."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('777ac0db-b1b6-50f5-bf5f-1524112cc63a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('b4abd9fc-e813-5aa7-8416-c011903fab55', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-15 — Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management","body":"Coordinate power exposure with sprinting, jumping, strength, practice, competition, and recovery. No universal contact count, frequency, microcycle, or maintenance dose governs all athletes. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-8 and G3 104 D104-12. The athlete''s jumps in practice count. No frequency, contact count, or maintenance dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-15). — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('184260ed-6bc6-5a6b-8d2b-ed41d6950907', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('53852f9b-f748-5eb9-b5e5-f1dc939527d3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B for quality-oriented dosing; D for exact field prescriptions","body":"No exact power-training dose is established across methods — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('a9ebf326-d6fc-5b94-a7cb-610166349d8b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Power work generally requires more recovery than hypertrophy-oriented training when preserving output is the priority — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('45a021ba-63ef-5ce6-a5bc-5f74218ab9bd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Exact rest requirements vary by task, load, athlete, and objective — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('5bd91fe0-9943-5874-a1fd-2b8f2746988c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Velocity or power drop-off can indicate changing session quality where valid measurement exists — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('e3398cc3-0b7b-5984-9a00-d4d3935997b3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not verified","body":"A universal velocity-loss cutoff — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('db992249-f7b3-5eab-8613-f91822b0979c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D — specific sequencing remains contextual","body":"Prioritizing high-quality power and speed tasks before substantial fatigue when maximal output is the objective — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-17; EP105-02; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('c668a83a-f835-5f89-8d37-bf885f687550', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class D for exact templates; B for integrating with total high-intensity load","body":"No universal weekly frequency, high/low organization, or microcycle model is established — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-02; EP105-19; EP105-21"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('12f4a5b1-9c1c-53a9-a577-682be7867f11', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — describes the intervention literature, not a prescription","body":"Plyometric interventions commonly used 1–3 sessions weekly over 4–16 weeks with no clear dose-response evidence for exact optimal variables — Basis: EP105-19; EP105-19"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('fa7bb789-4715-5ffc-a8fa-6f32ddd2f673', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"More contacts always produces more power\" — Basis: EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-19; EP105-21"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('fcded6fe-062f-5636-a655-715d30a1c7e9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/B boundary — may train fatigue tolerance, not maximal power","body":"\"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('97b146d2-f76c-52b9-8821-a5d4b089aa2b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('168fe9f0-19be-5b0c-86ba-dc44184b8854', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-15 — Integrate the Weekly High-Output Load","body":"Account for total sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure."}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('b5ad6443-e3e6-5143-951d-f0cf6d5a8d0b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-05 — Progress Plyometric Demand, Not Just Contacts","body":"Progress landing demand, velocity, amplitude, direction, unilateral demand, external load, complexity, and exposure."}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('f01f2f91-fc0b-567a-beef-d324f2e51f24', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calendar."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('976012b4-2f8f-56ca-8e78-8fa7d29a98e3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"dose, rest, frequency, order, and organization (CJ-08, CJ-10 through CJ-14)."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('3719eb05-bfe6-5dd5-88c8-6b741614157b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Adding power volume to a full week · counting contacts as the dose (CA-11) · cutting rest to increase difficulty · importing a velocity-loss threshold · treating \"power first\" as an inviolable rule · justifying fatigued power work as sport-specific (CA-26) · no stop criteria written in advance."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('0488c8ad-73e7-531d-838b-82b76d2f6395', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"dose literature is heterogeneous across ages, sports, and training status; the descriptive intervention ranges above are not prescriptions for any G3 athlete."}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('228cf696-4274-592e-95b6-665e70dc60eb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '46896eda-5d71-5b00-815a-3d5dd6e53818', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"For a G3 in-season team, this module usually produces fewer, better exposures placed where output can be expressed, with practice jump volume counted and gym contacts reduced accordingly — which is exactly Case 5."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('626cecc0-916e-5c12-a99b-03418cc66760', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '46896eda-5d71-5b00-815a-3d5dd6e53818', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Volleyball Athlete With High Jump Volume. A G3 Volleyball Development athlete, 16, mid-season. Five practices weekly with high jump volume, a Saturday tournament format with up to five matches, and two G3 slots. The current gym program includes a plyometric block written at the start of the season and unchanged since — three sessions weekly with a fixed contact count. The athlete reports persistent lower-leg soreness and her landing quality visibly degrades late in practice.","steps":[{"id":"88a6b992-b87d-595c-a3ce-f490ad37e04e","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"343acb27-a593-5c38-b810-4a290ff61c2d","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"615b71fb-1f72-5947-a6d5-ca39536a2b82","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"204a8619-c05b-58bc-96f5-1de33bd727da","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What changes?\n\nFailure modes: Adding volume to a full week · retaining a fixed contact prescription · reading soreness as effectiveness · declaring plyometrics unsafe · ignoring the landing-quality observation · promising the change prevents injury.\n\nDefensible variation: Any reduction justified by the counted exposure passes, including removing gym plyometrics entirely for a period w'
    || 'hile retaining landing quality work. Keeping the fixed contact count fails."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('b7748170-b5a7-506f-bcfd-3f5f843b3777', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '46896eda-5d71-5b00-815a-3d5dd6e53818', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-3 and PS-4."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('3a103626-c414-58e5-93d5-cc17647b01ea', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '97e5b2c9-9476-547e-8522-17064f3d152f', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('e4c3362c-4660-5526-8cd4-f0b859858770', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '97e5b2c9-9476-547e-8522-17064f3d152f', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"3b3e50c7-fb5d-506f-849e-25d376bfdaad","title":"G3-105 · M10 Assessment — Dosage, Rest, Exercise Order & Weekly Integration"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('ce02ee66-e524-5246-a9b7-4de78296b8fd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'f5626564-f1af-58da-8dd5-532c0e509e73', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"Two questions this module refuses to answer with a number: how much power work maintains power in-season, and what a female athlete needs that a male athlete does not."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('3214cd13-5a20-55e2-8f15-747942bf2e0a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'f5626564-f1af-58da-8dd5-532c0e509e73', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To organize power development over time without adopting a validated-sounding model, and to establish the youth and female-athlete positions this course carries."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('8616e0d2-1d0c-5f4b-b80f-b2457e012032', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'f5626564-f1af-58da-8dd5-532c0e509e73', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"f4164b84-5af2-5b1a-adb6-e23bcb50d28d","text":"M11.1 Describe concurrent and mixed-method routes to strength and power adaptation."},{"id":"3d79a282-e758-56ed-ab76-5d7d1ac2d6fb","text":"M11.2 Reject mandatory strength-then-power sequencing."},{"id":"bd9878bf-a645-50d2-9b1b-ad3f4239e167","text":"M11.3 Describe named periodization systems as influential frameworks."},{"id":"cf488e01-bb13-5a65-834e-183035acb29d","text":"M11.4 Prescribe in-season maintenance as a defended reduction with a verification plan."},{"id":"e4b5e37e-cd60-512b-a203-8b7b17623194","text":"M11.5 State the unresolved status of exact maintenance and minimum-effective doses."},{"id":"fd280d06-18ac-5c75-9ac9-f94e3a2a37f9","text":"M11.6 Apply youth progression by competence and exposure."},{"id":"98d2627f-0033-54ad-b776-cab2a719f5f3","text":"M11.7 State the female-athlete evidence position and what it does and does not license."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('acdf9e5f-6e3b-55a4-a984-9619263659cf', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'f5626564-f1af-58da-8dd5-532c0e509e73', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M10 · Sign-off: PS-3"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('915807b6-9579-568c-80b7-e976469ab737', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L11.1 Multiple routes","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('8e81f5e1-acc7-5d95-a963-1511430fbf52', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Concurrent and mixed approaches are supported; no sequence is mandated. This closes the loop opened by D105-04."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('531c8eb5-53ce-52a6-b5e8-a4e36a0ee0d1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L11.2 Systems as frameworks","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('01b46620-f43e-507e-a7ad-928dbbca8250', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Phase potentiation, blocks, French contrast, triphasic, and similar systems taught as Class E — influential, organizing, not validated templates (§17.4)."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('c8910130-cf7e-5d9d-8689-fc85094520b9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L11.3 In-season maintenance","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('165d19c6-cac8-5fe2-8cfd-f34a20fa6324', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Retain quality exposure at reduced volume where recovery permits. The exact dose is Unknown, and G3 publishes none — the form is a defended reduction with a verification plan, inherited from G3 104 M9."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('8f25f672-8f82-501c-b300-f69187b533cb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L11.4 Youth: what is supported","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('1b871f44-36e8-51ae-905e-c5a6547950e1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Supervised, progressed resistance and plyometric training improves outcomes; low injury rates in properly prescribed settings; no age gate; no strength-ratio gate."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('54320aa1-3336-57e3-92bf-9444bb8a367d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L11.5 Youth: what is not licensed","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('3e60b768-1a80-5d05-9064-7344d091fd8c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Adult loading, volume, and testing prescriptions do not transfer to middle-school athletes because a youth study showed a method is safe. Both halves are stated together."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('f8a40628-241e-53f5-bde8-ab97fb034abe', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L11.6 Female athletes","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('bd3f923f-b2bd-548e-af49-b365bfdd86ce', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Same foundational principles; individual sport demands, maturation, history, and recovery matter; cycle evidence is not converted into deterministic prescriptions; underrepresentation is disclosed at the point of the claim (§18.2)."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('d7e832a8-b207-5571-99c5-64ae879970c8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L11.7 Writing the block","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('84707d98-f0b0-5a5d-ab61-3ea82b3e279f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Assembling M10''s integration and this module''s constraints into a real training period for a real athlete."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('3cbc908c-86d8-5085-ab5a-63ad5b0742fa', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-14 — Youth Can Train Power","body":"Properly supervised and progressively exposed youth can sprint, jump, land, throw, perform plyometrics, and use appropriate ballistic and resistance methods without arbitrary age or strength-ratio gates. — Teaching boundary: The claim is conditioned on supervision, technical coaching, and progression — stated every time (AS-105-14). And youth evidence does not validate adult loading, volume, or testing prescriptions for middle-school athletes (Condition 2). — Evidence class: A/B"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('ea2c8d17-2e3b-5d87-9bf8-de3df36076eb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-15 — Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management","body":"Coordinate power exposure with sprinting, jumping, strength, practice, competition, and recovery. No universal contact count, frequency, microcycle, or maintenance dose governs all athletes. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-8 and G3 104 D104-12. The athlete''s jumps in practice count. No frequency, contact count, or maintenance dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-15). — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('5f0a4053-4092-5b62-ae84-89c1a6c20ca1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-04 — Strength Supports Power but Does Not Gate It","body":"Strength matters, especially in weaker and less-trained athletes, but G3 does not require a universal strength phase or squat ratio before appropriate power training. — Teaching boundary: Rejecting the gate is not rejecting strength. Competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demand still govern what an athlete does (AS-105-06) — those are coaching judgments, not a ratio. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('7f4933a9-ab86-50c1-b9da-28e9c3f54cf5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Multiple routes to strength and power adaptation are supported, including concurrent and mixed-method approaches — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('97c10ab4-88f6-5ddd-a344-1e3adc139dc1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Mandatory strength-then-power sequencing — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('8da7b447-dddd-531e-9797-9f02113a16e5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class E — influential applied frameworks, not universally validated templates","body":"Phase potentiation, blocks, French contrast, triphasic and similar systems — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-H1 to EP105-H4; EP105-02; EP105-H1; EP105-H4"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('0a3f5900-6db3-5772-9e18-8b86b8e75f05', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D","body":"Maintaining important neuromuscular exposures in-season with reduced volume and retained intensity/quality — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('08a600c5-985d-5687-a625-c3195d82b51e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class U — Unknown","body":"Exact universal in-season maintenance and minimum-effective doses — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('3bc4462d-8de7-5353-92c2-7e414b7ab2fd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A/B","body":"Youth resistance and plyometric training can improve strength, jump, sprint, and other fitness outcomes when supervised and progressed — Basis: EP105-10, EP105-11, EP105-12, EP105-13; EP105-10; EP105-11; EP105-12; EP105-13"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('66604883-f908-5d40-84f6-6fa08fa8e7d1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Age-based prohibitions and arbitrary strength thresholds as universal gates — Basis: EP105-10, EP105-11, EP105-13; EP105-10; EP105-11; EP105-13"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('a082ac0e-0fb4-5ec7-bef9-9c9fdb8a5eff', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Low injury rates in properly prescribed youth resistance training and weightlifting under supervision — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('2209bcbb-f098-5a52-9244-6433917f1f52', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D","body":"Progression considering training age, coordination, maturity, exposure, and supervision — Basis: EP105-11, EP105-12; EP105-11; EP105-12"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('d7e19fbb-e665-5016-818b-3f2fa73ba1e3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not established — Condition 2","body":"Youth evidence validating adult loading, volume, or testing prescriptions for middle-school athletes — Basis: EP105-10, EP105-11; EP105-10; EP105-11"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('935dd619-00ae-5cee-983e-2b342d031394', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Female athletes improve strength, jump, sprint, and COD outcomes through resistance and plyometric training — Basis: EP105-20, EP105-22; EP105-20; EP105-22"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('e43e235b-4adf-5b18-bb36-5cc162105650', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not supported","body":"Fundamentally different power-training principles for female athletes — Basis: EP105-20, EP105-22; EP105-20; EP105-22"}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('3a55d221-68fc-5af1-8267-a91072d587b1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C / Unknown","body":"Broad cycle-based power prescriptions — Basis: Evidence Pack §35"}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('cc05e289-3d1a-58fa-8d8f-8d2de3ffa108', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-14 — Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure","body":"Supervise, progressively dose, technically coach, and scale to maturity and training age without unsupported prohibitions. — Enforcement: supervision ratio documented for every G3 youth power group; the safety claim is stated with its conditions."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('28920c7d-e4d4-55fd-bae7-f5cc788dc9ff', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-15 — Integrate the Weekly High-Output Load","body":"Account for total sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('2e719516-7c12-5542-8296-18278e3d301b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-06 — No Universal Strength Gate","body":"Use competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demands instead of arbitrary bodyweight ratios."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('d605fc0a-990c-50f4-8a81-12773fd07b7f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calendar."}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('432bc2dc-e58c-5665-9629-9153a9d4d59e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the organization (CJ-14); the in-season dose (CJ-15, CJ-16); individual modification for any athlete (CJ-21)."}')::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('a1c24237-aa17-53e1-80cd-b700c039b40c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Strength-then-power sequencing (CA-16) · a named system presented as validated · publishing an in-season maintenance dose · age gates or strength gates for youth (CA-09, CA-02) · transferring adult prescriptions to middle-school athletes on the strength of a youth safety finding · building phase-based programming from cycle evidence (CJ-21)."}')::jsonb, 'c1'),
  ('98e06b56-2282-55fd-9c1a-92b1b7c54045', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"many power-method studies are male-dominant; medicine-ball evidence includes young female handball samples but is sport-specific; youth evidence supports supervised progressive training without establishing adult prescriptions."}')::jsonb, 'c2'),
  ('4a9a3ea3-db2e-593b-8bcf-d5d5f4480140', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5898cfe3-9928-52b7-ac5b-417f0ff43b98', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"In G3 Youth S&C the module usually produces simpler exposures with real progression criteria and documented supervision; in-season it usually produces a smaller, protected, quality-first exposure with an explicit statement that maintenance — not development — is the objective."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('85bcc1d0-f0a7-575b-aec7-400fe9d6f1a7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5898cfe3-9928-52b7-ac5b-417f0ff43b98', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Middle-School Athlete New to Power Training. Ana, 12, joins a G3 Youth group of 18 with one coach, two 50-minute sessions weekly. No structured training history. Coordinated for her age, attentive, no injury history. She lands noisily and asymmetrically from a two-foot jump and has never been coached to land. Her father asks when she can start \"real explosive training,\" and mentions a coach who told him power work should wait until 16 and until she can squat 1.5× bodyweight.","steps":[{"id":"2d739dfc-f017-5eb1-8651-4835eb0574b5","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"e49ba7bb-81f6-5452-a761-babb81e2fadc","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"3020ca86-0b7b-527d-a599-3ee1a29fa7ed","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"e245a226-e703-5ffb-9c9f-17e94ef8708e","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What does Ana''s power exposure consist of, how does it progress, and what do you tell her father?\n\nFailure modes: Accepting either gate · substituting a different threshold · \"explosiveness\" language with a 12-year-old · adult contact counts or loading applied to youth · complexity beyond the supervision ratio · promising injury prevention · leaving landing uncoached because it look'
    || 's like nothing.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who introduces light external load once landing is competent passes with a stated criterion. A coach who stays unloaded for the block and says why also passes."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('393a14a7-9360-58a0-aa8e-f7273e3034f7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5898cfe3-9928-52b7-ac5b-417f0ff43b98', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-3 — Contextual Power Session Design. See §14.4. PS-3 gates M12."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('d61236e4-cd9c-56ab-9301-facbfce5adef', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9a6ce921-5123-52ee-b97b-0106eb589384', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('8dbe482f-d032-53a1-b7f0-9781625df843', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9a6ce921-5123-52ee-b97b-0106eb589384', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"88b78a25-d114-5216-8791-eb91aa64e881","title":"G3-105 · M11 Assessment — Periodization, Maintenance, Youth & Female Athletes"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('e8aa2780-d777-50c5-b8f7-ef417292e212', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"PS-3 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M11. Gates M12. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('ed028621-43a1-55eb-a697-c01be4504da1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('adbc51f3-aa13-5349-b00b-0523499eaa54', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"02a903a4-4ce4-57f8-82a7-184bef299cef","text":"Accounts for age, training age, maturation, and competence in what is set up and coached."},{"id":"5a19f931-077a-5c55-b449-6bd989164a18","text":"Integrates the athlete''s existing sport exposure and recovery into the session as delivered."},{"id":"a5a5885a-1ac4-512b-b035-fc0f867b2dab","text":"Chooses method, dose, and rest for the context rather than for the template."},{"id":"e0c66ab2-3070-5da7-a8dc-09afbb1e5529","text":"Manages fatigue visibly — adjusts when output or landing quality changes."},{"id":"0466fb66-88ea-5d42-a918-e3acb7812ba1","text":"Justifies the progression in terms an athlete and a sport coach could follow."},{"id":"5bfcf3f4-4a19-5ea3-83ca-8c5ddd642bb7","text":"Identifies which elements of the session are Coach Judgment."},{"id":"f084afc3-d4c0-5e13-8caf-d07ce62b01a0","text":"Interprets their own force-time or method-claim evidence (A8, A9) inside its construct, protocol, and error — and states what it does not establish."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('3be3f595-36ce-5e05-9504-e25ef9d5d45a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('fa5ecde9-1318-563f-a36c-f970e6aacf86', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"• force-time interpretation (A8)\n\n• method claim audit (A9)\n\n• weekly integration (A10)\n\n• contextual block (A11)\n\n• plyometric progression (A6). PS-3 covers M8–M11, per the course gating architecture (§6.3). A8 and A9 are the written evidence from M8 and M9; A6, A10, and A11 are the delivery evidence. All five are carried forward into PS-4, where they are read against the integrated plan rather than against a single session."}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('d68c0818-0abc-5c15-9a5d-344544dae9ce', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Assessor questions","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('eb96c49f-1821-5ad5-b230-5f303486eeb1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"72907a42-79ef-5fbf-bc45-df058108cb2f","text":"What did this athlete already do this week?"},{"id":"8600d4cd-78e7-5d65-93c8-1cbb5cc93001","text":"What told you to change the dose?"},{"id":"309ec2eb-8b44-5e06-aede-43ad0fff2ed4","text":"Why that progression now?"},{"id":"c04d670f-6bd7-5e04-8609-4a6c928ca557","text":"What did your force-time or method evidence actually establish, and what did it not?"},{"id":"19a02b70-cbd6-51c2-a60f-0a8f7e7145d4","text":"Which of these choices would another qualified coach reasonably make differently?"}]}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('d8404cf2-e3b5-50df-a66c-23bbc7bcc3d5', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('065aca52-cf0e-5baa-9c67-f8ed9348e186', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('4a521eaf-8af2-5b72-9905-44be4ca3f48f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8df19aac-e6e7-58c4-b236-44787b266fab', 'quote', 1, ('{"text":"The dashboard will offer you a diagnosis. The last thing this course does is teach you to decline it."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('1886b9a0-133a-5519-9161-f9e0fd1a6a51', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8df19aac-e6e7-58c4-b236-44787b266fab', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"To select and interpret power tests, govern technology use, refuse readiness and asymmetry thresholds, and run the full G3 decision model on a power case."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('1b97dfc1-e1a6-5486-87bb-188788ef9908', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8df19aac-e6e7-58c4-b236-44787b266fab', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"2cf7137f-ac45-552f-958f-786604bd5bbd","text":"M12.1 Select a test matching the construct being evaluated."},{"id":"8877a3fe-bb7f-5482-a6d3-549d20ebb7fe","text":"M12.2 State what CMJ, squat jump, drop jump/RSI, broad jump, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, and VBT velocity each measure."},{"id":"647f4666-ee03-55eb-bd79-111b4be0eb86","text":"M12.3 Apply standardization: protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation, baseline."},{"id":"a73a9042-d2e1-5678-bc86-d52934b9e343","text":"M12.4 State the reliability limits of derived force-plate metrics."},{"id":"11a38be1-a983-52e5-abc3-af70226647c4","text":"M12.5 Treat power equations as estimates."},{"id":"dfd10232-c38a-59c1-85a1-809eed3d8e76","text":"M12.6 Interpret change against baseline, typical error, and context."},{"id":"7483aa9f-3a5d-5a3d-ac76-7600b65a3080","text":"M12.7 Refuse universal readiness and asymmetry thresholds without substituting new ones."},{"id":"6a0c5eeb-dd7a-5aa9-b759-0da17959cb82","text":"M12.8 Run the G3 decision model on a power case and defend it."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('86ff1b60-9ab8-5046-b5ba-deafd60db4b3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8df19aac-e6e7-58c4-b236-44787b266fab', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires PS-3 · Sign-off: PS-4, then T-01"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('e74ac40f-c1c6-5193-9c9f-d61ec231bad7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.1 Construct first","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('b831919b-c002-5552-a983-b50e96597698', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Decide the construct, then the test. Reversing that order is how a jump becomes a readiness score."}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('57009996-51ef-5d8c-b060-fdfd242870d9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.2 What each test measures","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('34a31c56-bd44-529f-84bb-2c7b0fa36d18', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The seven-row table in §15.2, taught as the operating reference."}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('fd38df8d-8dd9-598e-928a-67ffca163588', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.3 Standardization","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('196d7989-148c-5b65-bd47-98161967c676', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline — recorded at time of testing (AS-105-09, inherited G3 102 AS-06)."}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('57ff6887-caa4-5d92-a279-c630deac7f8c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.4 Reliability of the derived","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('31b46a70-1458-5f81-93ba-b17440d965d4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Basic outcomes over dashboard breadth (D105-11), with the pre-specified metric set from M8."}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('13967151-1fd2-55cb-b1e8-373d98aed3b1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.5 Estimates are estimates","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('15247da7-d2f8-51be-8e01-c8a7fd62b228', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Power equations, flight-time methods, contact mats, cameras, and plates each carry assumptions; cross-system comparison is not interpretation."}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('d4e36593-5af5-5b47-8927-cf1078c85927', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.6 Interpreting change","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('f41fa9d1-f1e0-5036-b67c-e3308d3af48e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"Baseline, typical error, context, and other athlete information — never one number (AS-105-12). This is Case 7."}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('80a904c9-1b25-5726-baf8-cdf1e20bd3f8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.7 Refusing the thresholds","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('d2b21f9a-a0e7-50d3-91d5-578641fd81e3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"No readiness threshold and no asymmetry cutoff, and no replacement number (AS-105-13). This is Case 9."}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('4c0f95f6-f300-5047-b048-8c3b3bd09339', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"L12.8 The integrated case","level":3}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('65918cd2-5de5-51c5-9762-86aea012f721', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The full G3 102 model run on a power decision, producing the one-page plan the coach defends."}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('dbb4a800-faf9-59c4-8508-ce85504e2377', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-11 — Simple Reliable Metrics Outrank Metric Volume","body":"Prioritize standardized, reliable, decision-relevant measures over dashboard complexity. — Teaching boundary: This is not an argument against force plates. It is an argument for a small, pre-specified, reliable metric set chosen before the data is collected. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('34daa1f9-f7ab-5aac-af89-df3496a33eb1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-12 — Reactive Metrics Are Task Measures","body":"RSI, RSI-modified, jump height, contact time, and force-plate variables do not independently define readiness, stiffness, injury risk, or explosiveness. — Teaching boundary: These metrics are worth collecting when standardized and decision-relevant. They are not worth a label. — Evidence class: A that RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio; F for the claim that RSI alone completely measures reactive ability; F/C for the claim that low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit; B/C for RSI and RSI-modified contributing to a broader standardized monitoring battery"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('8a77adf7-0e5f-5f59-a0be-f6d987a7de70', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('84d758ea-6e5f-5703-b71a-fcf698234e58', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-10 — Technology Must Serve a Decision","body":"Force plates, VBT, jump apps, and profiling systems are useful only when sufficiently reliable, interpretable, and actionable. — Teaching boundary: Low-tech environments must remain capable of G3-quality coaching. The course is written so that a coach with a tape measure and a stopwatch loses no conceptual ground. — Evidence class: B"}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('9eb3475b-47f8-5186-a504-a031437f52cc', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"CMJ height is a standardized vertical-jump outcome, not a complete power, RFD, or readiness diagnosis — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('7a61bfa7-c92b-5f05-a795-bd5c3ac88e5b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Drop jump / RSI is a task-specific flight-to-contact ratio, not complete reactive ability, stiffness, or injury risk — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('c998af65-8480-554a-aaf1-70b989d43adb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Broad jump is a horizontal jump outcome, not a sprint-acceleration mechanism diagnosis — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('2029e6bd-dbb4-5270-898f-11bd91640a32', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Loaded-jump results do not create universal F-V diagnoses — Basis: EP105-05, EP105-07; EP105-05; EP105-07"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('1c78a7ca-a40d-5819-9217-28485e8dc094', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Medicine-ball throws are task-specific projection outcomes — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('9d4aed41-1eea-557b-9999-6b803a490e38', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"VBT velocity is exercise-, load-, and device-specific — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('cc2b4a18-5402-5e34-8a72-fc074f12cbec', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Force plates can measure force-time data and derive outcomes; reliability is metric-specific, with basic outcomes generally more reliable than several derived variables — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('85d86dd5-d87d-51d4-a573-e43bb1cba1b9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A/B for standardized jump height as an outcome; C/F for unqualified estimated-power claims","body":"Peak-power equations are estimates that may not match device-derived values or retain validity across body sizes, protocols, and populations — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-24; EP105-08; EP105-24"}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('397331b1-1600-5ffe-a55d-0fa2ff72b38b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Smartphone-based CMJ tools can show acceptable validity for selected metrics in specific implementations; validation is not automatically transferable — Basis: EP105-24; EP105-24"}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('69926cb3-8be1-5626-a514-1f8c14a0a750', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C","body":"CMJ and related measures are useful within-person monitoring tools with standardized protocol, baseline, typical error, and decision context — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('2deea91d-5e01-59c2-a159-624af0b85dd4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Automated readiness determination from CMJ height, RSI-modified, or any single force-plate metric — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('b39f4b91-fb52-5714-bdbb-c31b40617bf2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F for universal cutoff interpretation; B/C for monitoring repeated task-specific asymmetry when rel…","body":"No universal 10% or 15% asymmetry cutoff is established as automatically performance-limiting, injury-predictive, or corrective — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('f8668cd7-4b7f-5ca8-a007-79dbc9156ccd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('5ef8ca39-d55b-54ae-be58-285b46e93a44', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('d2bcdc6d-87c5-5c3c-a979-75bd1ce72d15', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('9c154313-2aff-5236-ad6b-2343ada7e306', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-12 — Interpret Change Against Error and Baseline","body":"Readiness and performance changes require repeated baselines, reliability and error context, and other athlete information."}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('30ddfdf6-6afa-5c09-b26d-03872c9c1624', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-13 — No Universal Asymmetry Threshold","body":"A fixed 10% or 15% value does not automatically trigger intervention."}')::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('120789ef-046e-5eca-9fb5-e5b5dbdffed4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"which tests are feasible and decision-relevant in a given facility (CJ-19); how to interpret a measured asymmetry (CJ-20) or a readiness signal (CJ-18)."}')::jsonb, 'c1'),
  ('8aa5e27a-a757-5650-a052-7683b46b6f65', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"CMJ height read as readiness (CA-22) · RSI read as reactive ability (CA-20) · force plates read as explanations (CA-19) · asymmetry read as a problem (CA-21) · estimated power compared across systems · a metric set chosen after the data arrived · running the decision model without naming the output requirement."}')::jsonb, 'c2'),
  ('5e13bd74-b783-573d-a5f8-1a12bbfc41cd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"CMJ metric and reliability work is device- and protocol-dependent, largely adult; app validation is implementation-specific."}')::jsonb, 'c3'),
  ('ab89c097-451a-55e8-be6c-50f846179499', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2b46333b-aa0f-5d2d-a0aa-61e00085d519', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"The artifact is a G3 power testing report another coach can read: what was measured, in what task, under what protocol, against what baseline, what it does and does not establish, and what decision it changed."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('0aba6c09-f69f-55e5-b3ab-9773caaffe80', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2b46333b-aa0f-5d2d-a0aa-61e00085d519', 'scenario', 1, (('{"intro":"Athlete Labeled \"Velocity Deficient\" From One F-V Profile. Sofia, 19, collegiate athlete. A single vertical-jump force-velocity profile, collected once on a portable system by a visiting practitioner using a method the G3 staff cannot fully reconstruct, returns a label: \"velocity deficient, prioritize velocity-oriented training.\" Her sprint and jump performance are unremarkable but not poor. No sprint testing, no technical observation, and no training history were included. The sport coach has seen the label and is asking about a velocity block.","steps":[{"id":"84f8f7ad-da0f-5e59-b918-a2c8b72e2efe","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"ecc33876-5157-5bd1-a235-b6bc85c1715c","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"58a854fc-64a8-5728-958b-a5c1fd8116df","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"f529d3ea-7284-5fd7-999d-18fdda868912","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What does this profile license?\n\nFailure modes: Accepting the label · rejecting profiling entirely in reaction · comparing this profile to one collected by another method · recording \"velocity deficient\" as an athlete attribute · using \"optimal profile\" as the target (CJ-03) · letting the sport coach''s expec'
    || 'tation set the program.\n\nDefensible variation: Discarding the profile and evaluating from scratch passes. Retaining it as one input, corroborated, passes. Building the velocity block fails."}'))::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('ce14be95-f00a-512f-854a-2ad398819c41', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2b46333b-aa0f-5d2d-a0aa-61e00085d519', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-4 — Integrated Power Microcycle (§14.5), then T-01 — Power Development Programming Defense (§14.6)."}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('30bae400-9b95-5f08-bb13-78efa82da1fa', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '31e64387-f51d-5eac-a2f8-3f23562bc340', 'rich_text', 1, ('{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('0d982d38-59cb-5247-b576-52237c0a4a16', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '31e64387-f51d-5eac-a2f8-3f23562bc340', 'assessment_reference', 1, ('{"assessmentId":"18adb854-ba77-5255-969b-14c2f3405bf7","title":"G3-105 · M12 Assessment — Testing, Force Plates, Readiness & Integrated G3 Decision-Making"}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('38149255-6cbc-50b0-a7e5-fa7efea6d4ca', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"PS-4 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M12. Requires the four-week delivery window complete. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('a606f392-7197-50b0-ab60-8990c8cc420a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('b724a1d5-95c2-5889-a987-85a079bd151c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"9ae1b204-2f07-5702-b74d-773c32cb1abf","text":"Analyzes sport demands and states what the week already contains."},{"id":"e4179cea-336a-53c1-a2ef-ade8496b6b43","text":"Integrates strength, sprint/COD, power, and recovery rather than stacking them."},{"id":"702a36ab-9d19-5f26-9cba-c746f3fe9c72","text":"Avoids redundant high-intensity exposure, and can point to what was removed."},{"id":"563573ed-9a3a-5c83-8ef3-9145ae02e663","text":"Defines the monitoring — what will be measured, under what protocol, against what baseline."},{"id":"c67f3905-f71d-5fb5-be7c-1524e44d2c5c","text":"Defines the adjustment logic in advance."},{"id":"c922950a-cc5b-5adb-809b-d599e5cfa8ad","text":"Identifies which elements are Coach Judgment."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('a98e861d-00d8-55ef-87e2-d34e5cb4780e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('4219c3a2-37bf-5905-a3f8-48c4414c4b5c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"15478d05-1b8e-5573-99ed-0e8b508509ff","text":"force-time interpretation (A8)"},{"id":"c0fc9907-3c79-58f0-93df-c2ce5cd54aff","text":"method claim audit (A9)"},{"id":"fa7d85c2-604b-5069-89fc-acc2154c42bc","text":"weekly integration (A10)"},{"id":"bf3bec02-ed6c-52ac-a038-5920bc88d41f","text":"integrated power plan (A12)"},{"id":"201c5bf5-4870-5b37-a98d-d490fafc7b17","text":"the plan as actually delivered over the window."}]}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('ea2dbf43-4a9d-5971-8e09-c94ec69f6bf0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Assessor questions","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('dfd6f454-19a1-5634-93c8-53bbdd1e2779', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"fe74a2d8-0a78-5da8-8e16-1cadd02ef49a","text":"What is the high-output load this week, counting everything?"},{"id":"ce4b804b-66ec-5947-b8a2-dc7915ead7b2","text":"What did you take out?"},{"id":"aea60184-62ed-5765-a488-111d6055f482","text":"What will you measure, and what decision would it change?"},{"id":"9eae8545-a8a3-5c4f-bf90-ad00faab64ab","text":"What is your adjustment trigger?"}]}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('11da5227-3319-5cd7-8848-26d66c227b4f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"}')::jsonb, 'a7'),
  ('e92de5a0-23fd-546b-8b9a-4df0dbccfa83', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window. PS-4 cannot be scheduled until the delivery window is complete. Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."}')::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('3d074f05-5b75-5517-a012-012084ec4a46', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"T-01 — Terminal defense","body":"20–30 minutes with an approved assessor, using the coach''s own athlete or group and their own artifacts. The assessor works the ten obligations (§1.4) in order and probes at least four. — Required for: course competency completion — The defense is evaluated live by an approved G3 evaluator. Failure on T-01 cannot be offset by module performance."}')::jsonb, 'a0'),
  ('7c4cb882-79dd-5048-8342-869e318c04c6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Must defend","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a1'),
  ('a949c2c2-c5e8-5cea-b170-1966d7549aa2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"4706e2ea-60f7-5e17-9ffb-3f230be25e57","text":"terminology"},{"id":"0a8bf85a-465c-5c98-913a-0805eb437693","text":"demand"},{"id":"1b39090e-f9b4-55b6-9b28-c3f4d8252916","text":"hypothesis"},{"id":"cc4e159e-aba0-5343-ba36-d0f9d302ad49","text":"method"},{"id":"39757025-bcb3-531c-a1f0-6578fd685c86","text":"loading, dose, and rest"},{"id":"6f277d7c-e56e-5cd9-9771-7020dd99c104","text":"quality criteria"},{"id":"953d0ab7-15e7-5a06-a511-549ce60769e6","text":"progression"},{"id":"8b89dc53-e704-5a2d-9f9e-bd2743f29738","text":"weekly integration"},{"id":"c41e0de3-5912-5126-b8c9-4e73a33abd73","text":"testing"},{"id":"b5515a72-0a7f-5492-a164-f747cf049af5","text":"technology"},{"id":"f5e79e9b-c212-506a-ab1a-482e46bac3ba","text":"evidence classification"},{"id":"0e8451ec-5a5f-5e3a-9c49-0055eed057ce","text":"transfer assumptions"},{"id":"95b3504a-542a-5b95-a060-0ff4bf818dd7","text":"and the uncertainty that remains."}]}')::jsonb, 'a2'),
  ('90eaf2f2-c45b-5a4b-b34b-9376eb702328', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'heading', 1, ('{"text":"Mandatory probes","level":3}')::jsonb, 'a3'),
  ('75334d70-8be1-5926-9558-2e1e7d11f32f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'checklist', 1, ('{"items":[{"id":"98cf1416-bcea-57b7-b808-0c4c019d0780","text":"What output requirement does this plan address, in what task, and how will you know it changed?"},{"id":"da14d191-50af-5382-80fd-a767bb17a86c","text":"State your limitation hypothesis, your confidence in it, and what would refute it."},{"id":"7455903d-ce4f-5f1b-b00f-ff1a7971d961","text":"Why this method rather than the alternatives — and what would you do without this equipment?"},{"id":"a659754c-ba2b-5150-ba37-e5d037902df9","text":"Which part of what you just told me is evidence, which is G3 standard, and which is your judgment?"},{"id":"4365e4aa-41bc-5882-b26d-97f4f185bcda","text":"Which of your evidence is acute and which is chronic?"},{"id":"8a363197-b4b5-52ff-853e-f8b75087ccc3","text":"What would make you change this plan? Be specific."},{"id":"829bd1d2-0805-5a6d-8f4b-143dc3d3b5c7","text":"Tell me what you would say to this athlete''s parent about injury risk."}]}')::jsonb, 'a4'),
  ('626d9576-ab62-582a-bcd7-d2197b653420', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"danger","title":"Non-compensable failures","body":"cannot answer probe 4 (evidence vs G3 standard vs judgment) · cannot answer probe 5 (acute vs chronic evidence) · makes an injury-prevention guarantee on probe 7 · cites a threshold, percentage, contact count, or required method as justification"}')::jsonb, 'a5'),
  ('f0319e23-fea1-580f-b391-256212dec478', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"info","title":"Defense rubric","body":"§12.3 defense rubric"}')::jsonb, 'a6'),
  ('36bd23ba-0a0a-5e07-a013-ec61c3f1a3dd', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', 'callout', 2, ('{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."}')::jsonb, 'a7')
on conflict (id) do nothing;

    || '9b51-6ca9-5909-9824-65ea538500dc","text":"M1.5 Explain why \"explosiveness\" is not a stable scientific construct."},{"id":"65b9d768-1386-5126-9f40-adc42b729f9a","text":"M1.6 Replace an \"explosiveness\" statement with the actual quality and task."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"3e024672-eae4-5f7b-bdd5-d572c6da49f7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"entry module"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"951bd250-a095-5ce5-9df0-6146f013291f","type":"lesson_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"courseId":"b17cac49-2d2b-59c4-bc80-5de456998e75","lessonId":"460effc6-93c3-5f5f-8336-fdae680089d1","label":"Recall — the G3 reasoning system (G3 101, Module 12)","note":"Evidence classes, certainty language, and the three-layer discipline are governed by G3 101."},"position":"a4"},{"id":"e8883402-c706-5ae9-8846-f97d63953f88","type":"lesson_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"courseId":"4832fade-c765-5fcd-b784-d1a4103394fd","lessonId":"81e0bab6-4baa-5a42-93cb-7c47b740ac4f","label":"Recall — the G3 programming decision model (G3 102, Module 1)","note":"DEMAND → PROFILE → GAP → PRIORITY → PRESCRIPTION → RESPONSE → ADJUST is governed by G3 102 and is never '
    || 'modified downstream."},"position":"a5"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('de531192-1b87-5379-87cf-5d684a6984d4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '33bfcdb2-a39d-507e-b8f6-474f91cb4573', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 36, (('[{"id":"5319d3ee-07fc-5ca1-9cc1-d661f5451b02","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L1.1 The word that means nothing","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"72c2aadd-d0f4-5e79-843d-05e580ba4e8b","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Four coaches use \"explosive\" for four different qualities in one session. The cost is that none of them can select a method or a test."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"e6755a8b-155d-55ea-bfbe-aab48d1364af","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L1.2 Power, properly defined","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"7388e240-b447-5f58-9505-23c191972a38","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Rate of work; force × velocity in aligned conditions; and why the definition already contains the task-specificity of D105-01."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"7ffdc5e7-df86-54ff-a7f1-d8a265a1c8b4","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L1.3 The neighbours","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"286995f4-9b79-55e9-b3f4-287b26047a47","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Force, strength, work, impulse, velocity, acceleration — each defined against power and against each other."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"035fbfa5-eb5'
    || '0-5f05-8c8b-86adb6bd89b2","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L1.4 Peak, mean, and the protocol","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"66a36866-8951-5faf-b79e-afbb94798a1d","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Why a power number without its interval, model, and device is not a result."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"ac690f0a-d76a-5a39-a042-1968cd7dce77","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L1.5 Relative and absolute","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"b71a64bb-4b30-5beb-962a-699bfb1980e8","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"When normalizing helps, when it distorts, and why neither is universally superior."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"7f6ba796-231c-5302-9654-bf8f0050eb2f","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L1.6 RFD, introduced honestly","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"b6b986c5-fbf0-544e-911c-2c9d7bc0e15e","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Conceptually important, measurement-sensitive, and not a synonym for power. Full treatment in M8."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"cf30b094-3d72-5edd-9e4d-51cf756f8379","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L1.7 Rewriting the claim","level":3},"position":"'
    || 'b2"},{"id":"1b8dc31b-598d-51cd-9b46-4ae0df9b053c","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Coaches convert real statements — \"she needs more explosiveness,\" \"he''s a power athlete\" — into task-and-quality language, or flag them as unanswerable."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('8a876057-6af5-583e-9756-8a2e70d2c521', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'f681fb59-04ba-595c-85e8-d2fd0df0801f', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 23, (('[{"id":"2b607d27-895f-5d32-a8d7-6ac9872e37f2","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"c986952a-0c97-5a8e-9f8e-6040b085d7ec","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-02 — Related Qualities Are Not Interchangeable","body":"Strength, force, impulse, velocity, power, and RFD interact but are distinct. — Teaching boundary: Distinct does not mean unrelated — these qualities interact continuously in every athletic task. The doctrine forbids substitution, not integration. \"Explosiveness\" is Class F as a precise diagnostic term and is replaced by the actual quality and task. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"2d4bf4d9-2e0a-5ef3-9feb-ebca82860c29","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":'
    || '"Evidence — Class A / Known","body":"Force, strength, work, mechanical power, impulse, velocity, and acceleration are distinct constructs — Basis: EP105-01, EP105-02; EP105-01; EP105-02"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"8d36a66a-60d2-58ce-8cb3-8dbc90744bcd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A / Known","body":"Mechanical power is rate of work; force × velocity applies when vectors are aligned — Basis: EP105-01, EP105-02; EP105-01; EP105-02"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"d47a8c05-f9c8-52e8-aa9a-3f593dfbc38c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B / Probable","body":"Peak power is the highest instantaneous power in a defined interval and model — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-08; EP105-02; EP105-08"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"05c42316-8a48-59bf-849a-dd20f4743cde","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B / Probable","body":"Mean power requires its interval and calculation to be specified — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"a6049190-32dd-5481-a871-46dcfdfb366c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A for defini'
    || 'tion; B/C for routine field interpretation","body":"RFD is distinct from power — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"c506c305-14bf-5452-ab3f-e129742fef47","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B / Context dependent","body":"Relative power is context dependent, not universally superior — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"e4dda8cc-7548-50f9-9e51-24244e27ff10","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Explosiveness\" as a precise diagnostic term — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-03; EP105-02; EP105-03"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"8a45f1dc-28ee-50c3-9d31-bb88bd989ba7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"c2559fc0-4bb3-5656-b423-307bf760794a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label C'
    || 'MJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."},"position":"b0"},{"id":"c084e7f0-0799-5228-90a8-0096b139a69b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"none. Definitions are not negotiable inside G3."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"69f03088-101a-55ed-a5a9-db2ce2af47b1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Using \"explosiveness\" as a diagnosis (D105-02; Class F as a precise term) · reporting a power value with no task or calculation · using power and RFD interchangeably · assuming relative power is always the better expression · treating a peak-power figure as an athlete attribute."},"position":"b2"},{"id":"f9b287db-213c-54f2-8d80-207cc24ccacf","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"definitional evidence is mechanical and not population-limited; the field-interpretation caveat on RFD carries adult laboratory origins."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('12b7230a-285c-5a6a-92a8-88a079bcf8a9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c05661d6-6188-5ba6-9fd7-96c339badce5', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 14, ('[{"id":"8055b429-eac1-5522-a5a5-3211b88fa063","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Applied immediately to G3 session plans and testing language. A block labeled \"power\" is incomplete; \"vertical projection output in a loaded jump for an athlete whose demand is repeated jump performance\" can be evaluated by someone else."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"4dacc37e-eda4-545e-842f-337f916a4e45","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Scenario M1-A — Rewrite the claim. Eight real coaching statements rewritten into G3 form with task, quality, and measurement named — or flagged as unanswerable without more information.","steps":[{"id":"39048161-f1c9-5a2e-80d6-4a7237946b30","situation":"Work the case with your assessor: name the decision, the evidence class behind it, and what would change your answer."}]},"position":"a1"},{"id":"48013c1e-1f74-57f4-908e-cf6837d6af98","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"None. Terminology accuracy carries into PS-1 and is scored in T-01."},"position":"a2"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('4b41e060-486c-51f1-996b-8bb15675a210', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '90dd064f-dee2-5b1b-b0dc-684135aa808a', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 8, ('[{"id":"cd94155b-9766-5b66-98da-23015a80569d","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"8b361a99-3a5e-5961-9261-2e6ab0670799","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"379465b5-7587-53d7-956d-00e5a283bcab","title":"G3-105 · M1 Assessment — What Power Actually Is"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('d0c9e67f-c358-5155-bcbf-cda59d7646a8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '37f4fa18-78ed-5804-b932-525d309a4b52', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"26a44607-43f1-5a46-b404-bb364e086838","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Jump height can improve for at least five different reasons. Until you know which one, you do not know what you trained."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"90cf79b6-36c1-5d79-b7ca-b292ae7dbc30","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To ground power reasoning in impulse, force-time, and body mass, and to install the association-versus-transfer discipline before any method is discussed."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"0cf15f1d-15f4-5395-a648-f86bfe714a01","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"3978a280-63cd-5c1b-8a31-9364e1194530","text":"M2.1 Explain work/time and force × velocity as descriptions, not prescriptions."},{"id":"611396ec-993e-5fd1-97f5-4cfffc2b1f62","text":"M2.2 Explain impulse–momentum and why net impulse relative to body mass governs takeoff velocity."},{"id":"2be5e6a6-ee5e-59e9-a055-692e5aaed1f5","text":"M2.3 Name the multiple pathways by which a jump outcome can change."},{"id":"a09c3d8e-e4fc-52e5-aee6-077e5aa2ab3a","text":"M2.4 Explain force-time constraints — why time available changes which quality matters."},{"id":"fc1a02b3-ef84-5a2f-9472-716b4d7d2'
    || '9ec","text":"M2.5 Distinguish association from transfer."},{"id":"3c35510f-cd5c-55c8-8cbc-b71c5736450a","text":"M2.6 Explain why directional similarity does not guarantee transfer."},{"id":"b8eabb65-8b4a-533c-8130-1220813d286c","text":"M2.7 State a transfer pathway for any method proposed."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"e1515b43-6d66-59d0-86f5-951e48ff7cd6","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M1"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('0366de73-c413-5255-82b0-a6b2612deaad', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '39ec8274-1d2d-5287-bcd0-78bf3164659e', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"3220dd87-35d1-5981-9c0b-67adddb9b804","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L2.1 Work, time, force, velocity","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"04ac93fc-934d-5037-82fb-bd82976fab01","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The mechanics, stated so they describe rather than prescribe."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"e78f9c3b-ee5f-56ed-ba1e-a01eab4b2739","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L2.2 Impulse and momentum","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"f70cc175-b5bf-5a12-8143-05126a6fe9e6","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The force-time integral, and why it is the honest way to think about jumping and acceleration."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"d0b04ee6-21f8-5de1-8298-9f1f8d95b13c","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L2.3 Five ways a jump improves","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"2a9fe49b-730f-521b-9a12-7d79d0bffcf0","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Force magnitude · force-application time · timing and coordination · movement strategy · body mass. This lesson is the mechanical basis for the entire limitation-hypothesis method in M3."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"100ea49d-ac6a-57ae-84d1-fdb8f0aa'
    || '07be","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L2.4 Time available changes the question","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"2c40e6e4-bb6c-5e67-9e06-1501ffe00e94","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"When time is constrained, peak force achieved too late does not help — which is why impulse and RFD enter the conversation, and why neither is universally primary."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"880d9ce1-b712-55d1-bc85-d2c2dab9fc40","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L2.5 Association is not transfer","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"97c13d7a-3966-5cb5-b097-d609648e259f","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Test outcomes correlate with sport tasks. That identifies neither the limiting quality nor the method."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"b4c0db1e-eabc-5f8e-858e-1cbd1c141d8a","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L2.6 Direction, carefully","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"39803e32-1e7a-5ab8-8a5b-d568cb6f77c4","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Horizontal and vertical tasks can both contribute to sprint-related outcomes; visual direction alone establishes nothing (D105-08)."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"e51b33c'
    || '6-5fcc-5768-a93a-ee89a126a631","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L2.7 Stating the transfer pathway","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"d23c7e0f-1457-532c-bd27-ee970363ada3","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"For any method: what adaptation, expressed in which task, supported by what exposure, and what would show it did not occur."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('c2d64e0f-ef07-5119-be22-a03a19bf724e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '86c27c82-e437-5372-b473-029307330ebb', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"fab8955c-fd6d-5b8c-80db-9815dca2dd34","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"c38bb41d-6232-567b-9448-9c3574fadac9","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-07 — Transfer Must Be Demonstrated, Not Assumed","body":"Improvement in a jump, lift, throw, profile, or laboratory metric does not automatically establish sport transfer. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-7 and G3 104 D104-2. This is not a claim that transfer is rare — it is a requirement that the transfer pathway be stated and, where possible, observed. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"6df47427-a80f-5cc8-bf57-6c60fb0a30d0","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-08 — Directional Si'
    || 'milarity Is Not Sufficient Specificity","body":"Horizontal, vertical, unilateral, bilateral, or visually sport-like exercises do not guarantee direction- or sport-specific transfer. — Teaching boundary: Direction may matter. What is unsupported is the inference from visual direction to guaranteed transfer. Selecting unilateral or bilateral work by task, competence, tolerance, and desired loading is Class D — legitimate coaching inference, labeled as such (CJ-12). — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"2d87e86c-3a67-5627-9502-443582d30909","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Force and velocity jointly determine external mechanical power — Basis: EP105-01; EP105-01"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"4fb72256-6cc6-595f-a1f0-8056a11fb22e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Impulse is the force-time integral; net vertical impulse relative to body mass determines takeoff velocity — Basis: EP105-01, EP105-08; EP105-01; EP105-08"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"cc4a97f5-fcda-59d2-a310-3298a7dc8540","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A",'
    || '"body":"Jump height can change through force magnitude, force-application time, timing/coordination, movement strategy, or body mass — Basis: EP105-01, EP105-08; EP105-01; EP105-08"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"caef7796-ad9f-5955-8ae7-bfb579984854","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A/B — mechanical principle; task application is reasonable extrapolation","body":"Impulse is often more task-informative than peak force alone when time is constrained — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"76bd5f17-4b2b-5f04-8920-1313d3ea2567","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Power-related test outcomes are associated with sprinting, jumping, and COD — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-07; EP105-02; EP105-07"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"54cad49a-6aac-5163-9f05-8e3780b3d1ae","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Association does not identify the causal quality to train — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-07; EP105-02; EP105-07"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"adbe4dec-63f6-58f9-8a60-05aec3c58e53","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"'
    || 'Evidence — Class B — spans different sports and interventions","body":"Resistance training can improve sport-specific performance in elite athletes — Basis: Evidence Pack §5; supporting record not itemized (§20.3b)"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"111d3e5c-cf2c-5150-bd30-2211d50c4523","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Directional similarity guarantees transfer — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-07; EP105-02; EP105-07"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"b69efb46-0ecb-54f8-ae4b-08201c76f35d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Vertical jump alone predicts or develops maximal velocity — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"b7d69106-f5f4-55aa-a7d0-44e406a559ce","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."},"position":"b2"},{"id":"36ac8a45-e713-5abc-8d85-b20434ed0a42","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 App'
    || 'lied Standard AS-105-02 — Build Limitation Hypotheses From Multiple Sources","body":"Use task performance, strength/force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context before labeling an athlete."},"position":"b3"},{"id":"75ff72f4-9e45-5808-b09d-626bd10fd91d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"which pathway to pursue for a given athlete — that is M3''s hypothesis work."},"position":"b4"},{"id":"d2472e8a-4528-52ff-844a-6a63e11856f4","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Treating jump height as a power diagnosis · reading a correlation as a prescription · vector-matching claims (CA-14) · assuming vertical work develops maximal velocity · ignoring body mass in tasks the athlete must move · reporting exercise improvement as sport transfer (§1.2)."},"position":"b5"},{"id":"7d103459-330a-5d81-a4e2-74f83e9195eb","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"transfer evidence spans athlete populations with substantial heterogeneity; elite-sample findings are not genera'
    || 'lized to developing athletes."},"position":"b6"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('08395677-cd9c-5687-9150-ff5c46ffcf75', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '88e4e95a-2751-5612-b3ce-b3b6b7e32fd7', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"1606b3c5-c994-5f20-b877-ebd267275edd","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The practical use is diagnostic restraint. When an athlete''s jump improves, the competent coach can name which pathways plausibly moved and which were not addressed — before deciding what the improvement means."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"c309c9b0-6fd7-5e33-8fce-c60d816f14c3","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Strong High-School Athlete With Poor Acceleration. Marcus, 17, three years of well-coached strength training. Trap-bar deadlift and squat among the strongest in the program absolutely and relative to body mass. CMJ above team average. First-step and 10 m acceleration among the poorest in the squad, consistently, across two testing occasions on the same surface with the same timing method. Video shows a tall first step, late shin angle, and a hesitant initiation under reactive conditions. Sport practice is dense; there has been no dedicated sprint-technical exposure.","steps":[{"id":"491aa3ab-2a22-5ec8-9918-9789b8c5746c","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"195b0f4a-0f76-50af-a69d-573b9170e1fc","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"a66765c7-9b98-5b98-bb2e-c163c9284ad6","situa'
    || 'tion":"Defense"},{"id":"ae400b12-dbf3-5108-abbf-f46620d87ba9","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is the limitation hypothesis, and what does the block contain?\n\nFailure modes: \"He needs more power\" as the plan · a jump metric used to diagnose acceleration · an F-V label applied without a profile, or with one · vector-matching justification · ignoring the absence of sprint exposure · a hypothesis with no falsifier.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who prioritizes reactive and initiation work, or one who prioritizes sprint-technical exposure, both pass with the hypothesis and falsifier stated. Adding a strength block fails absent a force-capacity argument the data does not support."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"f68cbe3b-590a-5c1f-b877-32e13ecbbc8a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-1."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('1181538b-d687-5e02-88b2-9bf7a4e92a11', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6c957173-5ff0-56a6-8458-ba3834e8de83', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"b291b868-3a15-5760-8066-add2bdaa7d00","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"11300a9d-a78f-5c22-a4fe-cc3c06ac3c73","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"d32a1c8d-354c-5a0a-af14-db0d589e9795","title":"G3-105 · M2 Assessment — Mechanical Foundations & Athletic Transfer"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('28c6f006-4b30-5ce2-847c-7e3a3b3962ec', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4ccb9340-6e34-53c3-acae-ffbc5c0be001', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"196f7894-dfc2-5339-9ced-89b04f5344ba","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"\"Force-limited\" is a hypothesis, not a diagnosis, and not an identity. The skill is holding it loosely enough to be wrong."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"74cdff48-b519-58b1-81f5-5005cf179523","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To establish what strength contributes to power and where that contribution ends, and to teach the multi-source, provisional limitation hypothesis that drives every method decision in this course."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"135a72bf-8e58-555d-8741-19198c8ddabb","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"a6f56cbd-f8f1-5ca0-8242-bf42fa1c304d","text":"M3.1 State what maximal strength contributes to power output."},{"id":"efe0a843-aaca-5022-bd29-9cfe07ec4433","text":"M3.2 Explain why the contribution is generally larger in weaker or less-trained athletes."},{"id":"e9f09adf-205a-53dd-9073-b0221a5bfa31","text":"M3.3 Explain diminishing returns and task-specific limits."},{"id":"afd6d449-9b5f-5f67-bf1a-a6be5a0463d9","text":"M3.4 Reject the universal strength-before-power sequence and the universal squat ratio."},{"id":"b2fa9d0d-840e-508b-966'
    || '9-4649c8632c11","text":"M3.5 Build a limitation hypothesis from multiple sources."},{"id":"1d950230-7013-54ac-89ce-cec02f6b6d8c","text":"M3.6 State the confidence level and review point for any hypothesis."},{"id":"42a65a3b-1e52-5013-b555-0b061cdf4b42","text":"M3.7 Revise or abandon a hypothesis the response does not support."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"86adfcb3-7d3b-5478-b465-88e7415d5989","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M2"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('4eada124-ec7e-5573-a5a0-e9b53e9e1526', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a15c7a04-f35c-5177-92eb-da59929b87f0', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"b377cb2b-4486-5b0d-b4c6-3f3b6ce27b44","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L3.1 What strength buys","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"f837d2ed-efb5-5d07-a59b-b8c5853a6665","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Larger force capacity can increase force available across velocities. Real, and neither complete nor independent of coordination and velocity expression."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"af70f335-2b4f-5e1a-a162-ed5e39164c0d","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L3.2 Who benefits most","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"8f69bc8f-e103-58e2-b30a-4845e9c05e8e","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The contribution is generally more consequential in weaker or less-trained athletes — which makes \"weak\" a contextual judgment, not a number."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"1bf0431b-0a2b-5713-adb2-8545eb8f97ac","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L3.3 Where it stops","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"1fef5e61-d7da-5d35-b53b-4d2363da7b9b","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Diminishing returns; task-specific limits; and why \"more strength always means more power\" is Class F."},"position":"a5"},{"i'
    || 'd":"409634d7-a3d9-5652-bab0-effa71a26b3c","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L3.4 No gate","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"6c279885-f445-5dab-96d8-6fbe282b4e37","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The universal strength phase and the squat-ratio prerequisite are unsupported, the latter explicitly. What replaces them: competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demand (AS-105-06)."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"e3f1941c-cb56-5f36-9c72-4271caecb013","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L3.5 Building the hypothesis","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"3d8b8273-03f1-55c4-91d3-75ce88e44716","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Five sources: task performance, force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context. A hypothesis built on one of them is a label."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"8c34af57-afa3-58be-9b38-b0c7010ea081","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L3.6 Confidence and review","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"12f3563a-6c7e-52be-9676-6be80c506e22","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Every hypothesis carries how confident the coach is and when it will be revisited.'
    || ' This is what makes it provisional rather than an identity (AS-105-08)."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"7d1d5b66-ea0c-5234-b20e-873ef6ec6dc1","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L3.7 Being wrong well","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"bc8fd29a-83f9-5b63-93c1-8ecf058ab5b0","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"What a non-response means, and how to abandon a hypothesis without abandoning the athlete''s block."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('ee9e613f-03dc-5299-b3e6-e4be49f1014d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3f859629-9ce9-5a48-b5f7-89030ed4846b', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"4098eeff-0896-5343-b448-4161c97c9b7f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-04 — Strength Supports Power but Does Not Gate It","body":"Strength matters, especially in weaker and less-trained athletes, but G3 does not require a universal strength phase or squat ratio before appropriate power training. — Teaching boundary: Rejecting the gate is not rejecting strength. Competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demand still govern what an athlete does (AS-105-06) — those are coaching judgments, not a ratio. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"5cab7ccc-5b4f-5c0b-8097-8106800bb66a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-02 — Related Qualities Are Not Interchangeable","body":"Strength, force, impulse, velocity, power, and RFD interact but are distinct. — Teaching boundary: Distinct does not mean unrelated — these qualities interact continuously in every athletic task. The doctrine forbids substitution, not integration. \"Explosiveness\" is Class F as a precise diagnostic term and is replaced by the actual quality and task. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"e5'
    || '7b6e77-de3d-5852-ac26-e5f120793380","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-07 — Transfer Must Be Demonstrated, Not Assumed","body":"Improvement in a jump, lift, throw, profile, or laboratory metric does not automatically establish sport transfer. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-7 and G3 104 D104-2. This is not a claim that transfer is rare — it is a requirement that the transfer pathway be stated and, where possible, observed. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"28bcdbda-8db1-5fe1-bf3c-a69e57ee8015","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A / Known — contribution differs with task and athlete status","body":"Strength is a contributor to power — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"c138a3ca-a94d-5f76-b44c-686f52611fec","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B / Probable — \"weak\" must be task- and population-contextualized","body":"Weaker or less-trained athletes often benefit materially from strength development — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"22b0dbd0-daff-5a80-8e7d-abe98eb358a1","type":"'
    || 'callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Concurrent development of strength and power-oriented tasks is supported in many populations — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"bff1bd91-1fed-5b85-89cc-5e1f52c6a34d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Athletes must get strong before any power training — Basis: EP105-11, EP105-10; EP105-11; EP105-10"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"d3b76325-b218-59f4-93be-0f5dc9970583","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — no validated universal threshold; the 1.5× prerequisite is explicitly unsupported","body":"A universal squat ratio is required before plyometrics — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"c9f8a2a9-580c-592d-8ca2-f56f3a5f8110","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — diminishing returns and task-specific limits exist","body":"More strength always produces more sport power — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"eef283ea-d0dc-50d1-8f99-3f79a2617278","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data"'
    || ':{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — concurrent and mixed approaches are defensible","body":"Strength and power require separate phases — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"5141050c-5d41-59ee-b1cc-7bbad4bd0720","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"A single metric identifies the limiting quality — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-08; EP105-02; EP105-08"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"0def3fd1-cfaa-5b56-a512-6234e09f4968","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-02 — Build Limitation Hypotheses From Multiple Sources","body":"Use task performance, strength/force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context before labeling an athlete."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"ab3f1518-8b5a-557e-a438-0387ccf9e274","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-06 — No Universal Strength Gate","body":"Use competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demands instead of arbitrary bodyweight ratios."},"position":"b2"},{"id":"b7edfaf0-babd-5a8e-ab72-3bb702550c46","type":"callout","schemaVersi'
    || 'on":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."},"position":"b3"},{"id":"20ff7ab6-f971-5e87-89eb-a7d6dcf65805","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the hypothesis itself, its confidence, and its review point (CJ-05, CJ-06)."},"position":"b4"},{"id":"26c53af0-5be5-5e53-80c6-875ce28a7df4","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Strength-before-power absolutes (CA-01) · squat-ratio prerequisites (CA-02) · separate-phase requirements (CA-16) · labels treated as identities · single-source hypotheses · never revisiting a hypothesis that the response contradicted."},"position":"b5"},{"id":"a785430b-616a-5919-bdab-4e4da1617a77","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"the strength–power evidence is adult-weighted; the squat-ratio rejection derives from a youth-focused review'
    || ' and is stated with that origin."},"position":"b6"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('989aa351-43c9-5517-b3e8-46eaeb6e6294', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3bb65f4a-9f32-514f-8a29-8e283d59628a', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"4d917875-fe3d-5f3f-aad5-0037db6e7bff","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Applied at every G3 intake and every phase review. The artifact is a two-sentence hypothesis with its sources, confidence, and review point — short enough that coaches actually write it."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"a741778f-c496-5ff6-9ca0-7abc12b854d9","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Strong High-School Athlete With Poor Acceleration. Marcus, 17, three years of well-coached strength training. Trap-bar deadlift and squat among the strongest in the program absolutely and relative to body mass. CMJ above team average. First-step and 10 m acceleration among the poorest in the squad, consistently, across two testing occasions on the same surface with the same timing method. Video shows a tall first step, late shin angle, and a hesitant initiation under reactive conditions. Sport practice is dense; there has been no dedicated sprint-technical exposure.","steps":[{"id":"6c916f02-2602-5027-bea3-e4e28b9e3e2f","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"5ca321c0-f264-5d02-8077-07ac2d9e96e2","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"a0164a05-1ed6-5a67-b9d9-0d7582380ebd","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"'
    || '9881ad34-69a6-590e-812e-6605ad41d16b","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is the limitation hypothesis, and what does the block contain?\n\nFailure modes: \"He needs more power\" as the plan · a jump metric used to diagnose acceleration · an F-V label applied without a profile, or with one · vector-matching justification · ignoring the absence of sprint exposure · a hypothesis with no falsifier.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who prioritizes reactive and initiation work, or one who prioritizes sprint-technical exposure, both pass with the hypothesis and falsifier stated. Adding a strength block fails absent a force-capacity argument the data does not support."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"eafb6319-8a45-591a-8bd6-7b1db9ce680c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-1 (completed at M4). Observed elements from M3: the coach states a hypothesis and its confidence out loud, from real athlete information."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('e73ab9d0-ed35-51f9-a6b7-40e972a8a534', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '60f30586-7676-5ab5-869a-e6afbb62006f', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"c5bedb5f-470a-52ca-be81-06cc29836009","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"97e5996e-0148-5d1e-9901-6c134be5e285","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"71df8dd0-bf2f-5801-8cb1-f7f11fb8dab4","title":"G3-105 · M3 Assessment — Strength–Power Relationship & Athlete Limitation Hypotheses"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('3edd1759-94cb-581c-aace-36a0aa2cbf19', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'a0b6a4d2-463c-5ce3-b9bb-5a2b4aa3a541', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"b515b427-efa7-5e23-9e87-af3a9ac67945","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"A profile is a description produced by assumptions. It can start a conversation. It cannot end one."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"ae789722-ad43-583e-936d-77e87f0dfec5","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To teach F-V profiling and load-power relationships accurately — what they describe, what they cannot diagnose, and why the load that maximizes acute power is not automatically the training load."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"3ee010e3-2a3c-5cc9-9fbc-d669f62cf1b8","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"cdff9f65-08e7-5d1b-b617-ac5a3270008f","text":"M4.1 Describe F0, V0, Pmax, slope, imbalance, and \"optimal profile\" as modeled outputs."},{"id":"99dee662-cbf9-534f-adbb-740cacb6bba6","text":"M4.2 State the reliability and validity limits of vertical-jump profiling methods."},{"id":"2ec2386d-b1f9-57a7-bee8-e1c1a73c57bc","text":"M4.3 Explain why external load-velocity relationships are not the intrinsic muscle force-velocity relationship."},{"id":"f0fe8424-cd56-545b-aff2-f4af0bc6b6e2","text":"M4.4 Describe the load-power curve and why the peak load varies."},{"id'
    || '":"a0bc4254-f981-5e50-8508-9f04c25cdea3","text":"M4.5 Distinguish acute peak-power load from optimal chronic training load."},{"id":"791f57d7-ffa5-5fa7-a1b1-6761ae44ebad","text":"M4.6 Use a profile to generate a hypothesis without diagnostic overreach."},{"id":"fa0c1769-ba25-5e2d-b740-f37f028d65a7","text":"M4.7 State when profiling should be optional, advanced, or excluded."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"6083a85e-b8c5-5507-861b-132f9691bd71","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M3 · Sign-off: PS-1"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('c1b33a8e-5201-588e-a3e7-4bf47ad8aa72', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3d853ca-886b-5db1-b517-a9efc90e3ae0', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"8cfb348a-efc8-5b02-b75a-04a8aa0a69de","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L4.1 What the model does","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"21999f55-4968-54da-bf71-d9a78a40f368","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Measured points, fitted relationship, derived parameters. Every step is an assumption doing work."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"945b72db-b181-5c31-8dc1-3e00b423a894","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L4.2 Reliability, stated in numbers","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"c1b23285-5c12-5653-a2f6-4646af85dc70","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"High linearity does not mean reliable slope; reported cross-method variation for slope is large enough that two methods can label the same athlete differently. That fact — not the label — is what coaches need."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"2ddcbbe3-7a2a-5a70-bdf0-6825c1dc8f58","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L4.3 Validity is method-dependent","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"11b522a3-a489-5bb8-b94d-039b772cc38d","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Method selection materially changes interpretation, which means a profile without its met'
    || 'hod is uninterpretable."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"70004bf2-f96b-59a8-bc3d-fa610e2a7cf5","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L4.4 External load-velocity is not muscle F-V","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"51c07bc4-1399-5c79-b166-8530adb936d1","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The two are routinely conflated; the conflation is what makes \"deficit\" language sound biological."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"7528be5d-8380-520c-97e6-07355b9326bb","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L4.5 The load-power curve","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"17fd76af-55c4-5028-9f90-7db9928ecf8b","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Intermediate loads often maximize acute power, and the exact load moves with exercise, athlete, metric, and protocol. G3 publishes no percentage."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"d9ffa81b-ce16-57f7-ab54-14084690a014","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L4.6 Acute versus chronic","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"5d3da0b3-7a4d-5453-af97-4094ee1e1a47","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"§2.4''s acute–chronic rule applied to loading: what produced the highest power once does not determine the'
    || ' training load for twelve weeks."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"c5fc674a-dce1-513b-916c-acca6a745891","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L4.7 When to profile","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"471fee30-e2c9-528e-b809-73115487f52e","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"An organizational question, answered by AS-105-11: does it change a decision here, at acceptable cost, with a method you can standardize?"},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('11c613e9-c19f-5abf-9ec1-9bbc4900eb4e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2908478f-ef31-54b6-9409-adaf651b81a9', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"d6eb4e6a-93ca-53c6-8c7c-21f99f69f606","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-09 — Profiling Generates Hypotheses","body":"Force–velocity profiles may describe performance and support hypotheses; profile labels are not definitive diagnoses. — Teaching boundary: The model–measurement rule (§2.4) governs this doctrine. A profile is a description produced by assumptions. No F-V threshold, deficit classification, or optimal-profile target is published (CJ-03, CJ-04). — Evidence class: B for describing performance across selected loads with transparent protocol; C/D for generating training hypotheses; F for definitively diagnosing \"force deficiency\" or \"velocity deficiency,\" for using an \"optimal profile\" as an exact target for all athletes, and for replacing sprint/jump testing, technical observation, and sport context"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"489c73e9-21c4-515a-a2ac-a59aa1024d0b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-05 — Acute Output Does Not Dictate Chronic Prescription","body":"The load producing the highest acute measured power is not automatically the best long-term training loa'
    || 'd. — Teaching boundary: No load percentage is published anywhere in this course (CJ-01, CJ-02). Acute peak-power testing may describe an exercise response for an athlete; it does not set the chronic training load. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"38349ac2-b890-5204-ba62-09779b320b97","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"21fce82f-5b0f-5c7b-be74-5aa1052fff20","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"F-V models combine measured variables with modeled theoretical outputs and are not direct measurements of muscular capacity — Basis: EP105-05, EP105-07; EP105-05; EP105-07"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"264326b0-e419-5343-b600-d9f9172722c5","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","ti'
    || 'tle":"Evidence — Class B — reported coefficients of variation of 14–30% in the cited reliability work","body":"Vertical-jump profiles can show high linearity while slope reliability across methods is poor — Basis: EP105-05; EP105-05"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"1ab4f890-40e6-51ab-a3bf-fa71695bc01c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Validity is method-dependent; one common method showed poor-to-fair validity while a two-point method with distal loads performed better — Basis: EP105-06; EP105-06"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"e43e8696-d038-5a63-b820-7064fe41eadd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Describing performance across selected loads with transparent protocol — Basis: EP105-05; EP105-05"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"270616c8-b2cc-538d-81c8-1510f240bd8b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/D — when corroborated by performance and observation","body":"Generating training hypotheses from a profile — Basis: EP105-07, EP105-25; EP105-07; EP105-25"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"a276e54d-738c-58b9-b768-f4c8da5bc663","type":"callout","'
    || 'schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Definitively diagnosing \"force deficiency\" or \"velocity deficiency\" — Basis: EP105-05, EP105-06, EP105-07; EP105-05; EP105-06; EP105-07"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"6d050378-c742-5643-8802-bbf8a2abda9e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","body":"Using \"optimal profile\" as an exact target for all athletes — Basis: EP105-07, EP105-16; EP105-07; EP105-16"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"c68f5533-d918-5879-8869-52d7bed2075d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Replacing sprint/jump testing, technical observation, and sport context with profiling — Basis: EP105-07; EP105-07"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"f5c26351-8426-5864-b39b-b61c93d40d76","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C — Emerging; sprint effects non-significant in one review","body":"Profile-based individualized training improving vertical-profile outcomes and jump height — Basis: EP105-16, EP105-25; EP105-16; EP105-25"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"3cefad45-82c5-5ecf-bb73-5c4d54115bb9","type":"callout","schema'
    || 'Version":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Maximal measured power often occurs at an intermediate load — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"4c6b136d-3200-560c-97f7-89541af5b913","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"The exact peak-power load varies by exercise, athlete, method, external vs system power, body mass, and peak vs mean outcome — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b3"},{"id":"f63ae84b-729e-549e-b17b-d71373f4b128","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"A universal \"30% 1RM\" rule — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b4"},{"id":"b395c0f4-0fc9-518f-8733-efe840b75cfb","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"The acute peak-power load is the best chronic training load — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b5"},{"id":"c6ca6faf-2b1f-5fdc-bca6-8db6f76564ff","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-08 — Treat F-V Labels as Provisional","body":"Force- and velocity-deficit labels require corroboration. — Enfo'
    || 'rcement: NovaKore stores a limitation hypothesis with confidence and review point; no label may be stored as a fixed athlete attribute."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"f86162d3-6c17-5e4f-b53e-daa82b80f3c8","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-02 — Build Limitation Hypotheses From Multiple Sources","body":"Use task performance, strength/force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context before labeling an athlete."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"6b79abb7-08b4-5e49-bab7-babd6839da45","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."},"position":"b8"},{"id":"142b40f7-afc4-541c-98b9-01ab4ac40229","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."},"position":"b9"},{"id":"7db04c80-a08d-5fde-a30e-d0493c6bac2c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether to profile at a'
    || 'll; how much weight a profile carries (CJ-03, CJ-04); the training load (CJ-01, CJ-02)."},"position":"c0"},{"id":"3d16a103-ef9e-508b-9b12-ab8df82ed4fd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Reading a profile as a diagnosis (D105-09, AS-105-08) · prescribing 30% 1RM (CA-04) · using an acute peak-power load as the chronic load (CA-05) · comparing profiles across methods or devices · treating \"optimal profile\" as a target · letting a profile replace observation."},"position":"c1"},{"id":"c9d7da36-f2a9-5aee-9851-80b80bbd13d4","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"profiling reliability and validity work is largely adult and vertical-jump-specific; the emerging individualization evidence is new, with varying profile methods, and requires downstream risk-of-bias review before organizational adoption."},"position":"c2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('5739871e-0153-568e-a673-ae8c3144fafb', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5ccc1e15-0ce9-5872-9b17-eed48c42c16b', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"2c1a5d75-44b0-5a9b-a92a-627bdafb9a37","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"In most G3 environments profiling is optional. Where it is used, the defensible form is: one standardized method, transparent protocol, results described rather than labeled, and any hypothesis corroborated against actual performance and observation before it changes a program."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"7ddb4ae8-7da3-5082-b69e-0c755d438a91","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Coach Prescribing 30% 1RM for Every Power Exercise. A G3 coach has standardized every power exercise at 30% 1RM — jump squats, bench throws, and pulls — citing \"the optimal power load.\" Athletes range from a 14-year-old novice to a fourth-year collegiate athlete. The coach also cites an in-house testing day where several athletes produced their highest measured power near that load in the jump squat.","steps":[{"id":"ba511be7-b6b2-5dc0-a897-a76ddeb7d258","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"208b3dc7-732a-50ab-88a8-49277588e7e4","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"6655e0bb-b16c-5f58-b918-c3de31a41277","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"6b645bd2-c0f4-5ab7-82b3-dc3503bd0d76","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":'
    || '"Decision required: What is right about this, what is wrong, and what replaces it?\n\nFailure modes: Replacing 30% with another number · keeping the rule for one exercise \"because it was measured\" · dismissing the coach''s testing entirely · treating the acute result as chronic evidence · applying a 1RM-percentage scheme to a novice youth athlete without comment.\n\nDefensible variation: Retaining a loading range as a starting point for a specific exercise and athlete, labeled as a working convention, passes. Retaining any figure as \"the optimal power load\" fails."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"d832cd2a-22a5-5d4c-9549-8fc31beac293","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-1 — Power Evaluation & Method Selection. See §14.2. PS-1 gates M5."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('88b3f771-a1a0-5b4e-84d8-d3e21ff4db13', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '73f93ca7-7dd4-50ab-8e07-78295a95bf39', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"dd06828f-bf04-5251-92fb-74beabffee72","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"a87a989f-8a10-5bab-9009-530a27d1a67b","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"c57e5687-598a-508b-a392-6d25be59769b","title":"G3-105 · M4 Assessment — Force–Velocity & Load–Power Models"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('23faf8fa-974c-5124-a08f-403c61d4cef1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6d9a306d-d657-5d88-9e20-c2103322d57e', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'PS-1 · Power Evaluation & Method Selection', true, null, (('[{"id":"71a5acf7-d417-5a06-82c2-94cf651d3adc","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"PS-1 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M4. Gates M5. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"824012e3-d843-55c2-892c-4d41265a68be","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3},"position":"a1"},{"id":"b760606a-d31b-51b8-8115-901c24fac86e","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"571bf233-4ac4-5572-829c-b7b5111eafaf","text":"Defines the demand and the output requirement before discussing methods."},{"id":"b2a8816b-6a90-5c44-bd3f-3f962a66e804","text":"Uses multiple sources — task performance, force capacity, observation, history, context — to build the hypothesis."},{"id":"3f465166-7811-5f3f-b56c-4fc949fcfa76","text":"States the hypothesis as provisional, with confidence and a review point."},{"id":"0b5c2abb-a3f3-5f5e-9e65-068feae087b4","text":"Selects a method and states the alternatives considered and why they were not chosen."},{"id":"6ae7b75e-c6cc-54e8-83e8-8b5e28a2ec3c","text":"Separates evidence from infer'
    || 'ence when asked about any element."},{"id":"e270228e-9b96-5a9f-86e0-afc0bf39c539","text":"Uses no threshold, ratio, percentage, or label as justification."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"c06d60fe-ad07-5565-89f2-4ad4581627f4","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3},"position":"a3"},{"id":"9fc57a06-74f3-5f8a-87b9-23fae99467f6","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"c88db82f-1f6c-566d-84a8-589762f49e04","text":"power language audit (A1)"},{"id":"1c1a3d0e-ef04-5102-980d-8c11ab010dee","text":"transfer statement (A2)"},{"id":"7ebfc50c-de78-560d-89a4-1e5ef9c41232","text":"limitation hypothesis (A3)"},{"id":"db99cc32-f47c-5f63-b92c-71a0b512158c","text":"profile interpretation (A4)."}]},"position":"a4"},{"id":"2d438c53-13fd-5a52-b351-fcd5326c272c","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Assessor questions","level":3},"position":"a5"},{"id":"fd4ce593-948e-5837-bf87-c91772c1a3ce","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"ce771e0b-43b7-51e5-8afb-15000993d3f8","text":"What is the output requirement here?"},{"id":"62634158-cd35-5c81-a65b-d8e841f0a375","text":"What are your sources for that hypothesis, and h'
    || 'ow confident are you?"},{"id":"f01432e7-ffdb-5825-9fc0-7ff3bbccca39","text":"What would make you abandon it?"},{"id":"d092af18-0070-5351-ab55-4e2e4cb91275","text":"What else could you have used instead of this method, and why not?"}]},"position":"a6"},{"id":"1783a07a-957f-55f0-a289-231b02b0c5ce","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"28187c81-0a8b-5f81-b658-dafbe41fe80f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."},"position":"a8"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('34b2f119-e027-5ed0-919c-b80d96c4791d', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '4344f312-7d4b-539d-be65-f13c37a72927', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"90cbb503-8338-5cba-ac6d-ee0fa7b312d0","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Trying to move fast and moving fast are related, and they are not the same thing. Both matter, and the difference is coachable."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"f3f247bb-4f60-563c-8e08-b5b6228a77c6","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To teach ballistic methods as a viable family with defined boundaries, and to establish intent as a real training variable without overclaiming what intent alone achieves."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"77338fb8-d02b-5e61-b458-351acae4e852","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"c9398b2d-ec70-5635-9127-c73d60419f76","text":"M5.1 Define ballistic exercise by continued acceleration or projection."},{"id":"35a81028-4e9f-5eaf-9f56-8c9e5909c473","text":"M5.2 Select among loaded jumps, jump squats, bench throws, and ballistic push-ups by athlete and constraint."},{"id":"49978371-ebef-5e7c-a185-25211e5f664f","text":"M5.3 Explain why ballistic intent is not the same as light load."},{"id":"084b7859-ab6c-50f1-ba4c-c7f52cb1d91e","text":"M5.4 Apply maximal concentric intent appropriately across loads."},{"id":"49142385-963e-5da8-be24-73ca2d28'
    || '7dd6","text":"M5.5 State what intent alone does and does not establish."},{"id":"d70c4920-4612-5098-907e-c25f5ba683af","text":"M5.6 Select loading without a universal percentage."},{"id":"0abf8f85-c7e1-5e26-a841-0266497a30e0","text":"M5.7 Preserve output quality within a ballistic set."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"e1c5bb29-d528-5580-9515-15e7f5cac150","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires PS-1"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('0473bbea-7de8-5452-98e9-3342dc9a91f8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc6d59a1-2491-5576-b6d4-4bbb7cc21252', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"e73b8ea5-f261-5603-a6ae-921584cea4b9","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L5.1 What makes a movement ballistic","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"80d99600-7292-5d79-9518-6b67f7d6a9be","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Continued acceleration through the movement or implement release, rather than deliberate end-range deceleration."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"4e47c160-d85e-5a9b-a8d7-efa7591dd018","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L5.2 The family","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"7e2f95ea-2dae-55a6-b92f-78d1ff7c8a37","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Jumps, loaded jumps, jump squats, bench throws, ballistic push-ups, medicine-ball throws — with their equipment, spotting, and tolerance demands stated."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"04bc83f9-8311-5f01-b161-502aaaae729d","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L5.3 Intent versus load","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"041eb962-f372-5ac6-ab9e-1348b02688ec","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"A light non-ballistic lift may still require deceleration; a ballistic movement may permit fuller acceleration. Load and ballistic character are diff'
    || 'erent variables."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"5bc4230e-f06c-5efe-9c0e-4d0c47016bec","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L5.4 Intent across loads","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"c2558dbe-3993-5bc1-ae68-128a94c4b316","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Maximal intended concentric acceleration is relevant even where external velocity is constrained by a heavy load — and it does not make heavy and light training equivalent."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"88bd4133-8467-598c-a0cf-cd5506dc3654","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L5.5 Selecting the load","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"a8f58aa4-01a6-5b09-812d-c7f4e53c7051","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"By intended adaptation, athlete competence, and preserved output. No percentage. Where a coach uses a descriptive load test, it describes that athlete in that exercise on that day."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"61ecfeae-4dfd-5fd7-a150-c4ab5f16103c","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L5.6 Quality within the set","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"41e36f1c-038e-5a40-92af-866f00dce429","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"What output decline looks '
    || 'like, and the stop criteria the coach sets in advance (AS-105-04)."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"c22e8000-2dcd-5696-8caf-307053bbd2f4","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L5.7 Benefit-to-cost","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"afdadf92-d92c-52e6-9103-340411a4eb68","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"AS-105-03 applied: when a simpler ballistic option delivers the needed stimulus at lower equipment, learning, and supervision cost, it is the better selection."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('29a4ddd8-4a9b-5ce5-ace4-34aded49efb4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '70928a4c-ff73-5c0b-86b4-b1ff42752435', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"bb7146a9-4891-538c-bb94-f8687eaa423a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"6941a1fa-c821-5ce3-bebf-0a49d18b3faf","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-05 — Acute Output Does Not Dictate Chronic Prescription","body":"The load producing the highest acute measured power is not automatically the best long-term training load. — Teaching boundary: No load percentage is published anywhere in this course (CJ-01, CJ-02). Acute peak-power testing may describe an exercise response for an athlete; it does not set the chronic training load. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a'
    || '1"},{"id":"d40f6a33-2fbe-5a17-988f-503e8906745c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"0a4bb7dc-249a-5452-b142-7b2eeaea480a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Ballistic, plyometric, and weightlifting methods are all viable power-training categories — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-04; EP105-02; EP105-04"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"92c6e663-8e88-5907-ad8c-c4289841f5af","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — reasonable for trained adolescents and adults; youth use requires competence and supervision","b'
    || 'ody":"Jump squat / loaded jump as a method — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"338b44a0-678a-5501-8a01-ab3b2fa5c75a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — most evidence from trained adult males; requires equipment and spotting","body":"Bench throw as a method — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"407621e9-51b2-5190-811f-761981ec3863","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C — limited direct comparative evidence","body":"Explosive push-up as a method — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"423683ea-c6de-5dc6-ad1e-10abc2b5d18d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"No single peak-power load is universally superior — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"1d465070-f069-5d7b-922a-12c5366ff094","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Ballistic intent is not identical to using a light load — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"36471371-60f3-563b-a0f9-a5fdbe1325d6","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"ton'
    || 'e":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B / Probable","body":"Maximal intended concentric acceleration is a relevant training principle — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"82a5369d-b46c-5273-bfbb-ade4fae16190","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Intent alone guarantees identical adaptation across loads — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"a2c01b76-57f9-5705-b0bf-2e1b0dd9717e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Explosive intent matters only with light loads\" — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"300e95c0-6032-5337-b585-9f6333cd305e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Power training must use light loads\" — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"111013ad-26c8-5b9c-acfb-578094352f28","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calen'
    || 'dar."},"position":"b3"},{"id":"491a0abc-89a8-5ab8-844a-c6ff77895b66","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation."},"position":"b4"},{"id":"0534f397-808e-50c9-aab9-ae6f8a58b008","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."},"position":"b5"},{"id":"40245b31-95ee-5a4b-ae1b-d20e294756e4","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the load (CJ-01, CJ-02); which ballistic variants suit this athlete and facility."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"e1df44d2-4ce5-595f-b871-01e8abbb1644","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Light-load-only power training (CA-03) · a universal 30% 1RM (CA-04) · intent claimed to matter only with'
    || ' light loads (CA-17) · using the acute peak-power load as the training load (CA-05) · sets long enough that the last reps train something else · loaded jumps prescribed to athletes without the competence to land them."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"fb704f73-ab6c-58d9-bd55-fdcad3705eff","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"bench-throw and much ballistic evidence comes from resistance-trained adult males; youth application requires competence, supervision, and progression rather than scaled adult loading (Condition 2)."},"position":"b8"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('476dae27-f0c3-5963-9261-edd68fe1a1b3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '95c04cb7-430c-50fc-9879-4aefe4c8d17c', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"0c3475f0-2e41-5a4a-bc0d-6d3ac0569dc9","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"For a G3 group with limited equipment, unloaded and lightly loaded jump variants plus medicine-ball throws cover most ballistic needs with low technical and equipment overhead — a defensible selection under AS-105-03, not a compromise."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"9a5dc171-39e5-5b5c-82af-f39c6f93c1a1","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Coach Prescribing 30% 1RM for Every Power Exercise. A G3 coach has standardized every power exercise at 30% 1RM — jump squats, bench throws, and pulls — citing \"the optimal power load.\" Athletes range from a 14-year-old novice to a fourth-year collegiate athlete. The coach also cites an in-house testing day where several athletes produced their highest measured power near that load in the jump squat.","steps":[{"id":"85ecb686-f5b3-55ea-a099-9cce177632d2","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"2c333d6b-5e68-51ca-a2a4-1f9838ed1088","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"9571eab1-3f29-5285-ab82-e5b873a87e4e","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"c3adca47-e6a8-57b4-b018-a12e9f04dd46","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is right about thi'
    || 's, what is wrong, and what replaces it?\n\nFailure modes: Replacing 30% with another number · keeping the rule for one exercise \"because it was measured\" · dismissing the coach''s testing entirely · treating the acute result as chronic evidence · applying a 1RM-percentage scheme to a novice youth athlete without comment.\n\nDefensible variation: Retaining a loading range as a starting point for a specific exercise and athlete, labeled as a working convention, passes. Retaining any figure as \"the optimal power load\" fails."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"c5f70d97-e08d-5bc9-8b12-5322836a8150","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-2."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('6dc7b86c-e630-50aa-8cb9-622b4958f188', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '61a4aff6-6108-55ab-838f-6d371aa35a02', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"9835c733-a435-5974-bec9-8805ef0db86a","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"136e5903-7199-5c60-b4d7-305fdb0280fe","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"d66a8fd6-30ae-57c3-a289-533cd59b5a4a","title":"G3-105 · M5 Assessment — Ballistic Training & Explosive Intent"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('68fbd3ad-39da-57bd-b53a-7867937daa86', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3b8a624a-b8d9-5f21-9746-ad73ae3d6065', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 12, (('[{"id":"fb6f8399-b3da-535d-86ad-3d8837fc530d","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"\"Plyometrics\" names a family whose members differ more than they resemble each other. Progress the demand, not the count."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"a3d6fcc2-9960-5ab6-beff-292096412545","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To teach plyometric training as a family of distinct demands, RSI as a task ratio rather than a diagnosis, and landing as a coached, progressed exposure with conservative safety language."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"d509fd07-ebae-55d3-987b-c453396a9aed","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"15086824-fd68-56a4-bb36-37abbc788335","text":"M6.1 Describe the SSC without reducing it to stored elastic energy."},{"id":"65217993-aca5-548c-b552-02d55940a4ac","text":"M6.2 Distinguish plyometric tasks by landing, eccentric, contact-time, coordination, and tissue demand."},{"id":"07c473e7-3ed1-5062-9391-262fc9667a69","text":"M6.3 State what plyometric training is supported to improve."},{"id":"a0e1af11-6a05-5403-937c-1c5081128752","text":"M6.4 Interpret RSI and RSI-modified as task-specific ratios."},{"id":"acaffe48-701d-5db9-849b-12f3e3f0eebe"'
    || ',"text":"M6.5 Coach and progress landing and braking demand."},{"id":"2a2927f7-2090-5614-bbca-78a8771fbdc5","text":"M6.6 Progress plyometric demand rather than contact count."},{"id":"803131d9-c321-5af2-b98c-d41ed032e76f","text":"M6.7 Apply conservative causal language to landing and injury claims."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"41645d9f-f16f-57c6-bf02-823913f5a62a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M5"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('35e38f91-c204-5749-8bd4-f6343e7804b1', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '3dd0c107-e2e7-5bde-bd89-ad446a5b40f5', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 48, (('[{"id":"6fd1c03b-9235-5bef-b3a7-f55ae4570f4d","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L6.1 The SSC, honestly","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"bd841a57-3dd7-513d-a087-3ec35d0f0b17","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Eccentric, transition, concentric. Elastic behavior matters and does not explain performance alone. The ~250 ms convention is a convention."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"1c60bf7a-c4bc-54d0-967d-4c912d6c1e4a","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L6.2 The family, sorted by demand","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"c2db87b7-783e-5239-a888-c14e4dc4cbab","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Countermovement jumps, hops, bounds, drop and depth jumps, unilateral and bilateral, horizontal and vertical — sorted by landing force, eccentric demand, contact time, coordination, and tissue loading rather than by name."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"a03190c2-ffeb-58bd-98ba-8b4ef670f83e","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L6.3 What plyometrics are supported to improve","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"d8221eb0-5bde-5758-9697-a4a2bfab01cf","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Jump outcomes robustly; sp'
    || 'rint and COD in many populations; with heterogeneity stated and mechanism claims withheld."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"707081cd-8ee9-5b4f-b705-d1459ca8bf64","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L6.4 RSI and RSI-modified","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"64b9c325-8128-511c-99a4-6537c61c118c","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"What the ratios are, what standardization they require, and what they cannot tell you (D105-12)."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"c336e19e-9bdb-55df-b31e-c71c04678395","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L6.5 Landing as the trained quality","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"157aa3b1-8df5-5f55-89c9-05dd7f1143c5","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Braking and eccentric absorption; height, surface, direction, support, fatigue, and technique as the variables. Progressive supervised exposure is supported; single-cue prevention claims are not."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"cc3242cb-6414-5265-b8ab-9aac5ea51b26","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L6.6 Progress demand, not contacts","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"1971362f-12e0-53c0-bfb5-703bdcbe3510","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"'
    || 'text":"AS-105-05: landing demand, velocity, amplitude, direction, unilateral demand, external load, complexity, exposure. No contact classification is published."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"5e718523-2ef5-5bd3-9ffc-5620956a02d3","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L6.7 Safety language","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"85021225-19e8-5734-a199-452c22af64d8","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The accurate sentence, rehearsed: exposure and progression are defensible; guarantees are not (§19, CJ-22)."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('8946e24b-11e2-5d8b-9858-6681ccbbff35', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c378dea6-a40e-5e82-9d64-b7c02a75da5d', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 30, (('[{"id":"4ed2d04c-2d88-5f87-8645-df9675d2ba28","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"7c268b49-c1b1-5fd9-b35f-2fa2d198c396","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-12 — Reactive Metrics Are Task Measures","body":"RSI, RSI-modified, jump height, contact time, and force-plate variables do not independently define readiness, stiffness, injury risk, or explosiveness. — Teaching boundary: These metrics are worth collecting when standardized and decision-relevant. They are not worth a label. — Evidence class: A that RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio; F for the claim that RS'
    || 'I alone completely measures reactive ability; F/C for the claim that low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit; B/C for RSI and RSI-modified contributing to a broader standardized monitoring battery"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"c6546d24-6f64-51bd-81f3-014294e1e3d5","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-08 — Directional Similarity Is Not Sufficient Specificity","body":"Horizontal, vertical, unilateral, bilateral, or visually sport-like exercises do not guarantee direction- or sport-specific transfer. — Teaching boundary: Direction may matter. What is unsupported is the inference from visual direction to guaranteed transfer. Selecting unilateral or bilateral work by task, competence, tolerance, and desired loading is Class D — legitimate coaching inference, labeled as such (CJ-12). — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"a2841453-fa74-5d4e-a55b-d9e2e08f2eb1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-15 — Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management","body":"Coordinate power exposure with sprinting, jumping, strength, practice, competition, and recovery. No universal contact count,'
    || ' frequency, microcycle, or maintenance dose governs all athletes. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-8 and G3 104 D104-12. The athlete''s jumps in practice count. No frequency, contact count, or maintenance dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-15). — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"f9d09e23-db6f-5e81-a34b-e6d918258e47","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"f3b37aad-ebd1-534f-ac7c-69a2715fc939","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Plyometric training is a family of rapid SSC tasks with substantially different demands u'
    || 'nder one label — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-13; EP105-02; EP105-13"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"6c44ae5c-8b8b-5287-852d-cbf515854283","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A/B — heterogeneity is substantial","body":"Plyometric training improves jump and, in many populations, sprint and COD performance — Basis: EP105-14, EP105-20, EP105-21; EP105-14; EP105-20; EP105-21"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"1f6852fa-4121-5820-884d-2f0a0a3701ec","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Effects cannot be attributed exclusively to one mechanism such as tendon stiffness or RFD — Basis: EP105-14, EP105-20, EP105-21; EP105-14; EP105-20; EP105-21"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"6fb38de7-d7eb-5193-9543-20dda551dc1e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Plyometric versus resistance training shows no consistent difference for jump outcomes; one review found plyometrics superior at 5 m and 20 m sprint with no difference in several other outcomes — Basis: EP105-15; EP105-15"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"3ac8d85d-a6c1-577b-b801-0f11f0fc9ad3","type":"callout","schemaVersion'
    || '":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — neural activation, active muscle force, joint mechanics, pre-activation, and coordination also …","body":"\"Stored elastic energy\" as a complete explanation of SSC performance — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-03; EP105-02; EP105-03"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"86ae031b-9a07-56ed-a04d-1ffba5c46b32","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F — a practical convention","body":"The ~250 ms fast/slow SSC boundary as a validated biological threshold — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"5f288f03-1676-5eea-b33e-666b01b89a02","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"1877879d-b911-55a1-8a20-f176df82f3f2","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"RSI alone completely measures reactive ability — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-24; EP105-08; EP105-24"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"a29032ae-e9c5-55d9-8c01-bf3cb3ead95b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","bod'
    || 'y":"Low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"},"position":"b3"},{"id":"351c4403-1ed4-5452-bdd7-fab46a5cd08c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C","body":"RSI/RSI-modified contributing to a broader standardized monitoring battery — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-24; EP105-08; EP105-24"},"position":"b4"},{"id":"b4c2d7bf-564d-5479-9057-9e620a7551f7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Progressive, supervised landing exposure — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"},"position":"b5"},{"id":"a7ea7065-5d78-544e-bf0d-5f7209adec49","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/F","body":"Single-cue injury-prevention claims for landing — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"},"position":"b6"},{"id":"b8db6746-9bb3-506a-9c60-a4c5e40c7c2a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Universal contact-count classifications as evidence-based prescriptions — Basis: EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-19; EP105-21"},"position":"b7"},{"id":"7e4583d8-558a-5710-a7f4-358a658bea79","type":"callout","schemaV'
    || 'ersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — sample-specific, not a universal prescription","body":"Twice-weekly training over ~8–10 weeks associated with more stable improvements in adolescent team-sport samples — Basis: EP105-21; EP105-21"},"position":"b8"},{"id":"c57b0bab-ef9e-573f-97f5-61448303e191","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Depth jumps are the best plyometric\" / \"more contacts = more power\" / \"every jump must be maximal\" — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-02; EP105-19; EP105-21"},"position":"b9"},{"id":"050fcb2e-8d72-5775-b35f-569d6f37a8d2","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-05 — Progress Plyometric Demand, Not Just Contacts","body":"Progress landing demand, velocity, amplitude, direction, unilateral demand, external load, complexity, and exposure."},"position":"c0"},{"id":"9a4634d7-5a95-52c8-a278-f4b8e3d28dfe","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique r'
    || 'emain consistent with the intended adaptation."},"position":"c1"},{"id":"1a46e439-6d28-5b16-95bd-829e8a9dff34","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."},"position":"c2"},{"id":"641222e6-9024-55f5-bfc1-efd0109d551f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-14 — Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure","body":"Supervise, progressively dose, technically coach, and scale to maturity and training age without unsupported prohibitions. — Enforcement: supervision ratio documented for every G3 youth power group; the safety claim is stated with its conditions."},"position":"c3"},{"id":"740192ca-a27d-56c1-a0fb-47b395a3769f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the dose (CJ-08); whether and when depth jumps are used (CJ-09); progression rate."},"position":"c4"},{"id":"a221c600-0862-5a5f-b81e-c4941d5c3b14"'
    || ',"type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Counting contacts instead of progressing demand (CA-11) · treating depth jumps as universally best (CA-10) · every jump maximal (CA-12) · RSI read as reactive ability (CA-20) · low RSI read as a tendon diagnosis · plyometrics called inherently high risk (CA-08) · a landing cue promised to prevent ACL injury (CJ-22)."},"position":"c5"},{"id":"415c5ab5-4b50-50c8-afad-bb88b30d128f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"much plyometric meta-analytic evidence is youth and team-sport specific with high heterogeneity; the dose finding above concerns adolescent team-sport samples and is not generalized."},"position":"c6"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('25696229-7478-5a95-a7e2-a492afaaaf7f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1bae1aea-abf3-5183-aa8c-90e17fed8989', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 18, (('[{"id":"18ac58c0-757c-5b55-8d20-99eb8652fc1c","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"In G3 Volleyball Development the binding issue is usually that practice already supplies enormous jump and landing volume. The competent plan counts it first (AS-105-15) and often reduces gym contacts while raising their quality — which is Case 5."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"3edf1ecd-2516-5173-b7a2-11d2444dab70","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Middle-School Athlete New to Power Training. Ana, 12, joins a G3 Youth group of 18 with one coach, two 50-minute sessions weekly. No structured training history. Coordinated for her age, attentive, no injury history. She lands noisily and asymmetrically from a two-foot jump and has never been coached to land. Her father asks when she can start \"real explosive training,\" and mentions a coach who told him power work should wait until 16 and until she can squat 1.5× bodyweight.","steps":[{"id":"ec00387d-4e5c-5547-bffa-9277c1eb7a80","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"af1dcf57-4625-548a-a200-fa9b7dcc60c6","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"14b66402-0b87-5291-86f1-1275a84329d6","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"62d625e2-4f3d-5e19-964a-4d02f7'
    || '64a454","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What does Ana''s power exposure consist of, how does it progress, and what do you tell her father?\n\nFailure modes: Accepting either gate · substituting a different threshold · \"explosiveness\" language with a 12-year-old · adult contact counts or loading applied to youth · complexity beyond the supervision ratio · promising injury prevention · leaving landing uncoached because it looks like nothing.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who introduces light external load once landing is competent passes with a stated criterion. A coach who stays unloaded for the block and says why also passes."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"13fa63d3-6369-53c0-8a2e-03d1e0157507","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-2 (completed at M7). Observed elements from M6: the coach teaches and progresses a landing or plyometric task."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('52944305-cb10-5e64-92c1-41c2ef1a0ea9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '758d7ddc-2b5b-5b4f-bb7a-97617b2b011b', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 12, ('[{"id":"496a6bdc-8e5d-5f1b-a884-2865fd893650","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"2044e5e5-036c-58a7-b21e-833667c4a114","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"f87151a6-8dd1-5283-b055-d4ac866e817f","title":"G3-105 · M6 Assessment — Plyometrics, Reactive Strength & Landing"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('3067d945-9c42-5065-8ce6-2ddd8d6006a8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '27b5da3c-4fd6-5a96-8afd-531214e61144', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"661a3602-60cb-5c9e-a735-b44e83c909d3","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The clean is a good tool with a large tuition fee. Whether an athlete should pay it is a coaching decision, not an identity."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"3d11d521-b41b-5142-a4b5-11b189ece6eb","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To evaluate weightlifting derivatives, medicine-ball work, and upper-body ballistics on benefit-to-cost, and to settle the \"required versus dangerous\" argument in both directions."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"49a03e74-912c-5930-8d65-a5cac3ee62fb","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"326cb7b7-2096-5525-9e78-d31148c506aa","text":"M7.1 State what weightlifting training is supported to improve."},{"id":"56309ecd-b4b7-56f6-aedf-5d6c34b8c1a8","text":"M7.2 Weigh learning cost, equipment, mobility demand, opportunity cost, and coach competence."},{"id":"7b2b13c2-9a27-5a7a-8c31-f0c7c292f08c","text":"M7.3 Describe no-catch derivatives and their evidence status."},{"id":"b9c35517-f27d-5c82-bb27-ef63b031f110","text":"M7.4 State why catching is not established as necessary."},{"id":"59fc44d2-ede4-5fb8-b55f-b277df72d046","text":"M7.5 Select m'
    || 'edicine-ball tasks by movement, implement, and intended output."},{"id":"08a1ba02-3f67-52bb-a09f-77b8d3229698","text":"M7.6 Select upper-body power methods with their evidence limits stated."},{"id":"8b237614-d4d7-5274-b1f2-d7935d324c1d","text":"M7.7 Answer both the \"required\" and the \"too dangerous\" claims accurately."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"aef5eeb1-4e51-5b51-be88-ef1b391e8a1a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M6 · Sign-off: PS-2"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('751229ea-a670-5077-a5ca-bc3096c81fb9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'd8545f92-fb11-5c94-8d9d-284f1a4aadac', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"7e0e163a-bc45-562a-9df8-06fa32d85e09","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L7.1 What weightlifting delivers","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"c99eaf4b-078b-54e7-8e59-78feb969ff35","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Effective across strength, jump, sprint, and COD-related outcomes — and comparable to plyometric training in several of them. Useful, not necessary."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"6cb798be-3abf-5354-9d97-295b95bc2f4b","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L7.2 The tuition fee","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"d0551de0-1680-5779-8d02-80ef0995d60a","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Technical content, coaching competence, equipment, mobility and receiving demands, and the opportunity cost of the time spent learning. AS-105-03 in its most consequential application."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"619b25a6-8ff2-549a-8607-0e1f7a95e1ea","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L7.3 No-catch derivatives","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"4f135e79-9223-5679-b162-fd0993af9d3b","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Pulls, high pulls, jump shrugs — plausibly retaining force-velocity exposure w'
    || 'ith fewer constraints, on limited direct comparative evidence. Claims of identical adaptation stay conditional."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"a5391bbb-1c50-5f1c-91aa-f2e1f9315758","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L7.4 Medicine balls","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"58854b6f-a6fc-53ef-a435-53270ea2dd82","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"A genuine ballistic option for upper-body, rotational, and whole-body sequencing. Specify movement, implement, task, and intended output — not \"core power.\""},"position":"a7"},{"id":"f089c76f-ddb6-53f2-831e-c36a7d93b80f","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L7.5 Upper-body power","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"98a4a0a1-cd85-5edc-a67c-84db02a1e373","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Bench throws, ballistic push-ups, upper-body plyometrics; thinner and more exercise-specific evidence than lower-body jump research, stated as such."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"475c8e01-1145-5d4f-b757-f77e298ea995","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L7.6 Both absolutes, answered","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"fa77c02d-0103-5675-b114-c31074861114","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":'
    || '1,"data":{"text":"\"Required\" is Class F. \"Too dangerous\" sits on an F/B boundary where supervision, progression, and context decide. Coaches practise both answers."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"50cdf784-31e1-58ac-a2c9-22af0d9080ea","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L7.7 Choosing for this athlete","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"3d84559f-cc8a-569f-9dbc-1fd432b8e380","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"A worked benefit-to-cost comparison, which is Case 6."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('096a43ad-04e9-5839-b519-18cebf93f7dc', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '183f1504-9312-5198-8cde-f13d550840b3', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"ec0d66ad-6a6f-597b-9faf-383b96f47fe1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"a3adde2d-4ca8-5243-8583-84f26e60ace7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-07 — Transfer Must Be Demonstrated, Not Assumed","body":"Improvement in a jump, lift, throw, profile, or laboratory metric does not automatically establish sport transfer. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-7 and G3 104 D104-2. This is not a claim that transfer is rare — it is a requirement that the transfer pathway be stated and, where possible, observed. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a1"},{"id":'
    || '"717adcb0-f24a-546d-8638-36111c5c6361","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"f949112e-db1c-562a-a79d-e84086da88d2","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Weightlifting training is effective for strength, CMJ, squat jump, sprint, and COD-related outcomes — Basis: EP105-04; EP105-04"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"75f254cd-1dc9-5b1a-ae20-7dec2adc08d8","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — indicates utility, not unique necessity","body":"Weightlifting and plyometric training may yield similar improvements in speed, power, and strength outcomes — Basis: EP105-04; EP105-04"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"ee9ee67d-0fe7-5f2c-afea-0b393c07fefa","type":"callout","schemaVer'
    || 'sion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"No method family is established as universally superior for every athlete and power outcome — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-04, EP105-15; EP105-02; EP105-04; EP105-15"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"31ffad4f-f036-5adb-ae2e-45d31f735722","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D","body":"Weightlifting movements carry high technical content; value depends on benefit exceeding learning cost, equipment, mobility demands, opportunity cost, and coach competence — Basis: EP105-04; EP105-04"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"4ea319d0-e6a4-5978-ba2b-89301bb121e3","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C — direct comparative evidence limited","body":"No-catch derivatives may retain meaningful force-velocity exposure while reducing receiving-position constraints — Basis: Evidence Pack §13; supporting record not itemized (§20.3b)"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"9e50f8c5-0565-5a28-bb5e-ee976a8630af","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"Every athlete must clean or snatch to develop power\" — Basis: EP105-04; EP1'
    || '05-04"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"218861cb-fabc-5b25-99c9-b64085c08491","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","body":"\"Catching is necessary for power development\" — Basis: Evidence Pack §13"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"4b739040-c2dc-51bc-88eb-deb859918373","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/B boundary — risk depends on supervision, progression, and context","body":"\"Olympic lifts are inherently too dangerous for athletes\" — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"47d6824e-d001-55e5-84ee-82b73298247d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C","body":"Medicine-ball throws as a ballistic, projection-based option — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"8ba3beef-fc76-52df-839d-44019b5e95af","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — task-, sport-, and sex-specific","body":"A 12-week medicine-ball intervention in young female handball athletes produced greater throw-test gains than control — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"75d8b085-450a-5133-8640-79ee276f8'
    || 'bf1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C / limited","body":"Universal medicine-ball load prescriptions and broad sport-transfer claims — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"},"position":"b3"},{"id":"5d828fe8-7130-541c-a76d-a9a15e75ad93","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C — direct research more limited and exercise-specific","body":"Upper-body ballistic methods (bench throws, ballistic push-ups, upper-body plyometrics) — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-22; EP105-02; EP105-22"},"position":"b4"},{"id":"b677898c-3d22-59ef-a9a1-34d01bbe554c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calendar."},"position":"b5"},{"id":"8daab493-da50-5dfe-b889-3d297b3050e5","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-01 — Name the Output","body":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\" — Enforcement: every power_task o'
    || 'bject requires output_requirement and task (§21.4)."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"00cfcc32-3442-557a-b20e-a1baf52e57f0","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"f727fa5a-1794-50e2-b2a0-2ab7d83cf29b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-14 — Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure","body":"Supervise, progressively dose, technically coach, and scale to maturity and training age without unsupported prohibitions. — Enforcement: supervision ratio documented for every G3 youth power group; the safety claim is stated with its conditions."},"position":"b8"},{"id":"962d7877-b120-5f35-aa5f-abab36c56879","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether to teach weightlifting derivatives here (CJ-07); which variant; medicine-ball loading."},"position":"b9"},{"id":"44bc79b6-e949-50bc-8'
    || 'e9e-d747e99c627e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Olympic lifts declared required or inherently dangerous (CA-06) · catching declared necessary (CA-07) · medicine-ball work described as generic \"core power\" · upper-body evidence generalized beyond its exercise · a method chosen because the coach enjoys teaching it · learning cost ignored in a 30-athlete room."},"position":"c0"},{"id":"624c70a0-101f-5dbc-9680-193c4a867b29","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"bench-throw evidence is largely resistance-trained men; the medicine-ball intervention evidence is young female handball athletes and sport/test specific. Neither generalizes automatically."},"position":"c1"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('63aeec68-cbc2-5791-a481-79ce259c9dd0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b3a9fc52-e162-5c9e-9016-a1ff3af66ee7', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"5b6c509a-8ccc-5136-84a9-86225758e552","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"In a G3 scholastic environment with one coach and thirty athletes, the honest weightlifting answer is usually a derivative selected for coachability at that ratio — or a loaded-jump or medicine-ball alternative that reaches the intended stimulus without the tuition. Either is defensible; the reasoning is what is assessed."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"cd876925-b5cf-5bad-82b1-301c4f6037ba","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Olympic Lifts vs Loaded Jumps. A G3 scholastic program, one coach, 28 athletes, two 45-minute slots, limited platforms and bumper plates, mixed training ages from novice to third-year. The head coach — who is competent in weightlifting and enjoys teaching it — proposes teaching the power clean to the whole squad as the primary power method. A staff member argues Olympic lifts are too dangerous for high schoolers and should be replaced with loaded jumps.","steps":[{"id":"7ccb1d14-7cfc-5753-9f8b-1e61738e557b","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"ba13d516-1c4c-59fa-9805-0fc52a3e8d5c","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"6a69a5bb-bc8a-519f-a35b-b33fd1b1da4f","situati'
    || 'on":"Defense"},{"id":"65ba3ce1-385d-5738-912f-e494dba0a2f7","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: Which method, and how do you answer both arguments?\n\nFailure modes: Method chosen by coach preference or by fear · either absolute retained · learning cost ignored · no-catch equivalence overclaimed · the constraint of 28:1 in 45 minutes left out of the reasoning entirely."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"1f435804-16a0-5d02-8736-aa13af3217ae","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-2 — Power Exercise Coaching. See §14.3. PS-2 gates M8."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('bc99eecb-ce73-5c81-a2a8-912f2a265a9c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e11d14a2-aa69-5d52-8af0-d7ba9560be67', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"2e0a30aa-7462-506e-b17a-c85f22b4264f","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"08386b90-1581-5217-b12b-0f9daf65d54c","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"fb464687-9090-5050-8937-df6544929e58","title":"G3-105 · M7 Assessment — Weightlifting Derivatives, Medicine Balls & Upper-Body Power"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('b286a85b-f3c7-5623-b698-e838db71eb09', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b082c091-4245-5527-8496-97d588c55d2d', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'PS-2 · Power Exercise Coaching', true, null, (('[{"id":"a5985382-46d4-5e4b-94e5-6af247130fc1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"PS-2 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M7. Gates M8. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"1c9f048f-c316-56f9-a668-67370aac17c8","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3},"position":"a1"},{"id":"0cf45fb1-0a4c-5e46-a6f0-2f191e9b3c6f","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"873fdf92-bea7-5b45-b5bf-ee3aa00544c0","text":"Selects a task appropriate to the athlete, facility, and objective."},{"id":"4ab98b88-9172-5a2d-b525-4e2dc72974cc","text":"Coaches setup and intent precisely for the task''s demand."},{"id":"3e2c7985-a17f-56ca-bd28-0fc432e253aa","text":"Coaches landing or projection as a trained element, not an afterthought."},{"id":"3a3df8bf-e40c-5af8-b857-54a138b40788","text":"Preserves output quality across the exposure and acts when it degrades."},{"id":"856eb1f6-5647-5705-8432-c2ae77b60ed3","text":"Regresses or progresses appropriately, changing the right variable."},{"id":"8e153e4c-90d6-5481-b6b5-57352'
    || '35628fc","text":"States the stop and modify criteria before the set, and applies them."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"0d1f999b-e2fb-5d1a-8881-408948769cce","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3},"position":"a3"},{"id":"4f5201da-45b2-5d6c-8e61-1a47f155c041","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"f8b5cd1c-9e95-565d-b517-3713c768bbaf","text":"ballistic prescription (A5)"},{"id":"94199501-c116-5f6e-a398-213fe48fbd2c","text":"plyometric progression (A6)"},{"id":"5118c976-a345-59b4-a87f-a8e24a1d87e5","text":"method comparison (A7)."}]},"position":"a4"},{"id":"f5898338-be23-5cdd-a74a-001386a173dd","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Assessor questions","level":3},"position":"a5"},{"id":"5ea1ea27-8266-5df0-9c82-44623eb41e7b","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"dbe3d986-422e-5413-8c19-a136a905ffe0","text":"What is this exposure training, in what task?"},{"id":"a6dc17f2-bce4-5948-912e-f7440ff09312","text":"What are you watching for, and what ends it?"},{"id":"4437c003-265c-538a-a41a-1336291ac0b3","text":"Why this progression variable rather than another?"},{"id":"5939f1d8-65d8-5a04-90fd-6057'
    || 'bd703773","text":"What would you use if this equipment weren''t here?"}]},"position":"a6"},{"id":"ee9552a4-7c0b-5c4d-9e9c-f7ad41f30d97","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"33e8ced8-40c0-5fcb-a5e1-d4d27c354ebd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."},"position":"a8"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('04d03573-9d90-5430-976e-ce59176bc9f8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1a4fed1b-fd55-5c2d-928c-24e9197335b0', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"e8fd5d11-7367-57c9-a55e-422c8904e756","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Force-time data is the most seductive information in the field. It looks like an explanation and it is a description."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"d2b56738-673c-509c-a49e-21b1cc524e39","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To make coaches capable of interpreting force-time constructs accurately, and to establish the measurement-window and reliability limits that prevent RFD and derived metrics from becoming field diagnoses."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"bd268835-1965-54a8-9381-50aa8198c7ce","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"a27a0317-cca9-5608-92b0-6bc25d4609b8","text":"M8.1 Define RFD and state its measurement-window sensitivity."},{"id":"e14b829b-939d-58a2-a3fc-74e7e3718289","text":"M8.2 Distinguish early and late RFD determinants."},{"id":"424ed806-89dc-5e33-bd4f-3de31cc572bb","text":"M8.3 Distinguish impulse, peak force, and RFD, and state when each is task-relevant."},{"id":"fdafb5b7-a776-571a-87eb-15e3b94ed7ec","text":"M8.4 Explain why strength improvement and RFD change are not the same."},{"id":"c7fcc676-896e-5e4b-b1f6-6d0f3973cbb3","text":"M8.5 S'
    || 'tate the reliability limits of derived force-time metrics."},{"id":"8dc27534-cf51-585a-94d5-2d107e3a8b56","text":"M8.6 Explain why no single force-time variable is universally primary."},{"id":"b1501664-5036-588a-928c-5c3aeb38a576","text":"M8.7 State what field inference from force-time data can and cannot support."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"dc48e9c0-9901-55de-ae25-d969aaaee39e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires PS-2"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('c94c02c2-acef-56db-93b6-218856755573', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9864f8f6-6fa2-52a7-a3da-e4b8283eae7b', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"4ea0c7b6-8bcb-5453-94bd-b70fe2182f0a","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L8.1 RFD defined and bounded","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"14cb54e5-44a6-5f6b-b0ad-6870bef53e99","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The force-time slope, and the fact that the number depends heavily on the window and method chosen."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"6317bf24-3d76-5004-a0e2-d0060f06274d","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L8.2 Early and late","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"8dbaa73d-5b78-5290-bcf0-379b7fd74868","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Early windows are activation- and method-sensitive; later windows reflect maximal force capacity as time becomes available. Two different questions with one name."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"3abef07a-7086-5b70-8c7e-17056db722be","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L8.3 Impulse as the task question","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"7ccdd49f-473f-5daa-b6fc-1e279b070651","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"When momentum must change in limited time, impulse is usually the more task-informative construct — magnitude, direction, and available time toget'
    || 'her."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"d61e2b84-4a7b-560c-b9f8-caa3e819e7e3","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L8.4 Peak force, late","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"d8a07324-1562-53ea-93dc-f9655736099b","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Force achieved after the task is over is not useful force. This is why peak values alone mislead in time-constrained tasks."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"2c7a45e5-6f4b-5d49-bf1c-c58369e41079","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L8.5 Strength up, RFD flat","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"e4bdd534-1824-5b07-a35b-f0cb98fd6442","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"A documented outcome, and a permanent caution against assuming one adaptation implies the other."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"089bf0ee-ebb6-51de-94aa-20655a5dae65","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L8.6 Reliability of the derived","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"701ffad9-3da3-55f6-a210-a7747e3b0bc0","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Basic outcomes are generally more reliable than several derived eccentric, stiffness, and RFD variables — which is the mechanical basis for D105-11."},"position":"b1"}'
    || ',{"id":"cc8a4438-6af4-5d47-981d-b3c0c3316f5c","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L8.7 What a coach may infer","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"560b58e9-3db5-527e-bccb-285a9908a8c1","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Description, comparison against the athlete''s own standardized baseline, and hypothesis generation. Not cause."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('4e5cc1ba-a44d-5c96-90ee-84fed41a9db6', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b1ac85db-d5e5-583e-816a-64ebc2ddff91', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"30bdc4bb-6e54-5cc6-b372-753ac8eb1d66","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-02 — Related Qualities Are Not Interchangeable","body":"Strength, force, impulse, velocity, power, and RFD interact but are distinct. — Teaching boundary: Distinct does not mean unrelated — these qualities interact continuously in every athletic task. The doctrine forbids substitution, not integration. \"Explosiveness\" is Class F as a precise diagnostic term and is replaced by the actual quality and task. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"d24af595-9baa-5678-bda9-3ac8e6597f0d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-11 — Simple Reliable Metrics Outrank Metric Volume","body":"Prioritize standardized, reliable, decision-relevant measures over dashboard complexity. — Teaching boundary: This is not an argument against force plates. It is an argument for a small, pre-specified, reliable metric set chosen before the data is collected. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"5c0afc0f-86b9-53e7-9a23-d39fb550cd26","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Pow'
    || 'er Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"1dc4809f-5477-5cd9-98ef-4e4c51f2e21d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Early RFD is strongly influenced by rapid voluntary activation and motor-unit discharge rate — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"24b77300-e1f2-5a13-a7ed-681ff68e56b7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Both explosive and heavy-resistance training can improve RFD in various populations — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"589f156b-120e-5e27-bb12-37760382c972","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Valid and reliable RFD measurement is difficult — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"75acb62b-208c-5bfa-a566-5c86d395fd'
    || 'fd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Early and late RFD windows have different determinants; later windows are more influenced by maximal force capacity — Basis: EP105-03; EP105-03"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"ef92d1ae-17b7-5917-b2db-241e68aad1df","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — a specific intervention and sample","body":"Strength improvement does not necessarily produce RFD improvement — Basis: EP105-23; EP105-23"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"bb20f710-9879-5dfa-b1b6-9d8fd1f94500","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Impulse is the force-time integral and is directly relevant when momentum must change within a constrained time — Basis: EP105-01, EP105-02; EP105-01; EP105-02"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"90ed091a-860a-5072-aabe-fcdecbed28cf","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Peak force may be insufficient when achieved too late for the task — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"6a65ab6d-b5c7-5f3d-a161-65764e7d9e5f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2'
    || ',"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Any one force-time variable is universally primary — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-03; EP105-02; EP105-03"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"7b19eaad-3e44-523e-b51f-5dcc8c424647","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Several derived force-plate metrics show weaker or protocol-sensitive reliability than basic outcomes — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"23c43deb-f85e-5bec-be47-0393ef68538c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C","body":"Routine individual RFD diagnosis in field settings — Basis: EP105-03, EP105-09; EP105-03; EP105-09"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"341c8e1f-ed43-5e42-a7e6-085378ff0fa7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."},"position":"b3"},{"id":"563dfc91-37db-5384-b602-ea20c6fb606a","type":"callout","schemaV'
    || 'ersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."},"position":"b4"},{"id":"c6ccb0d3-76d7-5232-8b35-4e07403197c6","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."},"position":"b5"},{"id":"27c3d3bd-599b-55d6-8b67-ba14eec97d7d","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-12 — Interpret Change Against Error and Baseline","body":"Readiness and performance changes require repeated baselines, reliability and error context, and other athlete information."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"0fc9d038-2bcf-504e-b2ed-6acdb937d80c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether force-time measurement is worth running in a given environment (CJ-19)."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"1a0128ac-5918-5ab8-8c11-2a7b8ef17d8b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"'
    || 'Using RFD and power interchangeably · comparing RFD values across windows or methods · assuming a strength gain produced an RFD gain · reporting derived metrics with the same confidence as basic outcomes · force plates treated as explanations (CA-19) · building a program around a single force-time variable."},"position":"b8"},{"id":"e450f938-baa2-553c-8186-cda365d61004","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"RFD methodology evidence is laboratory-oriented and adult; field applicability is explicitly limited by the source itself."},"position":"b9"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('06eeb6ce-42ae-518e-9a2a-be7823d14f9a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'c4cb921c-9d41-557e-af01-d6eab6c09e18', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, ('[{"id":"631a60da-6ec8-51a8-a348-a587df71e76c","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"For G3 environments with force plates, this module produces the pre-specified metric set: a small number of reliable outcomes chosen before testing, with everything else left uncollected or explicitly exploratory."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"cbebb6ec-9bc6-5c54-9696-12497f84d33d","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Scenario M8-A — The dashboard. A force-plate report listing twenty-two metrics for one athlete. The learner selects the small set that could change a decision, states what each is for, and says what they would stop collecting.","steps":[{"id":"a3043958-6d51-520b-9f5f-8421723e0b5b","situation":"Work the case with your assessor: name the decision, the evidence class behind it, and what would change your answer."}]},"position":"a1"},{"id":"61e49ece-7898-50a4-b3a1-5de56441f095","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-3 — the force-time interpretation (A8) is required evidence at PS-3 — and carries into PS-4."},"position":"a2"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('18d485e6-cccc-5025-98d2-5eafc8e0bec4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '08aeded4-0f8e-5eb4-a80d-aea4d364c3ac', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"19b347b1-205d-5b11-a421-5790e1c9e4ba","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"7d67a367-095b-5196-bf4e-60f14f3544a3","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"04067deb-bf97-5a25-86d2-5fb28e960a0a","title":"G3-105 · M8 Assessment — RFD, Impulse & Force-Time Interpretation"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('ca088451-b913-5aa5-b70d-386d5da27f58', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9c2afa8c-79db-5500-9e63-bd3f485ef061', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"4071441d-13b3-54a0-bb82-22ae835b1cc0","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Four popular methods sit in this module. All four have real uses. None of them has the evidence its marketing implies."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"9c106882-12e3-5228-a610-c86038a0d26b","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To evaluate complex/contrast training, PAPE, cluster sets, and VBT accurately — separating acute from chronic evidence and measurement capability from programming superiority."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"2c1a8338-e966-56e3-9633-5340aa2cd14e","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"a54244bb-a4a9-562d-af9d-93275e9e09e7","text":"M9.1 Distinguish PAP from PAPE."},{"id":"1ed0cbc4-109c-5dcf-9cfa-005f750c9866","text":"M9.2 State what acute PAPE evidence supports and what it does not."},{"id":"46159c5b-835a-56e0-91f1-6c18161db999","text":"M9.3 State the chronic evidence status of complex and contrast training."},{"id":"8c93a4df-5bcc-5c79-aa30-6fcd4945d178","text":"M9.4 Describe cluster sets and their conditional use."},{"id":"2353745e-28af-5538-956d-999dd07c6a21","text":"M9.5 State what VBT provides and what it requires to be useful."},{"id":"6b5'
    || '6d7db-e765-56c7-9ca8-64fb9fe86129","text":"M9.6 Explain why no velocity-loss threshold is published."},{"id":"3465c63d-ad22-5508-b342-4e60321f0f99","text":"M9.7 Apply the acute–chronic rule to any method claim."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"2a444725-9ebb-5a8a-a233-c91ada1c68b7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M8"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('1ec683c7-746c-5261-aee7-b5845c25c4ac', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7cc723b6-9a9f-506d-b2db-5a7469064057', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"4cbe599c-2fb3-55e4-b666-bcab4bfc7e27","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L9.1 PAP and PAPE are different words","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"5b3d4dd2-cd7a-58f6-a1fe-6e2d813159c8","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Physiological potentiation versus a subsequent performance improvement reflecting potentiation-fatigue balance. Using them interchangeably is where the overclaim starts."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"227569c5-c340-5a96-9b53-0866ea35117a","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L9.2 What acute evidence shows","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"58f63136-3324-53ed-a620-b593208edc94","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Small, variable, context-dependent enhancement under selected protocols. Where the pack reports a response window from its analyzed studies, that window describes those studies — it is not a G3 prescription (CJ-05 reasoning)."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"efbbc689-a7cd-528a-b402-65949e28abe9","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L9.3 The chronic question","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"95af2939-2ab3-528e-aa98-2f83019fae97","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"C'
    || 'omplex and contrast methods can be useful; consistent superiority over simpler programming is not established and depends on status, pairing design, recovery, and total training quality."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"0fa369d8-032d-5cd9-b423-6edfc835b4fd","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L9.4 Cluster sets","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"79b55f10-c168-5a44-b278-7f12ec8fc7c5","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"A quality-preservation tool with conditional supporting evidence. Useful when preserving velocity or power is the operational goal; not inherently superior."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"f51e68bd-9b4e-5eee-8bfe-e24454c8c7fa","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L9.5 VBT, honestly","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"1a05b699-4ab3-537e-8854-c161832ff0ae","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Real feedback and real monitoring value, dependent on device, exercise, loading, familiarity, and whether it changes a decision. Not required, not superior, not dismissible."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"4d2dbb06-22b4-5036-80ee-d8c84d893d26","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L9.6 Why no threshold","level":3},"position":"'
    || 'b0"},{"id":"7bc6ce54-f5c9-50d2-b78a-dddffbd856ca","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"No universal velocity-loss cutoff is verified across exercises and athletes. A coach may set a local convention; it is documented as a convention with a review date."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"16dfcbc5-7bad-592c-879a-007647dd63ac","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L9.7 The rule, applied","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"cfceafe4-00a4-58f9-828a-15bfa1a593d6","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Coaches practise sorting method claims into acute and chronic bins, which is the reasoning T-01 probes."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('59945a5a-ad38-5ba2-817a-d873ffb02107', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '195d4649-6514-5584-91b5-2ed45685ba77', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"9981349d-5a39-51c2-aa43-347462cf6511","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-13 — Acute Potentiation Is Not Chronic Superiority","body":"PAPE may be useful conditionally; it does not establish long-term superiority of complex or contrast methods. — Teaching boundary: Where the Evidence Pack reports an acute response window from a specific analyzed study set, that figure describes those studies. It is not a G3 prescription and is never published as one (CJ-05). — Evidence class: B/C for acute conditional PAPE effects, which are small and variable and context-dependent; C/B for chronic complex and contrast training, where superiority over simpler programming is not consistently established; F for \"PAPE proves complex training is superior"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"ffd00945-61fa-596f-bdce-20d12669e00c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-10 — Technology Must Serve a Decision","body":"Force plates, VBT, jump apps, and profiling systems are useful only when sufficiently reliable, interpretable, and actionable. — Teaching boundary: Low-tech environments must remain capable of G3-quality coa'
    || 'ching. The course is written so that a coach with a tape measure and a stopwatch loses no conceptual ground. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"20821ee9-0b31-511e-92c8-67a1597cfe2a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"68173d40-eec6-5e73-bb53-b07be637e105","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"PAP and PAPE are distinct terms and should not be used interchangeably — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"ed101336-bf7d-5a3f-81e7-eb538a00de07","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — C'
    || 'lass B/C","body":"Acute PAPE effects occur in some contexts and are small and variable — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"7d35b2d0-295d-5b36-ad78-af85c0f4a205","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C — acute outcomes only; the window describes those studies","body":"A 2025 meta-analysis of VBT conditioning activities reported enhancement with a response window in the analyzed studies and substantial context dependence — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"3981b793-6176-513c-8be4-6b912b9649d7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/B — superiority over simpler programming not consistently established","body":"Complex and contrast training can be useful chronically — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-18; EP105-17; EP105-18"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"cf5da774-c43f-5742-ac30-3e1a5a3a9a0c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"PAPE proves complex training is superior long term\" — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-18; EP105-17; EP105-18"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"ccbdc047-0857-5cc5-988f-361c18bd7b6f","type":"callout","schemaVer'
    || 'sion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C/B — method- and population-dependent","body":"Cluster sets can preserve repetition quality; a 2024 complex-training study reported more favorable changes in certain outcomes with cluster incorporation — Basis: EP105-18; EP105-18"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"f79d1389-0957-5db8-85ce-63bbd8b9bd6e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F/C","body":"\"Cluster sets are inherently superior for power\" — Basis: EP105-18; EP105-18"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"b0b3004a-36ad-5ac4-af4f-86bd4de54127","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"VBT can provide feedback and monitor load-velocity, effort, and within-session velocity loss — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-24; EP105-17; EP105-24"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"f5878f8e-340c-5836-a024-0c1eb21af183","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"VBT usefulness depends on device validity, exercise specificity, loading, familiarity, and whether the measure changes a decision — Basis: EP105-17, EP105-24; EP105-17; EP105-24"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"c224491e-af56'
    || '-59e3-b8ab-dc8e800519e8","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"VBT is required for power development\" — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"345be439-6566-5eca-b92e-0a41e6dbed81","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not verified for all exercises and athletes","body":"A universal velocity-loss cutoff — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"b3"},{"id":"5ec6f669-e937-5f84-988a-41dfdd8b0ea4","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-07 — Separate Acute and Chronic Evidence","body":"Do not use PAPE, acute peak-power loads, or acute force-plate responses as proof of superior long-term programming. — Enforcement: acute and chronic claims are cited and labeled separately in every module evidence block."},"position":"b4"},{"id":"03003eff-08fa-5162-81c8-64b036112254","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."},"position":"b5"},{"id":"5a3d6a2a-da4f-5698-9e62-d3566dd07769","t'
    || 'ype":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"f69f183d-f6e9-57f1-afa0-b4ea90e6afaf","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"723c63c4-a968-588f-aa34-51b277c6ee66","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"whether to use any of these methods; any local velocity-loss convention (CJ-10); rest within contrast pairs (CJ-11)."},"position":"b8"},{"id":"8ffa3d36-dc5b-5f17-be34-76038135f059","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"PAPE cited as proof of chronic superiority (CA-23) · cluster sets declared inherently superior (CA-24) · VBT declared required (CA-18) · an imported velocity-loss threshold · a study''s response window quoted as a pr'
    || 'escription · dismissing all four methods because they are over-marketed."},"position":"b9"},{"id":"9c896b8f-bb60-5e04-866e-492241afa5be","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"PAPE, VBT, and cluster evidence is dominated by resistance-trained adults, frequently male, in laboratory or controlled settings."},"position":"c0"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('c9bbb370-0825-5834-a538-cacda9fb7696', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'b841a078-42eb-5ccb-a0bb-ca0c429b0efe', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"9b23042a-c4d1-56f2-aa55-cdf1708aaeaa","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The most common defensible G3 use is contrast pairing in a session where the objective is quality, with the coach able to say the pairing is an organizational choice with conditional support — not a potentiation system."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"27299b9f-06b5-5809-829e-55404c2bf388","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Complex Training and PAPE. A coach proposes rebuilding the squad''s power block around complex training, arguing that PAPE research demonstrates superiority over separate strength and power work. The proposal cites an acute meta-analysis reporting performance enhancement after conditioning activities, and specifies a fixed rest window taken from that analysis. The squad is scholastic, mixed training age, two slots weekly.","steps":[{"id":"318e125f-0e16-5f71-b821-ca84c371ab5b","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"c7b2128a-bb0b-5371-b6b0-3481af063d66","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"49b7190e-25d3-58a0-bf12-0609320d0677","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"047a3b4d-c371-51ff-af08-409079f87031","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What is defensible i'
    || 'n this proposal, and what is the rewrite?\n\nFailure modes: Acute evidence cited as chronic proof (CA-23) · an imported rest window presented as a prescription · population transfer ignored · cluster sets or contrast pairing declared inherently superior (CA-24) · abandoning the structure entirely in reaction to the overclaim.\n\nDefensible variation: Using complex pairing passes. Not using it passes. Retaining the superiority claim in any hedged form fails."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"79deeb2d-e970-5641-9b24-bf9bda9c1ed1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-3 — the method claim audit (A9) is required evidence at PS-3 — and carries into PS-4."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('e235d3da-cb95-526a-87dd-1a7ae3f95125', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '270c81c3-e403-507b-89ef-8325a1418939', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"d94a991c-c63e-53fd-abb3-57dfae0ef679","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"a1be79e0-9cd1-5e71-9579-12405a36f640","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"35c5426f-f5e0-5262-8361-0b58f04f6191","title":"G3-105 · M9 Assessment — Complex/Contrast, PAPE, Cluster Sets & VBT"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('44722617-c89e-5050-9b4c-18f405d044a2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '72af9cc7-20bd-53ff-83a6-17aed5e481c3', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"33b57c49-19d1-50a4-acd9-fb0a17371124","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The athlete''s jumps in practice count. Every honest power dose starts by subtracting."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"e805f723-e353-5728-9c26-6f6c4e52e3d7","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To teach dose, rest, and order as quality-preservation decisions, and to integrate power exposure with the total high-output load the athlete already carries."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"bcb153f9-ce2d-5ca3-a7e3-6e4a5bf380bb","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"dc96799f-2527-5b40-83dd-48678d4fa4d0","text":"M10.1 Set dose by preserved quality rather than by volume."},{"id":"499d9479-d7c0-5e64-bcb0-ba420143b394","text":"M10.2 Apply rest to preserve intended output."},{"id":"6f49553a-12bf-5495-ab44-f359bda9ab27","text":"M10.3 Sequence power work relative to other qualities."},{"id":"577ed825-5354-56d9-bbe7-1174f6433c6d","text":"M10.4 Explain the basis and limits of \"power first\" sequencing."},{"id":"6d04e369-6563-5c66-98b2-587f0206d3f4","text":"M10.5 Count existing sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure."},{"id":"9f313462-e149-52ca-9b9f-50ddd6d'
    || '3a696","text":"M10.6 State why no universal weekly frequency or microcycle model is published."},{"id":"8201c54e-1310-5e76-ab1f-3ad013162058","text":"M10.7 Define stop and modify criteria in advance."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"01af91a6-229b-511d-b6ce-65a185ed7f2f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M9"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('a5bfa934-b43a-5a9c-b1fc-91209b06f652', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '13214b9d-219e-5fe5-818c-3bc8cd432b8c', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"4e2e349f-073e-5026-ae3e-0033225f3c8c","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L10.1 Dose by quality","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"8c46b860-4015-536f-8060-229476e6e6b4","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The criterion is whether the athlete is still producing the intended output. Volume is an input, not a goal."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"33dbeca8-4a1e-5483-acb1-0f0a67e7bfbc","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L10.2 Rest for output","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"e8b99933-e43a-55d7-b897-9803faab72fb","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"More recovery than hypertrophy work when output is the priority; exact intervals contextual. No number published."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"0e65956e-d9d9-5f52-955d-88cb24080aa5","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L10.3 Order","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"e1c430b0-ec1c-5283-86c5-fd5844bb1266","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"\"Power first\" is supported principally as a practical way to protect speed, technique, and high-intensity output from preceding fatigue — a B/D position, not a universal law, and contrast work shows preceding st'
    || 'rength work is sometimes deliberate."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"959b0df8-1bbe-5266-a598-1151e41a3f69","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L10.4 Counting what exists","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"e5c9b35a-4721-5055-99e3-41e2da3a8652","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure — counted before anything is added (AS-105-15). In jump-heavy sports this usually reduces gym volume."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"9a3289ea-5827-59e7-a52c-608511c064d9","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L10.5 Weekly organization","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"f0ca9dd4-ada9-5f22-83b4-c7aeb3fb7379","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"No universal frequency, high/low model, or microcycle template. The interaction with practice, competition, training age, total workload, and recovery determines the practical dose."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"45779eb7-a73e-5b44-a15e-3804e941f970","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L10.6 Stop and modify criteria","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"c717a206-571b-5598-9a89-0fc4770361b1","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Writt'
    || 'en in advance: what output decline, technical change, or landing quality change ends the exposure."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"661ce268-caf7-5e24-86b5-531867a8c3c4","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L10.7 The subtraction habit","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"89484651-abbc-59fd-9e4b-1776238af091","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Building a week by removing redundant high-output exposure before adding anything."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('facb0bb7-a007-5db1-8c2f-a548a38f5bf9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '67b4e497-7f1f-5c23-a774-d31ca6779cba', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"777ac0db-b1b6-50f5-bf5f-1524112cc63a","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-06 — Output Quality Governs Power Training","body":"Preserve movement intent, technical execution, and output quality appropriate to the objective. Fatigue that materially changes the task changes the stimulus. — Teaching boundary: No rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11). \"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" is an F/B boundary claim: training under fatigue may develop fatigue tolerance, but it does not make maximal-power work more specific. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"b4abd9fc-e813-5aa7-8416-c011903fab55","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-15 — Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management","body":"Coordinate power exposure with sprinting, jumping, strength, practice, competition, and recovery. No universal contact count, frequency, microcycle, or maintenance dose governs all athletes. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-8 and G3 104 D104-12. The athlete''s jumps in practice count. No frequency, contact count, or main'
    || 'tenance dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-15). — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"184260ed-6bc6-5a6b-8d2b-ed41d6950907","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-03 — Power Development Is Method-Neutral","body":"Plyometrics, ballistic resistance exercise, sprinting, resistance training, medicine-ball work, and weightlifting derivatives can all contribute. No method is universally required. — Teaching boundary: Method-neutral is not method-indifferent. Methods differ in learning cost, equipment demand, fatigue cost, and coachability — which is exactly what AS-105-03 asks the coach to weigh. No G3 material may require any exercise or method. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"53852f9b-f748-5eb9-b5e5-f1dc939527d3","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B for quality-oriented dosing; D for exact field prescriptions","body":"No exact power-training dose is established across methods — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"a9ebf326-d6fc-5b94-a7cb-610166349d8b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Power work gen'
    || 'erally requires more recovery than hypertrophy-oriented training when preserving output is the priority — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"45a021ba-63ef-5ce6-a5bc-5f74218ab9bd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Exact rest requirements vary by task, load, athlete, and objective — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"5bd91fe0-9943-5874-a1fd-2b8f2746988c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Velocity or power drop-off can indicate changing session quality where valid measurement exists — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"e3398cc3-0b7b-5984-9a00-d4d3935997b3","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not verified","body":"A universal velocity-loss cutoff — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"db992249-f7b3-5eab-8613-f91822b0979c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D — specific sequencing remains contextual","body":"Prioritizing high-quality power and speed tasks before substantial fatigue when maximal output is the '
    || 'objective — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-17; EP105-02; EP105-17"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"c668a83a-f835-5f89-8d37-bf885f687550","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class D for exact templates; B for integrating with total high-intensity load","body":"No universal weekly frequency, high/low organization, or microcycle model is established — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-02; EP105-19; EP105-21"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"12f4a5b1-9c1c-53a9-a577-682be7867f11","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B — describes the intervention literature, not a prescription","body":"Plyometric interventions commonly used 1–3 sessions weekly over 4–16 weeks with no clear dose-response evidence for exact optimal variables — Basis: EP105-19; EP105-19"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"fa7bb789-4715-5ffc-a8fa-6f32ddd2f673","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"\"More contacts always produces more power\" — Basis: EP105-19, EP105-21; EP105-19; EP105-21"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"fcded6fe-062f-5636-a655-715d30a1c7e9","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info"'
    || ',"title":"Evidence — Class F/B boundary — may train fatigue tolerance, not maximal power","body":"\"Fatigue makes power work more sport-specific\" — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"97b146d2-f76c-52b9-8821-a5d4b089aa2b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-04 — Preserve Intended Quality","body":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation."},"position":"b3"},{"id":"168fe9f0-19be-5b0c-86ba-dc44184b8854","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-15 — Integrate the Weekly High-Output Load","body":"Account for total sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure."},"position":"b4"},{"id":"b5ad6443-e3e6-5143-951d-f0cf6d5a8d0b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-05 — Progress Plyometric Demand, Not Just Contacts","body":"Progress landing demand, velocity, amplitude, direction, unilateral demand, external load, complexity, and exposure."},"position":"b5"},{"id":"f01f2f91-fc0b-567a-beef-d324f2e51f24","type":"c'
    || 'allout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calendar."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"976012b4-2f8f-56ca-8e78-8fa7d29a98e3","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"dose, rest, frequency, order, and organization (CJ-08, CJ-10 through CJ-14)."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"3719eb05-bfe6-5dd5-88c8-6b741614157b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Adding power volume to a full week · counting contacts as the dose (CA-11) · cutting rest to increase difficulty · importing a velocity-loss threshold · treating \"power first\" as an inviolable rule · justifying fatigued power work as sport-specific (CA-26) · no stop criteria written in advance."},"position":"b8"},{"id":"0488c8ad-73e7-531d-838b-82b76d2f6395","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"dose literature is heterogeneous across '
    || 'ages, sports, and training status; the descriptive intervention ranges above are not prescriptions for any G3 athlete."},"position":"b9"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('fda051c6-bfbc-57ae-b47f-3516e79e8f1b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '46896eda-5d71-5b00-815a-3d5dd6e53818', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"228cf696-4274-592e-95b6-665e70dc60eb","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"For a G3 in-season team, this module usually produces fewer, better exposures placed where output can be expressed, with practice jump volume counted and gym contacts reduced accordingly — which is exactly Case 5."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"626cecc0-916e-5c12-a99b-03418cc66760","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Volleyball Athlete With High Jump Volume. A G3 Volleyball Development athlete, 16, mid-season. Five practices weekly with high jump volume, a Saturday tournament format with up to five matches, and two G3 slots. The current gym program includes a plyometric block written at the start of the season and unchanged since — three sessions weekly with a fixed contact count. The athlete reports persistent lower-leg soreness and her landing quality visibly degrades late in practice.","steps":[{"id":"88a6b992-b87d-595c-a3ce-f490ad37e04e","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"343acb27-a593-5c38-b810-4a290ff61c2d","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"615b71fb-1f72-5947-a6d5-ca39536a2b82","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"204a8619-c05b-58bc-96f5-1de33bd727da","situation":"Discussion"}],"'
    || 'debrief":"Decision required: What changes?\n\nFailure modes: Adding volume to a full week · retaining a fixed contact prescription · reading soreness as effectiveness · declaring plyometrics unsafe · ignoring the landing-quality observation · promising the change prevents injury.\n\nDefensible variation: Any reduction justified by the counted exposure passes, including removing gym plyometrics entirely for a period while retaining landing quality work. Keeping the fixed contact count fails."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"b7748170-b5a7-506f-bcfd-3f5f843b3777","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"Feeds PS-3 and PS-4."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('235f84d4-5cc2-58d0-b888-314e3d702969', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '97e5b2c9-9476-547e-8522-17064f3d152f', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"3a103626-c414-58e5-93d5-cc17647b01ea","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"e4c3362c-4660-5526-8cd4-f0b859858770","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"3b3e50c7-fb5d-506f-849e-25d376bfdaad","title":"G3-105 · M10 Assessment — Dosage, Rest, Exercise Order & Weekly Integration"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('f62be1c1-fb2d-5d7f-8d04-7fa71534d18b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'f5626564-f1af-58da-8dd5-532c0e509e73', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 11, (('[{"id":"ce02ee66-e524-5246-a9b7-4de78296b8fd","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Two questions this module refuses to answer with a number: how much power work maintains power in-season, and what a female athlete needs that a male athlete does not."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"3214cd13-5a20-55e2-8f15-747942bf2e0a","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To organize power development over time without adopting a validated-sounding model, and to establish the youth and female-athlete positions this course carries."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"8616e0d2-1d0c-5f4b-b80f-b2457e012032","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"f4164b84-5af2-5b1a-adb6-e23bcb50d28d","text":"M11.1 Describe concurrent and mixed-method routes to strength and power adaptation."},{"id":"3d79a282-e758-56ed-ab76-5d7d1ac2d6fb","text":"M11.2 Reject mandatory strength-then-power sequencing."},{"id":"bd9878bf-a645-50d2-9b1b-ad3f4239e167","text":"M11.3 Describe named periodization systems as influential frameworks."},{"id":"cf488e01-bb13-5a65-834e-183035acb29d","text":"M11.4 Prescribe in-season maintenance as a defended reduction with a verification plan."},{"id":"e4b5e37e-cd60-51'
    || '2b-a203-8b7b17623194","text":"M11.5 State the unresolved status of exact maintenance and minimum-effective doses."},{"id":"fd280d06-18ac-5c75-9ac9-f94e3a2a37f9","text":"M11.6 Apply youth progression by competence and exposure."},{"id":"98d2627f-0033-54ad-b776-cab2a719f5f3","text":"M11.7 State the female-athlete evidence position and what it does and does not license."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"acdf9e5f-6e3b-55a4-a984-9619263659cf","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires M10 · Sign-off: PS-3"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('a4a43ef3-e971-50a6-92bf-7048721591d8', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'e26f5489-72ef-5e39-bb23-450a47be7fa6', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 42, (('[{"id":"915807b6-9579-568c-80b7-e976469ab737","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L11.1 Multiple routes","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"8e81f5e1-acc7-5d95-a963-1511430fbf52","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Concurrent and mixed approaches are supported; no sequence is mandated. This closes the loop opened by D105-04."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"531c8eb5-53ce-52a6-b5e8-a4e36a0ee0d1","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L11.2 Systems as frameworks","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"01b46620-f43e-507e-a7ad-928dbbca8250","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Phase potentiation, blocks, French contrast, triphasic, and similar systems taught as Class E — influential, organizing, not validated templates (§17.4)."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"c8910130-cf7e-5d9d-8689-fc85094520b9","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L11.3 In-season maintenance","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"165d19c6-cac8-5fe2-8cfd-f34a20fa6324","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Retain quality exposure at reduced volume where recovery permits. The exact dose is Unknown, and G3 publishes none — the form is a defended reductio'
    || 'n with a verification plan, inherited from G3 104 M9."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"8f25f672-8f82-501c-b300-f69187b533cb","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L11.4 Youth: what is supported","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"1b871f44-36e8-51ae-905e-c5a6547950e1","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Supervised, progressed resistance and plyometric training improves outcomes; low injury rates in properly prescribed settings; no age gate; no strength-ratio gate."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"54320aa1-3336-57e3-92bf-9444bb8a367d","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L11.5 Youth: what is not licensed","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"3e60b768-1a80-5d05-9064-7344d091fd8c","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Adult loading, volume, and testing prescriptions do not transfer to middle-school athletes because a youth study showed a method is safe. Both halves are stated together."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"f8a40628-241e-53f5-bde8-ab97fb034abe","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L11.6 Female athletes","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"bd3f923f-b2bd-548e-af49-b365bfdd86ce","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text"'
    || ':"Same foundational principles; individual sport demands, maturation, history, and recovery matter; cycle evidence is not converted into deterministic prescriptions; underrepresentation is disclosed at the point of the claim (§18.2)."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"d7e832a8-b207-5571-99c5-64ae879970c8","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L11.7 Writing the block","level":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"84707d98-f0b0-5a5d-ab61-3ea82b3e279f","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Assembling M10''s integration and this module''s constraints into a real training period for a real athlete."},"position":"b3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('6989d5f2-2b41-59de-b0d4-d4cca25c8d11', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '6f5d4d9f-c19b-569d-8259-2142006a72de', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 26, (('[{"id":"3cbc908c-86d8-5085-ab5a-63ad5b0742fa","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-14 — Youth Can Train Power","body":"Properly supervised and progressively exposed youth can sprint, jump, land, throw, perform plyometrics, and use appropriate ballistic and resistance methods without arbitrary age or strength-ratio gates. — Teaching boundary: The claim is conditioned on supervision, technical coaching, and progression — stated every time (AS-105-14). And youth evidence does not validate adult loading, volume, or testing prescriptions for middle-school athletes (Condition 2). — Evidence class: A/B"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"ea2c8d17-2e3b-5d87-9bf8-de3df36076eb","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-15 — Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management","body":"Coordinate power exposure with sprinting, jumping, strength, practice, competition, and recovery. No universal contact count, frequency, microcycle, or maintenance dose governs all athletes. — Teaching boundary: Continuous with G3 103 D103-8 and G3 104 D104-12. The athlete''s jumps in practice count. No frequency, contact count, or maintenan'
    || 'ce dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-15). — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"5f0a4053-4092-5b62-ae84-89c1a6c20ca1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-04 — Strength Supports Power but Does Not Gate It","body":"Strength matters, especially in weaker and less-trained athletes, but G3 does not require a universal strength phase or squat ratio before appropriate power training. — Teaching boundary: Rejecting the gate is not rejecting strength. Competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demand still govern what an athlete does (AS-105-06) — those are coaching judgments, not a ratio. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"7f4933a9-ab86-50c1-b9da-28e9c3f54cf5","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Multiple routes to strength and power adaptation are supported, including concurrent and mixed-method approaches — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"97c10ab4-88f6-5ddd-a344-1e3adc139dc1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Mandatory strength-then-power sequencing — Bas'
    || 'is: EP105-02, EP105-11; EP105-02; EP105-11"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"8da7b447-dddd-531e-9797-9f02113a16e5","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class E — influential applied frameworks, not universally validated templates","body":"Phase potentiation, blocks, French contrast, triphasic and similar systems — Basis: EP105-02, EP105-H1 to EP105-H4; EP105-02; EP105-H1; EP105-H4"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"0a3f5900-6db3-5772-9e18-8b86b8e75f05","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D","body":"Maintaining important neuromuscular exposures in-season with reduced volume and retained intensity/quality — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"08a600c5-985d-5687-a625-c3195d82b51e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class U — Unknown","body":"Exact universal in-season maintenance and minimum-effective doses — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"3bc4462d-8de7-5353-92c2-7e414b7ab2fd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A/B","body":"Youth resistance and plyometric training can improve strength, jump'
    || ', sprint, and other fitness outcomes when supervised and progressed — Basis: EP105-10, EP105-11, EP105-12, EP105-13; EP105-10; EP105-11; EP105-12; EP105-13"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"66604883-f908-5d40-84f6-6fa08fa8e7d1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Age-based prohibitions and arbitrary strength thresholds as universal gates — Basis: EP105-10, EP105-11, EP105-13; EP105-10; EP105-11; EP105-13"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"a082ac0e-0fb4-5ec7-bef9-9c9fdb8a5eff","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Low injury rates in properly prescribed youth resistance training and weightlifting under supervision — Basis: EP105-10; EP105-10"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"2209bcbb-f098-5a52-9244-6433917f1f52","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/D","body":"Progression considering training age, coordination, maturity, exposure, and supervision — Basis: EP105-11, EP105-12; EP105-11; EP105-12"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"d7e19fbb-e665-5016-818b-3f2fa73ba1e3","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not establi'
    || 'shed — Condition 2","body":"Youth evidence validating adult loading, volume, or testing prescriptions for middle-school athletes — Basis: EP105-10, EP105-11; EP105-10; EP105-11"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"935dd619-00ae-5cee-983e-2b342d031394","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Female athletes improve strength, jump, sprint, and COD outcomes through resistance and plyometric training — Basis: EP105-20, EP105-22; EP105-20; EP105-22"},"position":"b3"},{"id":"e43e235b-4adf-5b18-bb36-5cc162105650","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class Not supported","body":"Fundamentally different power-training principles for female athletes — Basis: EP105-20, EP105-22; EP105-20; EP105-22"},"position":"b4"},{"id":"3a55d221-68fc-5af1-8267-a91072d587b1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class C / Unknown","body":"Broad cycle-based power prescriptions — Basis: Evidence Pack §35"},"position":"b5"},{"id":"cc05e289-3d1a-58fa-8d8f-8d2de3ffa108","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-14 — Youth Progress by Compe'
    || 'tence and Exposure","body":"Supervise, progressively dose, technically coach, and scale to maturity and training age without unsupported prohibitions. — Enforcement: supervision ratio documented for every G3 youth power group; the safety claim is stated with its conditions."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"28920c7d-e4d4-55fd-bae7-f5cc788dc9ff","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-15 — Integrate the Weekly High-Output Load","body":"Account for total sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"2e719516-7c12-5542-8296-18278e3d301b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-06 — No Universal Strength Gate","body":"Use competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demands instead of arbitrary bodyweight ratios."},"position":"b8"},{"id":"d605fc0a-990c-50f4-8a81-12773fd07b7f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-03 — Use the Simplest Effective Method","body":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility'
    || ', and calendar."},"position":"b9"},{"id":"432bc2dc-e58c-5665-9629-9153a9d4d59e","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"the organization (CJ-14); the in-season dose (CJ-15, CJ-16); individual modification for any athlete (CJ-21)."},"position":"c0"},{"id":"a1c24237-aa17-53e1-80cd-b700c039b40c","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"Strength-then-power sequencing (CA-16) · a named system presented as validated · publishing an in-season maintenance dose · age gates or strength gates for youth (CA-09, CA-02) · transferring adult prescriptions to middle-school athletes on the strength of a youth safety finding · building phase-based programming from cycle evidence (CJ-21)."},"position":"c1"},{"id":"98e06b56-2282-55fd-9c1a-92b1b7c54045","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"many power-method studies are male-dominant; medicine-ball evidence includes young female handball samples but is sport-specific; youth evidence supports supervised progressive training without establishing adu'
    || 'lt prescriptions."},"position":"c2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('c875fd31-2909-50c0-96d9-0dc411afbcfc', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '5898cfe3-9928-52b7-ac5b-417f0ff43b98', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 16, (('[{"id":"4a9a3ea3-db2e-593b-8bcf-d5d5f4480140","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"In G3 Youth S&C the module usually produces simpler exposures with real progression criteria and documented supervision; in-season it usually produces a smaller, protected, quality-first exposure with an explicit statement that maintenance — not development — is the objective."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"85bcc1d0-f0a7-575b-aec7-400fe9d6f1a7","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Middle-School Athlete New to Power Training. Ana, 12, joins a G3 Youth group of 18 with one coach, two 50-minute sessions weekly. No structured training history. Coordinated for her age, attentive, no injury history. She lands noisily and asymmetrically from a two-foot jump and has never been coached to land. Her father asks when she can start \"real explosive training,\" and mentions a coach who told him power work should wait until 16 and until she can squat 1.5× bodyweight.","steps":[{"id":"2d739dfc-f017-5eb1-8651-4835eb0574b5","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"e49ba7bb-81f6-5452-a761-babb81e2fadc","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"3020ca86-0b7b-527d-a599-3ee1a29fa7ed","situation":"Defense"},{"id":"'
    || 'e245a226-e703-5ffb-9c9f-17e94ef8708e","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What does Ana''s power exposure consist of, how does it progress, and what do you tell her father?\n\nFailure modes: Accepting either gate · substituting a different threshold · \"explosiveness\" language with a 12-year-old · adult contact counts or loading applied to youth · complexity beyond the supervision ratio · promising injury prevention · leaving landing uncoached because it looks like nothing.\n\nDefensible variation: A coach who introduces light external load once landing is competent passes with a stated criterion. A coach who stays unloaded for the block and says why also passes."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"393a14a7-9360-58a0-aa8e-f7273e3034f7","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-3 — Contextual Power Session Design. See §14.4. PS-3 gates M12."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('ed424e96-982c-5fe5-853f-000374337ed9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9a6ce921-5123-52ee-b97b-0106eb589384', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 10, ('[{"id":"d61236e4-cd9c-56ab-9301-facbfce5adef","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"8dbe482f-d032-53a1-b7f0-9781625df843","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"88b78a25-d114-5216-8791-eb91aa64e881","title":"G3-105 · M11 Assessment — Periodization, Maintenance, Youth & Female Athletes"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('e162c3e1-e290-5616-be1b-2e5846f8fc8e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8311200a-fd84-5a83-9b07-86b7799cb925', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'PS-3 · Contextual Power Session Design', true, null, (('[{"id":"e8aa2780-d777-50c5-b8f7-ef417292e212","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"PS-3 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M11. Gates M12. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"ed028621-43a1-55eb-a697-c01be4504da1","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3},"position":"a1"},{"id":"adbc51f3-aa13-5349-b00b-0523499eaa54","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"02a903a4-4ce4-57f8-82a7-184bef299cef","text":"Accounts for age, training age, maturation, and competence in what is set up and coached."},{"id":"5a19f931-077a-5c55-b449-6bd989164a18","text":"Integrates the athlete''s existing sport exposure and recovery into the session as delivered."},{"id":"a5a5885a-1ac4-512b-b035-fc0f867b2dab","text":"Chooses method, dose, and rest for the context rather than for the template."},{"id":"e0c66ab2-3070-5da7-a8dc-09afbb1e5529","text":"Manages fatigue visibly — adjusts when output or landing quality changes."},{"id":"0466fb66-88ea-5d42-a918-e3acb7812ba1","text":"Justifies the progression in terms an ath'
    || 'lete and a sport coach could follow."},{"id":"5bfcf3f4-4a19-5ea3-83ca-8c5ddd642bb7","text":"Identifies which elements of the session are Coach Judgment."},{"id":"f084afc3-d4c0-5e13-8caf-d07ce62b01a0","text":"Interprets their own force-time or method-claim evidence (A8, A9) inside its construct, protocol, and error — and states what it does not establish."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"3be3f595-36ce-5e05-9504-e25ef9d5d45a","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3},"position":"a3"},{"id":"fa5ecde9-1318-563f-a36c-f970e6aacf86","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"• force-time interpretation (A8)\n\n• method claim audit (A9)\n\n• weekly integration (A10)\n\n• contextual block (A11)\n\n• plyometric progression (A6). PS-3 covers M8–M11, per the course gating architecture (§6.3). A8 and A9 are the written evidence from M8 and M9; A6, A10, and A11 are the delivery evidence. All five are carried forward into PS-4, where they are read against the integrated plan rather than against a single session."},"position":"a4"},{"id":"d68c0818-0abc-5c15-9a5d-344544dae9ce","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Assessor questions","level"'
    || ':3},"position":"a5"},{"id":"eb96c49f-1821-5ad5-b230-5f303486eeb1","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"72907a42-79ef-5fbf-bc45-df058108cb2f","text":"What did this athlete already do this week?"},{"id":"8600d4cd-78e7-5d65-93c8-1cbb5cc93001","text":"What told you to change the dose?"},{"id":"309ec2eb-8b44-5e06-aede-43ad0fff2ed4","text":"Why that progression now?"},{"id":"c04d670f-6bd7-5e04-8609-4a6c928ca557","text":"What did your force-time or method evidence actually establish, and what did it not?"},{"id":"19a02b70-cbd6-51c2-a60f-0a8f7e7145d4","text":"Which of these choices would another qualified coach reasonably make differently?"}]},"position":"a6"},{"id":"d8404cf2-e3b5-50df-a66c-23bbc7bcc3d5","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"065aca52-cf0e-5baa-9c67-f8ed9348e186","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."},"position":"a8"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('8affed91-1248-5258-80f2-a391f67108af', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '8df19aac-e6e7-58c4-b236-44787b266fab', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Overview & objectives', true, 12, (('[{"id":"4a521eaf-8af2-5b72-9905-44be4ca3f48f","type":"quote","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The dashboard will offer you a diagnosis. The last thing this course does is teach you to decline it."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"1886b9a0-133a-5519-9161-f9e0fd1a6a51","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"To select and interpret power tests, govern technology use, refuse readiness and asymmetry thresholds, and run the full G3 decision model on a power case."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"1b97dfc1-e1a6-5486-87bb-188788ef9908","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"2cf7137f-ac45-552f-958f-786604bd5bbd","text":"M12.1 Select a test matching the construct being evaluated."},{"id":"8877a3fe-bb7f-5482-a6d3-549d20ebb7fe","text":"M12.2 State what CMJ, squat jump, drop jump/RSI, broad jump, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, and VBT velocity each measure."},{"id":"647f4666-ee03-55eb-bd79-111b4be0eb86","text":"M12.3 Apply standardization: protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation, baseline."},{"id":"a73a9042-d2e1-5678-bc86-d52934b9e343","text":"M12.4 State the reliability limits of derived force-plate metrics."},{"id":"11a38be1-a983-52e5-abc3-af70226647c4"'
    || ',"text":"M12.5 Treat power equations as estimates."},{"id":"dfd10232-c38a-59c1-85a1-809eed3d8e76","text":"M12.6 Interpret change against baseline, typical error, and context."},{"id":"7483aa9f-3a5d-5a3d-ac76-7600b65a3080","text":"M12.7 Refuse universal readiness and asymmetry thresholds without substituting new ones."},{"id":"6a0c5eeb-dd7a-5aa9-b759-0da17959cb82","text":"M12.8 Run the G3 decision model on a power case and defend it."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"86ff1b60-9ab8-5046-b5ba-deafd60db4b3","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Sequence & gating","body":"requires PS-3 · Sign-off: PS-4, then T-01"},"position":"a3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('3d12caea-fbcb-5c0e-851e-855ba8770cbf', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '1ad92cab-4a5c-50fd-bcc0-85bc5db41656', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Directed study', true, 48, (('[{"id":"e74ac40f-c1c6-5193-9c9f-d61ec231bad7","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L12.1 Construct first","level":3},"position":"a0"},{"id":"b831919b-c002-5552-a983-b50e96597698","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Decide the construct, then the test. Reversing that order is how a jump becomes a readiness score."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"57009996-51ef-5d8c-b060-fdfd242870d9","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L12.2 What each test measures","level":3},"position":"a2"},{"id":"34a31c56-bd44-529f-84bb-2c7b0fa36d18","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The seven-row table in §15.2, taught as the operating reference."},"position":"a3"},{"id":"fd38df8d-8dd9-598e-928a-67ffca163588","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L12.3 Standardization","level":3},"position":"a4"},{"id":"196d7989-148c-5b65-bd47-98161967c676","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline — recorded at time of testing (AS-105-09, inherited G3 102 AS-06)."},"position":"a5"},{"id":"57ff6887-caa4-5d92-a279-c630deac7f8c","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"te'
    || 'xt":"L12.4 Reliability of the derived","level":3},"position":"a6"},{"id":"31b46a70-1458-5f81-93ba-b17440d965d4","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Basic outcomes over dashboard breadth (D105-11), with the pre-specified metric set from M8."},"position":"a7"},{"id":"13967151-1fd2-55cb-b1e8-373d98aed3b1","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L12.5 Estimates are estimates","level":3},"position":"a8"},{"id":"15247da7-d2f8-51be-8e01-c8a7fd62b228","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Power equations, flight-time methods, contact mats, cameras, and plates each carry assumptions; cross-system comparison is not interpretation."},"position":"a9"},{"id":"d4e36593-5af5-5b47-8927-cf1078c85927","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L12.6 Interpreting change","level":3},"position":"b0"},{"id":"f41fa9d1-f1e0-5036-b67c-e3308d3af48e","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Baseline, typical error, context, and other athlete information — never one number (AS-105-12). This is Case 7."},"position":"b1"},{"id":"80a904c9-1b25-5726-baf8-cdf1e20bd3f8","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L12.7 Refusing the thresholds","l'
    || 'evel":3},"position":"b2"},{"id":"d2b21f9a-a0e7-50d3-91d5-578641fd81e3","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"No readiness threshold and no asymmetry cutoff, and no replacement number (AS-105-13). This is Case 9."},"position":"b3"},{"id":"4c0f95f6-f300-5047-b048-8c3b3bd09339","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"L12.8 The integrated case","level":3},"position":"b4"},{"id":"65918cd2-5de5-51c5-9762-86aea012f721","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The full G3 102 model run on a power decision, producing the one-page plan the coach defends."},"position":"b5"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('1a330977-d461-5711-bc11-7410993bd6f9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '927d8323-e632-5455-95d8-08a3bc012308', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Doctrine, evidence & standards', true, 30, (('[{"id":"dbb4a800-faf9-59c4-8508-ce85504e2377","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-11 — Simple Reliable Metrics Outrank Metric Volume","body":"Prioritize standardized, reliable, decision-relevant measures over dashboard complexity. — Teaching boundary: This is not an argument against force plates. It is an argument for a small, pre-specified, reliable metric set chosen before the data is collected. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a0"},{"id":"34daa1f9-f7ab-5aac-af89-df3496a33eb1","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-12 — Reactive Metrics Are Task Measures","body":"RSI, RSI-modified, jump height, contact time, and force-plate variables do not independently define readiness, stiffness, injury risk, or explosiveness. — Teaching boundary: These metrics are worth collecting when standardized and decision-relevant. They are not worth a label. — Evidence class: A that RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio; F for the claim that RSI alone completely measures reactive ability; F/C for the claim that low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit; B/C for RSI and RSI-modified contributing '
    || 'to a broader standardized monitoring battery"},"position":"a1"},{"id":"8a77adf7-0e5f-5f59-a0be-f6d987a7de70","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-01 — Power Is Task-Specific","body":"Interpret power within the movement, load, body mass, force-time constraints, and measurement method used to express it. — Teaching boundary: This does not make power measurement useless. It makes an unlabeled power number meaningless. Every power value in G3 carries its task, load, and calculation method. — Evidence class: A"},"position":"a2"},{"id":"84d758ea-6e5f-5703-b71a-fcf698234e58","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"note","title":"G3 Doctrine D105-10 — Technology Must Serve a Decision","body":"Force plates, VBT, jump apps, and profiling systems are useful only when sufficiently reliable, interpretable, and actionable. — Teaching boundary: Low-tech environments must remain capable of G3-quality coaching. The course is written so that a coach with a tape measure and a stopwatch loses no conceptual ground. — Evidence class: B"},"position":"a3"},{"id":"9eb3475b-47f8-5186-a504-a031437f52cc","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone"'
    || ':"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"CMJ height is a standardized vertical-jump outcome, not a complete power, RFD, or readiness diagnosis — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"},"position":"a4"},{"id":"7a61bfa7-c92b-5f05-a795-bd5c3ac88e5b","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Drop jump / RSI is a task-specific flight-to-contact ratio, not complete reactive ability, stiffness, or injury risk — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"c998af65-8480-554a-aaf1-70b989d43adb","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Broad jump is a horizontal jump outcome, not a sprint-acceleration mechanism diagnosis — Basis: EP105-02; EP105-02"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"2029e6bd-dbb4-5270-898f-11bd91640a32","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A","body":"Loaded-jump results do not create universal F-V diagnoses — Basis: EP105-05, EP105-07; EP105-05; EP105-07"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"1c78a7ca-a40d-5819-9217-28485e8dc094","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Medicine-ball throws are ta'
    || 'sk-specific projection outcomes — Basis: EP105-22; EP105-22"},"position":"a8"},{"id":"9d4aed41-1eea-557b-9999-6b803a490e38","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"VBT velocity is exercise-, load-, and device-specific — Basis: EP105-17; EP105-17"},"position":"a9"},{"id":"cc2b4a18-5402-5e34-8a72-fc074f12cbec","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Force plates can measure force-time data and derive outcomes; reliability is metric-specific, with basic outcomes generally more reliable than several derived variables — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"},"position":"b0"},{"id":"85d86dd5-d87d-51d4-a573-e43bb1cba1b9","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class A/B for standardized jump height as an outcome; C/F for unqualified estimated-power claims","body":"Peak-power equations are estimates that may not match device-derived values or retain validity across body sizes, protocols, and populations — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-24; EP105-08; EP105-24"},"position":"b1"},{"id":"397331b1-1600-5ffe-a55d-0fa2ff72b38b","type":"callout","schemaV'
    || 'ersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B","body":"Smartphone-based CMJ tools can show acceptable validity for selected metrics in specific implementations; validation is not automatically transferable — Basis: EP105-24; EP105-24"},"position":"b2"},{"id":"69926cb3-8be1-5626-a514-1f8c14a0a750","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class B/C","body":"CMJ and related measures are useful within-person monitoring tools with standardized protocol, baseline, typical error, and decision context — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"},"position":"b3"},{"id":"2deea91d-5e01-59c2-a159-624af0b85dd4","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F","body":"Automated readiness determination from CMJ height, RSI-modified, or any single force-plate metric — Basis: EP105-08, EP105-09; EP105-08; EP105-09"},"position":"b4"},{"id":"b39f4b91-fb52-5714-bdbb-c31b40617bf2","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Evidence — Class F for universal cutoff interpretation; B/C for monitoring repeated task-specific asymmetry when rel…","body":"No universal 10% or 15% asymmetry cutoff i'
    || 's established as automatically performance-limiting, injury-predictive, or corrective — Basis: EP105-08; EP105-08"},"position":"b5"},{"id":"f8668cd7-4b7f-5ca8-a007-79dbc9156ccd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-09 — Standardize Testing","body":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline."},"position":"b6"},{"id":"5ef8ca39-d55b-54ae-be58-285b46e93a44","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-10 — Measure the Construct Actually Tested","body":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed. — Enforcement: test_record requires construct_measured."},"position":"b7"},{"id":"d2bcdc6d-87c5-5c3c-a979-75bd1ce72d15","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-11 — Use Technology Only When Actionable","body":"Technology must answer a defined question."},"position":"b8"},{"id":"9c154313-2aff-5236-ad6b-2343ada7e306","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-12 — In'
    || 'terpret Change Against Error and Baseline","body":"Readiness and performance changes require repeated baselines, reliability and error context, and other athlete information."},"position":"b9"},{"id":"30ddfdf6-6afa-5c09-b26d-03872c9c1624","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"G3 Applied Standard AS-105-13 — No Universal Asymmetry Threshold","body":"A fixed 10% or 15% value does not automatically trigger intervention."},"position":"c0"},{"id":"120789ef-046e-5eca-9fb5-e5b5dbdffed4","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Coach Judgment","body":"which tests are feasible and decision-relevant in a given facility (CJ-19); how to interpret a measured asymmetry (CJ-20) or a readiness signal (CJ-18)."},"position":"c1"},{"id":"8aa5e27a-a757-5650-a052-7683b46b6f65","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Common coaching errors — claim audit","body":"CMJ height read as readiness (CA-22) · RSI read as reactive ability (CA-20) · force plates read as explanations (CA-19) · asymmetry read as a problem (CA-21) · estimated power compared across systems · a metric set chosen after the data arrived · running the d'
    || 'ecision model without naming the output requirement."},"position":"c2"},{"id":"5e13bd74-b783-573d-a5f8-1a12bbfc41cd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Population-transfer disclosure (AS-101-16)","body":"CMJ metric and reliability work is device- and protocol-dependent, largely adult; app validation is implementation-specific."},"position":"c3"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('d5267c0d-cb08-5196-bd97-30aec8ae91d3', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '2b46333b-aa0f-5d2d-a0aa-61e00085d519', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Case & application', true, 18, (('[{"id":"ab89c097-451a-55e8-be6c-50f846179499","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"The artifact is a G3 power testing report another coach can read: what was measured, in what task, under what protocol, against what baseline, what it does and does not establish, and what decision it changed."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"0aba6c09-f69f-55e5-b3ab-9773caaffe80","type":"scenario","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"intro":"Athlete Labeled \"Velocity Deficient\" From One F-V Profile. Sofia, 19, collegiate athlete. A single vertical-jump force-velocity profile, collected once on a portable system by a visiting practitioner using a method the G3 staff cannot fully reconstruct, returns a label: \"velocity deficient, prioritize velocity-oriented training.\" Her sprint and jump performance are unremarkable but not poor. No sprint testing, no technical observation, and no training history were included. The sport coach has seen the label and is asking about a velocity block.","steps":[{"id":"84f8f7ad-da0f-5e59-b918-a2c8b72e2efe","situation":"Situation"},{"id":"ecc33876-5157-5bd1-a235-b6bc85c1715c","situation":"Decision"},{"id":"58a854fc-64a8-5728-958b-a5c1fd8116df","situation":"Defense"},{'
    || '"id":"f529d3ea-7284-5fd7-999d-18fdda868912","situation":"Discussion"}],"debrief":"Decision required: What does this profile license?\n\nFailure modes: Accepting the label · rejecting profiling entirely in reaction · comparing this profile to one collected by another method · recording \"velocity deficient\" as an athlete attribute · using \"optimal profile\" as the target (CJ-03) · letting the sport coach''s expectation set the program.\n\nDefensible variation: Discarding the profile and evaluating from scratch passes. Retaining it as one input, corroborated, passes. Building the velocity block fails."},"position":"a1"},{"id":"ce14be95-f00a-512f-854a-2ad398819c41","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Practical sign-off linkage","body":"PS-4 — Integrated Power Microcycle (§14.5), then T-01 — Power Development Programming Defense (§14.6)."},"position":"a2"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('1280a720-3849-586e-a92c-5ae4c182d20b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '31e64387-f51d-5eac-a2f8-3f23562bc340', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'Module assessment', true, 12, ('[{"id":"30bae400-9b95-5f08-bb13-78efa82da1fa","type":"rich_text","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"This module closes with its assessment: the knowledge component (80% standard) and the applied competency component (at standard or better, one revision permitted). Written items are graded by a G3 assessor against the recorded keys and standards — applied coaching competence is never certified by written work alone."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"0d982d38-59cb-5247-b576-52237c0a4a16","type":"assessment_reference","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"assessmentId":"18adb854-ba77-5255-969b-14c2f3405bf7","title":"G3-105 · M12 Assessment — Testing, Force Plates, Readiness & Integrated G3 Decision-Making"},"position":"a1"}]')::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('35e06ba2-0bed-5e29-a549-9a520174d7af', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '7f05dab6-0130-5d58-a2ff-9e3eedfa9cf2', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'PS-4 · Integrated Power Microcycle', true, null, (('[{"id":"38149255-6cbc-50b0-a7e5-fa7efea6d4ca","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"PS-4 — Observed practical sign-off","body":"Timing: Completed at M12. Requires the four-week delivery window complete. — This sign-off is observed and recorded by an approved G3 assessor. It cannot be self-recorded."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"a606f392-7197-50b0-ab60-8990c8cc420a","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Observed behaviors","level":3},"position":"a1"},{"id":"b724a1d5-95c2-5889-a987-85a079bd151c","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"9ae1b204-2f07-5702-b74d-773c32cb1abf","text":"Analyzes sport demands and states what the week already contains."},{"id":"e4179cea-336a-53c1-a2ef-ade8496b6b43","text":"Integrates strength, sprint/COD, power, and recovery rather than stacking them."},{"id":"702a36ab-9d19-5f26-9cba-c746f3fe9c72","text":"Avoids redundant high-intensity exposure, and can point to what was removed."},{"id":"563573ed-9a3a-5c83-8ef3-9145ae02e663","text":"Defines the monitoring — what will be measured, under what protocol, against what baseline."},{"id":"c67f3905-f71d-5fb5-be7c-1524e44d2c5c","text":"Defines the adjustment'
    || ' logic in advance."},{"id":"c922950a-cc5b-5adb-809b-d599e5cfa8ad","text":"Identifies which elements are Coach Judgment."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"a98e861d-00d8-55ef-87e2-d34e5cb4780e","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Required artifacts","level":3},"position":"a3"},{"id":"4219c3a2-37bf-5905-a3f8-48c4414c4b5c","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"15478d05-1b8e-5573-99ed-0e8b508509ff","text":"force-time interpretation (A8)"},{"id":"c0fc9907-3c79-58f0-93df-c2ce5cd54aff","text":"method claim audit (A9)"},{"id":"fa7d85c2-604b-5069-89fc-acc2154c42bc","text":"weekly integration (A10)"},{"id":"bf3bec02-ed6c-52ac-a038-5920bc88d41f","text":"integrated power plan (A12)"},{"id":"201c5bf5-4870-5b37-a98d-d490fafc7b17","text":"the plan as actually delivered over the window."}]},"position":"a4"},{"id":"ea2dbf43-4a9d-5971-8e09-c94ec69f6bf0","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Assessor questions","level":3},"position":"a5"},{"id":"dfd6f454-19a1-5634-93c8-53bbdd1e2779","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"fe74a2d8-0a78-5da8-8e16-1cadd02ef49a","text":"What is the high-output load this week, counting everything?"},{"id":"'
    || 'ce4b804b-66ec-5947-b8a2-dc7915ead7b2","text":"What did you take out?"},{"id":"aea60184-62ed-5765-a488-111d6055f482","text":"What will you measure, and what decision would it change?"},{"id":"9eae8545-a8a3-5c4f-bf90-ad00faab64ab","text":"What is your adjustment trigger?"}]},"position":"a6"},{"id":"11da5227-3319-5cd7-8848-26d66c227b4f","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"success","title":"Recorded outcomes","body":"recorded · re-observation required"},"position":"a7"},{"id":"e92de5a0-23fd-546b-8b9a-4df0dbccfa83","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window","body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window. PS-4 cannot be scheduled until the delivery window is complete. Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."},"position":"a8"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031'),
  ('6e360a56-8397-54b8-a26b-001908ec7570', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', 'fc9ed188-04fb-5d2a-a50f-ac4577327c77', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 1, 'T-01 · Power Development Programming Defense', true, null, (('[{"id":"3d074f05-5b75-5517-a012-012084ec4a46","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"T-01 — Terminal defense","body":"20–30 minutes with an approved assessor, using the coach''s own athlete or group and their own artifacts. The assessor works the ten obligations (§1.4) in order and probes at least four. — Required for: course competency completion — The defense is evaluated live by an approved G3 evaluator. Failure on T-01 cannot be offset by module performance."},"position":"a0"},{"id":"7c4cb882-79dd-5048-8342-869e318c04c6","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Must defend","level":3},"position":"a1"},{"id":"a949c2c2-c5e8-5cea-b170-1966d7549aa2","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"4706e2ea-60f7-5e17-9ffb-3f230be25e57","text":"terminology"},{"id":"0a8bf85a-465c-5c98-913a-0805eb437693","text":"demand"},{"id":"1b39090e-f9b4-55b6-9b28-c3f4d8252916","text":"hypothesis"},{"id":"cc4e159e-aba0-5343-ba36-d0f9d302ad49","text":"method"},{"id":"39757025-bcb3-531c-a1f0-6578fd685c86","text":"loading, dose, and rest"},{"id":"6f277d7c-e56e-5cd9-9771-7020dd99c104","text":"quality criteria"},{"id":"953d0ab7-15e7-5a06-a511-549ce60769e6"'
    || ',"text":"progression"},{"id":"8b89dc53-e704-5a2d-9f9e-bd2743f29738","text":"weekly integration"},{"id":"c41e0de3-5912-5126-b8c9-4e73a33abd73","text":"testing"},{"id":"b5515a72-0a7f-5492-a164-f747cf049af5","text":"technology"},{"id":"f5e79e9b-c212-506a-ab1a-482e46bac3ba","text":"evidence classification"},{"id":"0e8451ec-5a5f-5e3a-9c49-0055eed057ce","text":"transfer assumptions"},{"id":"95b3504a-542a-5b95-a060-0ff4bf818dd7","text":"and the uncertainty that remains."}]},"position":"a2"},{"id":"90eaf2f2-c45b-5a4b-b34b-9376eb702328","type":"heading","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"text":"Mandatory probes","level":3},"position":"a3"},{"id":"75334d70-8be1-5926-9558-2e1e7d11f32f","type":"checklist","schemaVersion":1,"data":{"items":[{"id":"98cf1416-bcea-57b7-b808-0c4c019d0780","text":"What output requirement does this plan address, in what task, and how will you know it changed?"},{"id":"da14d191-50af-5382-80fd-a767bb17a86c","text":"State your limitation hypothesis, your confidence in it, and what would refute it."},{"id":"7455903d-ce4f-5f1b-b00f-ff1a7971d961","text":"Why this method rather than the alternatives — and what would you do without this equipment?"},{"id":"a659754c-ba2b-5150-ba37-e5'
    || 'd037902df9","text":"Which part of what you just told me is evidence, which is G3 standard, and which is your judgment?"},{"id":"4365e4aa-41bc-5882-b26d-97f4f185bcda","text":"Which of your evidence is acute and which is chronic?"},{"id":"8a363197-b4b5-52ff-853e-f8b75087ccc3","text":"What would make you change this plan? Be specific."},{"id":"829bd1d2-0805-5a6d-8f4b-143dc3d3b5c7","text":"Tell me what you would say to this athlete''s parent about injury risk."}]},"position":"a4"},{"id":"626d9576-ab62-582a-bcd7-d2197b653420","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"danger","title":"Non-compensable failures","body":"cannot answer probe 4 (evidence vs G3 standard vs judgment) · cannot answer probe 5 (acute vs chronic evidence) · makes an injury-prevention guarantee on probe 7 · cites a threshold, percentage, contact count, or required method as justification"},"position":"a5"},{"id":"f0319e23-fea1-580f-b391-256212dec478","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"info","title":"Defense rubric","body":"§12.3 defense rubric"},"position":"a6"},{"id":"36bd23ba-0a0a-5e07-a013-ec61c3f1a3dd","type":"callout","schemaVersion":2,"data":{"tone":"warning","title":"Practical window",'
    || '"body":"This course carries a minimum four-week practical window.  Practical observation time is additional to directed study and is collected on the floor."},"position":"a7"}]'))::jsonb, '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031')
