    || 'y":"The model–measurement rule (§2.4) governs this doctrine. A profile is a description produced by assumptions. No F-V threshold, deficit classification, or optimal-profile target is published (CJ-03, CJ-04).","sources":["EP105-05","EP105-06","EP105-07","EP105-16","EP105-25"]}'))::jsonb, 'a8'),
  ('7eeed2fd-2980-5ef0-8aa7-6b6cf25ea45f', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'doctrine', 'D105-10', 'Technology Must Serve a Decision', ('{"id":"D105-10","title":"Technology Must Serve a Decision","statement":"Force plates, VBT, jump apps, and profiling systems are useful only when sufficiently reliable, interpretable, and actionable.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Supported Practice. VBT usefulness depends on device validity, exercise specificity, loading, familiarity, and whether the measure changes a coaching decision; force plates improve data availability, not necessarily decision quality; app and device validation is not automatically transferable across software, protocols, or users. \"VBT is required\" is F. [EP105-08, EP105-17, EP105-24]","teaching_boundary":"Low-tech environments must remain capable of G3-quality coaching. The course is written so that a coach with a tape measure and a stopwatch loses no conceptual ground.","sources":["EP105-08","EP105-17","EP105-24"]}')::jsonb, 'a9'),
  ('6ef01ec9-a7eb-5732-b0a2-517b5e43102c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'doctrine', 'D105-11', 'Simple Reliable Metrics Outrank Metric Volume', ('{"id":"D105-11","title":"Simple Reliable Metrics Outrank Metric Volume","statement":"Prioritize standardized, reliable, decision-relevant measures over dashboard complexity.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Supported Practice. CMJ metric reviews report stronger reliability for several basic outcome metrics and less consistent reliability for selected RFD, eccentric, stiffness, and derived variables. More metrics can create more noise. [EP105-08, EP105-09]","teaching_boundary":"This is not an argument against force plates. It is an argument for a small, pre-specified, reliable metric set chosen before the data is collected.","sources":["EP105-08","EP105-09"]}')::jsonb, 'b0'),
  ('e46a77ee-7a56-5a3a-9e90-026cf2781687', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'doctrine', 'D105-12', 'Reactive Metrics Are Task Measures', ('{"id":"D105-12","title":"Reactive Metrics Are Task Measures","statement":"RSI, RSI-modified, jump height, contact time, and force-plate variables do not independently define readiness, stiffness, injury risk, or explosiveness.","evidence_class":"A that RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio; F for the claim that RSI alone completely measures reactive ability; F/C for the claim that low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit; B/C for RSI and RSI-modified contributing to a broader standardized monitoring battery","evidence_note":"A that RSI is a standardized task-specific ratio; F for the claim that RSI alone completely measures reactive ability; F/C for the claim that low RSI directly identifies a tendon-stiffness deficit; B/C for RSI and RSI-modified contributing to a broader standardized monitoring battery. [EP105-08, EP105-24]","teaching_boundary":"These metrics are worth collecting when standardized and decision-relevant. They are not worth a label.","sources":["EP105-08","EP105-24"]}')::jsonb, 'b1'),
  ('faa48ad7-232c-59ab-9903-7f64f1933ee7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'doctrine', 'D105-13', 'Acute Potentiation Is Not Chronic Superiority', ('{"id":"D105-13","title":"Acute Potentiation Is Not Chronic Superiority","statement":"PAPE may be useful conditionally; it does not establish long-term superiority of complex or contrast methods.","evidence_class":"B/C for acute conditional PAPE effects, which are small and variable and context-dependent; C/B for chronic complex and contrast training, where superiority over simpler programming is not consistently established; F for \"PAPE proves complex training is superior","evidence_note":"B/C for acute conditional PAPE effects, which are small and variable and context-dependent; C/B for chronic complex and contrast training, where superiority over simpler programming is not consistently established; F for \"PAPE proves complex training is superior.\" PAP and PAPE are not interchangeable terms. [EP105-17, EP105-18]","teaching_boundary":"Where the Evidence Pack reports an acute response window from a specific analyzed study set, that figure describes those studies. It is not a G3 prescription and is never published as one (CJ-05).","sources":["EP105-17","EP105-18"]}')::jsonb, 'b2'),
  ('7d89f944-8ae7-5a74-9ff1-d8572a5c12c7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'doctrine', 'D105-14', 'Youth Can Train Power', ('{"id":"D105-14","title":"Youth Can Train Power","statement":"Properly supervised and progressively exposed youth can sprint, jump, land, throw, perform plyometrics, and use appropriate ballistic and resistance methods without arbitrary age or strength-ratio gates.","evidence_class":"A/B","evidence_note":"A/B — Established/Supported. Youth resistance and plyometric training can improve strength, jump, sprint, and other fitness outcomes when appropriately supervised and progressed; evidence does not support age-based prohibitions or arbitrary strength thresholds as universal gates. \"Youth must wait until age 16,\" \"youth need a universal strength threshold before jumping,\" and \"youth should not do plyometrics\" are all F. [EP105-10, EP105-11, EP105-12, EP105-13]","teaching_boundary":"The claim is conditioned on supervision, technical coaching, and progression — stated every time (AS-105-14). And youth evidence does not validate adult loading, volume, or testing prescriptions for middle-school athletes (Condition 2).","sources":["EP105-10","EP105-11","EP105-12","EP105-13"]}')::jsonb, 'b3'),
  ('3584f05d-e348-5c15-b0d7-0610661d554e', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'doctrine', 'D105-15', 'Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management', ('{"id":"D105-15","title":"Power Programming Is Integrated Load Management","statement":"Coordinate power exposure with sprinting, jumping, strength, practice, competition, and recovery. No universal contact count, frequency, microcycle, or maintenance dose governs all athletes.","evidence_class":"B","evidence_note":"B — Supported Practice for integrating power exposure with total high-intensity load; D for exact microcycle templates; U — Unknown for exact in-season maintenance and minimum-effective doses; F for universal contact classifications. [EP105-02, EP105-19, EP105-21]","teaching_boundary":"Continuous with G3 103 D103-8 and G3 104 D104-12. The athlete''s jumps in practice count. No frequency, contact count, or maintenance dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-15).","sources":["EP105-02","EP105-19","EP105-21"]}')::jsonb, 'b4'),
  ('8210df9a-6199-5d28-aacb-c8f1816d64b9', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-01', 'Name the Output', ('{"id":"AS-105-01","title":"Name the Output","statement":"Identify the actual task and output requirement before prescribing \"power.\"","evidence_basis":"A (D105-01).","enforcement":"every power_task object requires output_requirement and task (§21.4).","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'b5'),
  ('ea58531c-1a59-5f06-95d5-925b66c0d083', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-02', 'Build Limitation Hypotheses From Multiple Sources', ('{"id":"AS-105-02","title":"Build Limitation Hypotheses From Multiple Sources","statement":"Use task performance, strength/force capacity, technical observation, training history, and context before labeling an athlete.","evidence_basis":"B — the Evidence Pack''s own supported-practice position.","enforcement":null,"boundary":"the output is a provisional hypothesis, not an identity (D105-09, CJ-04).","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'b6'),
  ('c4c4ae29-632c-5cbf-9c54-2051f59bdd78', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-03', 'Use the Simplest Effective Method', ('{"id":"AS-105-03","title":"Use the Simplest Effective Method","statement":"Where multiple methods can meet the objective, prefer the best benefit-to-cost option for athlete, coach, facility, and calendar.","evidence_basis":"D — applied inference from method-neutrality (D105-03).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"\"simplest\" means lowest total cost to deliver well, not lowest effort.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'b7'),
  ('64496560-a6a3-581f-a86b-a964c9b219d4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-04', 'Preserve Intended Quality', ('{"id":"AS-105-04","title":"Preserve Intended Quality","statement":"Manage reps, sets, rest, and exercise selection so output and technique remain consistent with the intended adaptation.","evidence_basis":"B (D105-06).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"no rest interval, repetition count, or velocity-loss cutoff is published (CJ-10, CJ-11).","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'b8'),
  ('c42d3c91-cd60-59e8-9041-9c2a6f81067c', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-05', 'Progress Plyometric Demand, Not Just Contacts', ('{"id":"AS-105-05","title":"Progress Plyometric Demand, Not Just Contacts","statement":"Progress landing demand, velocity, amplitude, direction, unilateral demand, external load, complexity, and exposure.","evidence_basis":"B for progressive individualized dosage; F for universal contact classifications as evidence-based prescriptions.","enforcement":null,"boundary":"CJ-08.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'b9'),
  ('92bdb910-c59c-5609-ae59-4bae9b302d17', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-06', 'No Universal Strength Gate', ('{"id":"AS-105-06","title":"No Universal Strength Gate","statement":"Use competence, exposure, readiness, supervision, and task demands instead of arbitrary bodyweight ratios.","evidence_basis":"F for the universal ratio (D105-04).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"CJ-05, CJ-06.","standing":"binding prohibition.","label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c0'),
  ('716431f4-7ce6-5496-b595-aec0c05151a2', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-07', 'Separate Acute and Chronic Evidence', ('{"id":"AS-105-07","title":"Separate Acute and Chronic Evidence","statement":"Do not use PAPE, acute peak-power loads, or acute force-plate responses as proof of superior long-term programming.","evidence_basis":"A as a methodological rule (D105-05, D105-13).","enforcement":"acute and chronic claims are cited and labeled separately in every module evidence block.","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c1'),
  ('ac3ed70c-cbeb-5792-a2d0-3113c1c7ec48', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-08', 'Treat F-V Labels as Provisional', ('{"id":"AS-105-08","title":"Treat F-V Labels as Provisional","statement":"Force- and velocity-deficit labels require corroboration.","evidence_basis":"C/F (D105-09).","enforcement":"NovaKore stores a limitation hypothesis with confidence and review point; no label may be stored as a fixed athlete attribute.","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c2'),
  ('5f58464f-1819-5ffc-a31b-24f33824d3e4', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-09', 'Standardize Testing', ('{"id":"AS-105-09","title":"Standardize Testing","statement":"Define protocol, equipment, warm-up, instructions, calculation method, and baseline.","evidence_basis":"A/B — reliability depends on task standardization, device, familiarization, and calculation. Inherited: G3 102 AS-06 (conditions recorded at time of testing).","enforcement":null,"boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c3'),
  ('9c488e20-1784-5662-9342-0a346258acf7', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-10', 'Measure the Construct Actually Tested', ('{"id":"AS-105-10","title":"Measure the Construct Actually Tested","statement":"Label CMJ, broad jump, RSI, loaded jump, medicine-ball throw, or bar-velocity results according to the task performed.","evidence_basis":"A (D105-01, D105-12).","enforcement":"test_record requires construct_measured.","boundary":null,"standing":"binding language rule.","label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c4'),
  ('98055ef2-7fe5-5b16-a5c1-1285c47584f0', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-11', 'Use Technology Only When Actionable', ('{"id":"AS-105-11","title":"Use Technology Only When Actionable","statement":"Technology must answer a defined question.","evidence_basis":"B (D105-10, D105-11).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"the question is defined before the data is collected; a metric that changes no decision is not collected.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c5'),
  ('7a26fdf9-6129-5c6a-90ec-a81ab7a1ca5b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-12', 'Interpret Change Against Error and Baseline', ('{"id":"AS-105-12","title":"Interpret Change Against Error and Baseline","statement":"Readiness and performance changes require repeated baselines, reliability and error context, and other athlete information.","evidence_basis":"B/C for multi-source monitoring; F for single-metric automated readiness decisions.","enforcement":null,"boundary":"CJ-18, CJ-19.","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c6'),
  ('71d57980-4ec8-5486-8429-3d63f743548a', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-13', 'No Universal Asymmetry Threshold', ('{"id":"AS-105-13","title":"No Universal Asymmetry Threshold","statement":"A fixed 10% or 15% value does not automatically trigger intervention.","evidence_basis":"F for universal cutoff interpretation (D105-12).","enforcement":null,"boundary":null,"standing":"binding prohibition. G3 publishes no threshold and substitutes none (CJ-20).","label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c7'),
  ('51a8a95e-9b57-5d7e-b826-f4c470b9f49b', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-14', 'Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure', ('{"id":"AS-105-14","title":"Youth Progress by Competence and Exposure","statement":"Supervise, progressively dose, technically coach, and scale to maturity and training age without unsupported prohibitions.","evidence_basis":"A/B (D105-14).","enforcement":"supervision ratio documented for every G3 youth power group; the safety claim is stated with its conditions.","boundary":null,"standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c8'),
  ('615654c2-60f8-5c92-9288-e261145f1ddc', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'applied_standard', 'AS-105-15', 'Integrate the Weekly High-Output Load', ('{"id":"AS-105-15","title":"Integrate the Weekly High-Output Load","statement":"Account for total sprint, jump, COD, strength, practice, and competition exposure.","evidence_basis":"B (D105-15).","enforcement":null,"boundary":"no frequency, contact count, microcycle model, or maintenance dose is published (CJ-08, CJ-13, CJ-14, CJ-15, CJ-16).","standing":null,"label":"applied_standard"}')::jsonb, 'c9'),
  ('1afd7da2-8829-5498-8c3a-8e9822ac00bf', '26f6aa4a-4ade-4bb0-842f-12ca2e5bc115', '9104a610-53ae-5fc5-b565-23db55089bb0', 'coach_judgment', 'CJ-01', 'Exact optimal power load', ('{"id":"CJ-01","area":"Exact optimal power load","evidence_position":"Intermediate loads often maximize acute power; exact load varies by exercise, athlete, metric, protocol","g3_supplies":"AS-105-01, D105-05","coach_decides":"The load for this athlete and exercise"}')::jsonb, 'd0'),
